</ul><p>Also mention: energy conservation derivation: m áº = F = âˆ’dU/dx; multiply by áº‹: m áº‹ áº + (dU/dx)áº‹ = 0 â†’ d/dt(Â½máº‹Â² + U) = 0.</p><p>And time period integral: t âˆ’ t0 = âˆ« dx/âˆš(2(Eâˆ’U)/m).</p><p>Also non-conservative examples: friction W = âˆ’Î¼mgd depends on path length; air drag; also time-dependent force F(t) is not conservative.</p><p>Common confusions: "conservative force doesn't mean energy conserved" â€” no, conservative means work path-independent &amp; mechanical energy conserved for isolated system. Also "potential energy is defined at a point" â€” only differences matter; reference point arbitrary. Also "F = âˆ’dU/dx means force is negative of slope" (students get sign wrong).</p><p>Also "which forces can have potential energy": only conservative.</p><p>Let me write part04 now with the two SVG figures and a table.</p></div></div></div></div></div><div><div class="body-sm flex items-center gap-1.5 cursor-pointer" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Expand"><span class="flex items-center gap-1.5 truncate"><span class="text-text-secondary">Write</span><span class="bg-surface-tertiary text-text-tertiary truncate rounded-sm px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[10px] leading-tight">build/part04.html</span><span class="text-text-tertiary text-[10px]">268 lines</span></span><button type="button" class="text-text-tertiary hover:text-text-secondary shrink-0 p-0.5 text-[10px]" aria-label="Open file">open</button><svg width="1.5em" height="1.5em" stroke-width="1.5" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" color="currentColor" class="text-text-tertiary ms-auto size-3 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200"><path d="M9 6L15 12L9 18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path></svg></div></div><div role="button" tabindex="0" aria-pressed="false" class="group/artifact relative block w-full overflow-hidden rounded-lg border text-start transition duration-150 bg-surface