anslate="no" class="relative pe-2 ps-6 pt-4" style="will-change: transform;"><div class="bg-sidebar-border absolute bottom-0 start-[6px] top-4 w-0.5 rounded-full"></div><div class="space-y-4 whitespace-normal size-full [&amp;&gt;*:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed"><p>Wait, the numerical problem at the end: U(x) = Â½kxÂ² âˆ’ (1/3)bxÂ³ with k=12 J/mÂ², b=4 J/mÂ³: dU/dx = kx âˆ’ bxÂ² = x(k âˆ’ bx) = 0 â†’ x = 0 or x = k/b = 3 m. Stable: dÂ²U/dxÂ² = k âˆ’ 2bx; at x=0: 12 &gt; 0 â†’ stable; at x=3: 12 âˆ’ 24 = âˆ’12 &lt; 0 â†’ unstable. Good â€” nice problem (and it's not asking for a solution, just practice; fine).</p><p>Hmm, "approximate turning points" for E=6 J â€” U(x) = 6xÂ² âˆ’ (4/3)xÂ³. Set = 6: 6xÂ² âˆ’ 1.333xÂ³ = 6. At x = 1: 6 âˆ’ 1.333 = 4.667. At x=1.2: 8.64 âˆ’ 2.304 = 6.336 &gt; 6. So turning point near x â‰ˆ 1.15: 6(1.3225) âˆ’ 1.333(1.5209) = 7.935 âˆ’ 2.028 = 5.907. So â‰ˆ1.17: 6(1.3689)=8.213; 1.333(1.6016)=2.135; = 6.078. So x â‰ˆ 1.16. And max of U: at x=3, U = 6(9) âˆ’ 1.333(27) = 54 âˆ’ 36 = 18 J. So E=6 J well below barrier â†’ also turning point on the other side: for x negative, U = 6xÂ² âˆ’ 1.333xÂ³ which is positive and grows for x&lt;0 â†’ turning point at about x â‰ˆ âˆ’1.0: 6 + 1.333 = 7.3; x = âˆ’0.93: 5.19 + 1.07 = 6.26; x = âˆ’0.9: 4.86 + 0.972 = 5.83. So â‰ˆ âˆ’0.92. Good, well-defined. Fine â€” problem is solvable.</p><p>Now Chapter 4 (Unit II part 1). This is big:
