</ul><p>That's a lot of SVGs; I'll do maybe 4-5 good ones (CM of system, elastic collision CM frame, rocket, Rutherford geometry, neutron collision).</p><p>Let me get the formulas right:</p><p>CM: R = (Î£ m_i r_i)/(Î£ m_i) = (1/M)âˆ« r dm.</p><p>Two-body: R = (m1 r1 + m2 r2)/(m1+m2). Relative coordinate r = r1 âˆ’ r2. Then r1 = R + (m2/M) r, r2 = R âˆ’ (m1/M) r.</p><p>CM frame: Î£ p_i = 0. Reduced mass Î¼ = m1m2/(m1+m2).</p><p>Total KE: K = Â½MVÂ² + Â½Î¼vÂ² where V = velocity of CM and v = relative velocity. Wait careful: the second term is Â½Î¼ vÂ² with v = relative velocity âœ“ (standard result). Let me verify with m1 = m2 = m, R with V: Â½(2m)VÂ² = mVÂ². Relative: Î¼ = m/2, v = v1 âˆ’ v2. For equal masses with v2 = 0: v1 relative = v1. Then K = mVÂ² + Â½(m/2)v1Â² where V = v1/2 â†’ mV1Â²/4 + m v1Â²/4 = Â½ m v1Â² âœ“ correct.</p><p>1-D elastic collision general:
v1' = ((m1âˆ’m2)/(m1+m2))v1 + (2m2/(m1+m2))v2
v2' = ((m2âˆ’m1)/(m1+m2))v2 + (2m1/(m1+m2))v1
For v2 = 0: v1' = (m1âˆ’m2)/(m1+m2) v1; v2' = 2m1/(m1+m2) v1.
Special cases: m1 = m2 â†’ v1'=0, v2'=v1 (exchange); m1 &gt;&gt; m2 â†’ v1' â‰ˆ v1, v2' â‰ˆ 2v1; m1 &lt;&lt; m2 â†’ v1' â‰ˆ âˆ’v1 (bounce back), v2' â‰ˆ 0.</p><p>In CM frame: each particle's speed unchanged in magnitude, just direction reversed (for elastic): u1' = âˆ’u1. In CM frame, v1_CM = m2 v_rel/(m1+m2) etc.</p><p>Energy transferred to target (m2 initially at rest): Î”E/E = 4m1m2/(m1+m2)Â² . âœ“ (derived from 2D formula with cosÎ¸*=... maximum at head-on and equal masses = 1.)*</p><p>2-D collision: in CM frame: E1' = (m1Â² + m2Â² + 2m1m2cosÎ¸*)/(m1+m2)Â² Ã— E0, and lab angle tanÎ¸1 = (m2 sinÎ¸*)/(m1 + m2 cosÎ¸*)? Let me derive: In lab frame, v1_lab_after = V_cm + u1' where u1' is the CM-frame velocity. Let's set m2 initially at rest. V_cm = m1 v0/(m1+m2) along x. u1 = v0 âˆ’ V_cm = m2 v0/(m1+m2) forward; u2 = âˆ’V_cm.*</p><p>After (elastic): |u1'| = |u1| = m2v0/M, direction at angle Î¸* from x-axis.
So lab velocity of particle 1: components: x: m2v0/M Â· cosÎ¸* + m1v0/M; y: m2v0/M sinÎ¸*.
tan Î¸_lab = (m2 sinÎ¸*)/(m1 + m2 cosÎ¸*) âœ“ matches standard.
Also v1_labÂ² = (v0Â²/MÂ²)(m1Â² + m2Â² + 2m1m2cosÎ¸*) âœ“ so E1' = E0(m1Â²+m2Â²+2m1m2cosÎ¸*)/MÂ² âœ“.
For equal masses: tanÎ¸1 = sinÎ¸*/(1 + cosÎ¸*) = tan(Î¸*/2) â†’ Î¸1 = Î¸*/2 âœ“. And Î¸1 + Î¸2 = 90Â° for equal masses âœ“.
Max scattering angle for m1 &gt; m2: sin Î¸_max = m2/m1 âœ“ (from geometry: the lab speed vector lies on a circle of radius u1 about the tip of V).*</p><p>Cross-section: for a given b â†’ Î¸; number scattered into dÎ© related to b db; dÏƒ/dÎ©. Mention that for scattering by a nucleus (Coulomb) we get Rutherford; for hard sphere it's isotropic in CM.</p><p>Also "charge particle scattering by a nucleus" â€” mention conservation of energy &amp; momentum used, the experiment (Geigerâ€“Marsden, Î±-particles on gold foil), and the two regimes: nuclear (strong-force) scattering at high energy vs Coulomb. Include derivation outline of b vs Î¸: b = (Z1Z2eÂ²/(4Ï€Îµâ‚€ Â· 2E))cot(Î¸/2)? Let's verify with standard: for Coulomb repulsion, b = (kq1q2/(m v0Â²)) cot(Î¸/2) = (kZ1Z2eÂ²/(2E)) cot(Î¸/2) since E = Â½mvÂ². Check: mvÂ² = 2E âœ“. So b = kZ1Z2eÂ²/(2E) cot(Î¸/2)? From b = kq1q2/(mvÂ²) cot(Î¸/2) = kq1q2/(2E) cot(Î¸/2) âœ“ yes.</p><p>And dÏƒ/dÎ© = (kZ1Z2eÂ²/(4E))Â² cscâ´(Î¸/2) âœ“ (derived earlier).</p><p>Numeric example: 5 MeV Î± on gold: computed earlier b(90Â°) = 2.28Ã—10â»Â¹â´ m. Let me recheck with the formula: kZ1Z2eÂ² = 8.99e9 Ã— 2 Ã— 79 Ã— (1.6e-19)Â² = 8.99e9 Ã— 158 Ã— 2.56e-38 = 8.99e9 Ã— 4.0448e-36 = 3.636e-26. 2E = 2 Ã— 5 Ã— 1.602e-13 = 1.602e-12. b = 3.636e-26/1.602e-12 = 2.27e-14 m âœ“ â‰ˆ 23 fm. Good. Note: textbooks often quote "for 5 MeV Î± on gold, b â‰ˆ 2.3Ã—10â»Â¹â´ m for 90Â° scattering" âœ“.</p><p>Also mention distance of closest approach for head-on: r_min = kZ1Z2eÂ²/E = 2b(90Â°)? For head-on (b=0), r_min = kZ1Z2eÂ²/E = 3.636e-26/8.01e-13 = 4.54e-14 m â‰ˆ 45 fm âœ“ = 2 Ã— 22.7 fm âœ“ consistent (r_min = 2b at 90Â°). Good.</p><p>Neutron moderation: derived formula E'/E0 = (m1Â² + m2Â² + 2m1m2 cosÎ¸*)/MÂ² where Î¸* CM scattering angle, m1 = neutron, m2 = nucleus. Maximum fractional loss when cosÎ¸* = âˆ’1 (head-on): E'min/E0 = ((m1âˆ’m2)/(m1+m2))Â² = Î± âœ“ matches definition Î±. Average: for isotropic scattering in CM, average cosÎ¸* = 0 â†’ average E'/E0 = (m1Â²+m2Â²)/MÂ², and the average log decrement Î¾ = 1 + Î± ln Î±/(1âˆ’Î±)... let me double check the standard formula: Î¾ = 1 + (Î± ln Î±)/(1 âˆ’ Î±) âœ“ where Î± = ((Aâˆ’1)/(A+1))Â². Yes standard (e.g., Lamarsh). âœ“ I computed C: 0.158, Pb: 0.0095 âœ“, H: Î±=0 â†’ Î¾ = 1 âœ“, D: A=2 â†’ Î± = 1/9 = 0.1111; Î¾ = 1 + 0.1111Ã—ln(0.1111)/(1âˆ’0.1111) = 1 + 0.1111Ã—(âˆ’2.1972)/0.8889 = 1 âˆ’ 0.24413/0.8889 = 1 âˆ’ 0.27465 = 0.7254. Hmm textbooks give Î¾_D2O â‰ˆ 0.509 with A=2 for deuterium... Let me check: for deuterium A=2, Î± = ((2âˆ’1)/(2+1))Â² = 1/9 = 0.1111 â†’ Î¾ = 1 + (0.1111)(ln 0.1111)/(0.8889) = 1 + (0.1111)(âˆ’2.1972)/0.8889 = 1 âˆ’ 0.2746 = 0.725. Some sources give Î¾ for deuterium â‰ˆ 0.725 âœ“ yes I think 0.725 is right (I earlier said 0.51 for D2O â€” the moderator D2O has Î¾ â‰ˆ 0.51 which accounts for oxygen too; the pure deuterium value is 0.725). Let me be careful and quote: hydrogen 1.0, deuterium 0.725, carbon 0.158, lead 0.0095 (commonly tabulated values: H 1.000, D 0.725, He 0.425, Be 0.209, C 0.158, O 0.120, U 0.00838, Pb ~0.0095). âœ“ Great, I'll use the table with collisions needed: n = ln(E0/Eth)/Î¾: H: 18.2, D: 25, C: 115, Pb: 1900. Wait 18.2/0.725 = 25.1 âœ“. Good, and this matches the well-known statement "about 25 collisions for heavy water/ deuterium".</p><p>I'll present n = ln(E0/E1)/Î¾, with E0 = 2 MeV, E1 = 0.025 eV â†’ ln(8Ã—10â·) = 18.2.</p><p>Let me double check ln(8e7): ln(8) = 2.0794, ln(10â·) = 16.118, total 18.197 âœ“.</p><p>Rocket equation derivation: variable mass system. Consider rocket of mass m at time t, velocity v; it ejects mass dm' = âˆ’dm with exhaust velocity u relative to rocket. Momentum conservation in free space (no external force):
Before: p = m v. After: (m + dm)(v + dv) + (âˆ’dm)(v âˆ’ u) [where dm &lt; 0]. Actually let's write standard: at t: m, v. At t+dt: rocket (m + dm), velocity v + dv; ejected mass (âˆ’dm), velocity v âˆ’ u.
