xt-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">details[open]</code>... Actually browsers: closed </p><details> hides children except summary. Setting <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">.dbody{display:block}</code> doesn't override the closed state in Chrome/Firefox because the content is hidden via the internal slot (the UA hides non-summary children of closed details using <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">content-visibility</code>/display none on the slot). The reliable CSS trick is <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">details &gt; summary ~ * {display: block}</code> doesn't work either. Modern approach: <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">details::details-content</code> or <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">details { display: block } details[open] ...</code> â€” hmm. The robust approach: in print, use JS to open all (my print button does that), and also add <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">@media print { details { open: ... } }</code> which isn't possible in CSS.*<p></p></details><p>Better: add CSS <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">@media print{ details:not([open]) &gt; *:not(summary){display:block} }</code> â€” this generally doesn't work in Chrome because closed details hides content via the UA shadow slot... Actually Chrome does respect <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">display:none</code> from UA on the slot: In Chrome, <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">details &gt; *:not(summary)</code> in a closed details has UA style <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">display: none</code> on the slot which can't be overridden. However, there's a newer CSS <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">::details-content</code> pseudo-element. Safer approach: keep the JS print button (opens all details before printing) and also add a <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">window.matchMedia('print')</code> listener + <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">onbeforeprint</code> handler to open all details automatically when the user does Ctrl+P. Let me add <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">window.addEventListener('beforeprint', ()=&gt;setAll(true))</code> â€” that handles Ctrl+P robustly.</p><p>Let me add that to the script in part09.</p><p>Also add <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm" data-streamdown="inline-code">@media print { details{ break-inside: avoid } }</code> maybe. Fine.</p><p>Let me implement the beforeprint handler.</p></div></div></div></div></div><div><button type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Expand" class="flex w-full select-none items-center gap-1.5 cursor-pointer"><svg width="1.5em" height="1.5em" stroke-width="1.5" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" color="currentColor" class="text-text-tertiary size-4 shrink-0"><path d="M13 16H18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path><path d="M6 8L10 12L6 16" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path><path d="M2 18V6C2 4.89543 2.89543 4 4 4H20C21.1046 4 22 4.89543 22 6V18C22 19.1046 21.1046 20 20 20H4C2.89543 20 2 19.1046 2 18Z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path></svg><span class="flex min-w-0 items-center gap-1"><span class="text-