<html><head><meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><div class="unithdr" id="unit1">
  <span class="marker core">Unit I Â· 15 Lectures</span>
  <h2>Physical Law and Frame of Reference</h2>
  <p>(a) Inertial &amp; non-inertial frames + transformation between translating frames + Galilean transformation &amp; invariance of Newton's laws Â· (b) Rotating frames, pseudo forces, Coriolis force, motion relative to Earth, Foucault's pendulum Â· (c) Conservative forces and potential energy curves.</p>
</div>

<section id="ch1">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 1 Â· Unit I (a)</span>Frames of Reference &amp; Galilean Transformation</h2>

<h3 id="s1-1">1.1 Sabse pehle: "frame of reference" kya hota hai?</h3>

<p>Ek simple sawal se shuru karte hain. Aap bus mein baithhe ho aur aapke haath mein ek pen hai. Pen <em>chal raha hai ya nahi</em>?</p>
<p>Jawab depend karta hai â€” <strong>kis ke hisaab se</strong> dekh rahe ho!</p>
<ul>
  <li>Bus ke andar baithe aapke liye pen <strong>rukha hua</strong> hai (aapke relative koi displacement nahi).</li>
  <li>Sadak par khade doston ke liye pen <strong>40 km/h se chal raha</strong> hai.</li>
  <li>Pen ke saath udte ek imaginary observer ke liye pen phir se rukha hua hai.</li>
</ul>
<p>Matlab: <strong>motion absolute nahi hai, wo hamesha kisi frame ke relative hota hai.</strong> Aur yahi se poora chapter shuru hota hai. Ye sirf philosophy nahi hai â€” isi se pata chalta hai ki Newton ke laws kis frame mein valid hain aur kis frame mein nahi.</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Frame of reference</p>
  <p><strong>A frame of reference is a coordinate system (a set of axes with a clock attached to it, i.e. an origin, three mutually perpendicular axes and a time-measuring device) relative to which the position and motion of an object are described.</strong></p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Hinglish mein: ek aisa "background set-up" jismein aap axes (x, y, z) aur ek ghadi (clock) fix karte ho, aur uske relative kisi bhi object ki position, velocity aur acceleration measure karte ho.</p>
</div>

<p>Ye ek important baat hai â€” frame mein sirf axes nahi, <strong>clock bhi</strong> hota hai. Kyunki velocity = displacement / time, to time measure kiye bina velocity ka koi matlab nahi.</p>

<p>Frame ke do hisse hote hain jo hum alag-alag study karenge:</p>
<div class="chips">
  <span class="chip"><strong>Translation</strong> â€” origin khisakta hai (moving train, lift)</span>
  <span class="chip"><strong>Rotation</strong> â€” axes ghoomte hain (Earth, merry-go-round)</span>
</div>
<p>Unit I ka part (a) translation par hai, part (b) rotation par.</p>

<h3 id="s1-2">1.2 Inertial aur non-inertial frame</h3>

<p>Newton ka first law kehta hai: <em>"Agar kisi body par net force zero ho, to wo apni state of rest ya uniform velocity banaye rakhti hai."</em> Lekin dhyaan se dekho â€” ye law <strong>kuch frames mein kaam karta hai, kuch mein nahi</strong>.</p>

<div class="stripe">
  <div class="box def">
    <p class="bt">Inertial frame</p>
    <p><strong>A frame in which Newton's laws of motion hold good, i.e. in which a body with no net external force on it has zero acceleration.</strong></p>
    <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Aise frames ya to "rest" mein hain ya constant velocity se chal rahe hain (constant velocity = no acceleration). Examples: Earth (approx), ek constant speed se chalti train, space mein dola-udta spacecraft.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="box warn">
    <p class="bt">Non-inertial frame</p>
    <p><strong>A frame which is accelerating (or rotating, or both) with respect to an inertial frame, and in which Newton's laws do not hold in their simple form â€” extra "pseudo forces" must be added.</strong></p>
    <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Examples: tez chal rahi train jab brake lage, lift jo upar ja rahi hai accelerate karte hue, ghoomta hua merry-go-round, rotating Earth (bahut chhoti acceleration, isliye use aksar inertial maan lete hain).</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h4>Ek thought experiment se samjho</h4>
<p>Aap ek aisi bus mein ho jo sudden brake lagati hai. Aapka pen (jo floor par rukha tha) aage ki taraf khisak jaata hai. Bus ke andar ke frame se dekho â€” pen par kisi ne bhi force nahi lagaya, phir bhi pen accelerate ho gaya! Newton's first law fail.</p>
<p>Lekin sadak par khade aadmi ke frame se dekho â€” bus brake lagi, pen ne apni <strong>inertia</strong> (aage chalte rahne ki tendency) ki wajah se apni speed maintain ki, aur bus uske neeche se nikal gayi. Bilkul consistent! Newton's law sahi chal raha hai.</p>
<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” asli baat</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Pen par koi force nahi tha â€” bus ke frame ki <strong>acceleration</strong> ne "extra" effect create kiya. Non-inertial frame mein aisa hi ek extra term aata hai jise hum <strong>pseudo force</strong> kehte hain (Chapter 2). Yaad rakho: inertial frame mein pseudo force ki zaroorat nahi, non-inertial frame mein zaroori hai.</p>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” kya "absolute inertial frame" exist karta hai?</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Physics mein perfect inertial frame koi nahi hai â€” Earth ghoom rahi hai, Earth Suraj ke around ghoom rahi hai, Suraj galaxy ke aroundâ€¦ Par agar acceleration bahut chhota ho, to use practical purposes ke liye inertial maan lete hain. Aise "lagbhag inertial" frame ko <em>laboratory frame</em> kehte hain. (Aur fundamentally, inertial frames ka idea hi special relativity ki taraf le jaata hai.)</p>
</div>

<div class="tw">
