<div class="box fact">
  <p class="bt">Did you know?</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Earth equator par apni axis ke around <strong>â‰ˆ 465 m/s</strong> ki speed se ghoom rahi hai, aur Suraj ke around <strong>â‰ˆ 30 km/s</strong> se. Phir bhi hum Earth ko lagbhag inertial frame maan lete hain â€” kyunki in rotations se paida hone wali accelerations (â‰ˆ 0.034 m/sÂ² centrifugal, Earth's surface par) hamare usual experiments ke liye bahut chhoti hain. Lekin lambi-duration wale experiments jaise <strong>Foucault's pendulum</strong> par ye asar saaf dikh jaata hai (Section 2.8).</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s1-3">1.3 Transformation of displacement, velocity and acceleration (translation ke liye)</h3>

<p>"Transformation" ka matlab: <strong>ek frame ke andar maapi gayi quantity ko doosre frame se kaise express karein.</strong> Ye syllabus ka core part hai aur CLO 2 ka poora target bhi.</p>

<p>Setup: Do frames lete hain.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Frame S</strong> (lab frame, inertial) â€” observer yahan baitha hai.</li>
  <li><strong>Frame Sâ€²</strong> â€” jo S ke relative <em>translate</em> kar raha hai (sirf khisak raha hai, ghoom nahi raha). Sâ€² ke origin ki position S mein <span class="m"><span class="vec">R</span>(t)</span> hai, aur Sâ€² ki velocity S ke relative <span class="m"><span class="vec">V</span>(t)</span> hai.</li>
  <li>Axes dono ke parallel hain (yani koi rotation nahi), aur suru mein dono ke origin overlap the.</li>
</ul>

