<h4>(i) Displacement ka transformation</h4>
<p>Diagram se seedha padho: particle ki position lab frame S mein <span class="m"><span class="vec">r</span></span> hai, aur frame Sâ€² mein <span class="m"><span class="vec">r</span>â€²</span> hai. Sâ€² ka origin S mein <span class="m"><span class="vec">R</span></span> par hai. To:</p>
<div class="eq">
  <span class="vec">r</span> = <span class="vec">R</span> + <span class="vec">r</span>â€²
  <span class="lbl">Position transformation (translation)</span>
</div>
<p>Yani <span class="m"><span class="vec">r</span>â€² = <span class="vec">r</span> âˆ’ <span class="vec">R</span></span>. Ye ek <strong>vector equation</strong> hai, matlab teen components ke liye teen scalar equations:</p>
<div class="eq">xâ€² = x âˆ’ X(t), &nbsp;&nbsp; yâ€² = y âˆ’ Y(t), &nbsp;&nbsp; zâ€² = z âˆ’ Z(t)</div>

<h4>(ii) Velocity ka transformation</h4>
<p>Ab dono taraf time ke respect mein derivative lete hain. Derivative lene ka matlab hai "rate of change" â€” aur ye sabse important idea hai:</p>
<ul class="deriv">
  <li>Start: <span class="m"><span class="vec">r</span> = <span class="vec">R</span> + <span class="vec">r</span>â€²</span></li>
  <li>Time derivative lo: <span class="frac"><span>d<span class="vec">r</span></span><span>dt</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>d<span class="vec">R</span></span><span>dt</span></span> + <span class="frac"><span>d<span class="vec">r</span>â€²</span><span>dt</span></span>
    <span class="whyline">Ye valid hai kyunki vector addition ka derivative term-by-term hota hai â€” koi assumption nahi lagayi.</span></li>
  <li>Pehchaan: <span class="m">d<span class="vec">r</span>/dt = <span class="vec">v</span></span> (particle ki velocity S mein), aur <span class="m">d<span class="vec">R</span>/dt = <span class="vec">V</span></span> (Sâ€² ki velocity S ke relative).
    <span class="whyline">dhyaan do: <span class="m">d<span class="vec">r</span>â€²/dt</span> bhi frame S ke time se liya gaya derivative hai, lekin kyunki axes parallel hain, ise hum <span class="m"><span class="vec">v</span>â€²</span> likh sakte hain (relative velocity frame Sâ€² mein).</span></li>
</ul>
<div class="eq">
  <span class="vec">v</span> = <span class="vec">V</span> + <span class="vec">v</span>â€² &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;âŸº&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="vec">v</span>â€² = <span class="vec">v</span> âˆ’ <span class="vec">V</span>
  <span class="lbl">Velocity transformation â€” classic relative velocity formula</span>
</div>
<p>Yahi wo formula hai jo aapne school mein padha tha: <em>"A ki velocity B ke relative = A ki velocity (ground) âˆ’ B ki velocity (ground)"</em>. Yahan bhi bilkul wahi baat hai.</p>

<h4>(iii) Acceleration ka transformation</h4>
<p>Ab velocity ka bhi derivative lete hain:</p>
<ul class="deriv">
  <li><span class="m"><span class="vec">v</span> = <span class="vec">V</span> + <span class="vec">v</span>â€²</span></li>
  <li>Derivative: <span class="frac"><span>d<span class="vec">v</span></span><span>dt</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>d<span class="vec">V</span></span><span>dt</span></span> + <span class="frac"><span>d<span class="vec">v</span>â€²</span><span>dt</span></span></li>
  <li>Pehchaan: <span class="m">d<span class="vec">v</span>/dt = <span class="vec">a</span></span>, &nbsp; <span class="m">d<span class="vec">v</span>â€²/dt = <span class="vec">a</span>â€²</span>, &nbsp; <span class="m">d<span class="vec">V</span>/dt = <span class="vec">A</span></span> (Sâ€² ki acceleration, jo ki "frame ki acceleration" hai).</li>
</ul>
<div class="eq">
  <span class="vec">a</span> = <span class="vec">A</span> + <span class="vec">a</span>â€² &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;âŸº&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="vec">a</span>â€² = <span class="vec">a</span> âˆ’ <span class="vec">A</span>
  <span class="lbl">General acceleration transformation (translating frame)</span>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why this is the key result</p>
  <p>Dekho ismein do cases ban rahe hain, aur poori Unit I ki neev yahi hai:</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Case 1: <span class="m"><span class="vec">V</span></span> constant</strong> â†’ <span class="m"><span class="vec">A</span> = d<span class="vec">V</span>/dt = 0</span> â†’ <span class="m"><span class="vec">a</span>â€² = <span class="vec">a</span></span>. Acceleration dono frames mein same! Frame S inertial hai to Sâ€² bhi inertial hai. Isse hi "invariance of Newton's laws" aata hai (Section 1.6).</li>
    <li><strong>Case 2: <span class="m"><span class="vec">V</span></span> constant nahi</strong> â†’ <span class="m"><span class="vec">A</span> â‰  0</span> â†’ <span class="m"><span class="vec">a</span>â€² = <span class="vec">a</span> âˆ’ <span class="vec">A</span></span>. Frame accelerate kar raha hai. Newton's law ko theek karne ke liye hum <span class="m">âˆ’m<span class="vec">A</span></span> naam ki <strong>pseudo force</strong> add karte hain (Chapter 2 mein detail).</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” sabse common galti</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Displacement aur velocity <strong>frame badalne par badal jaate hain</strong>, lekin (jab <span class="m"><span class="vec">A</span> = 0</span> ho to) acceleration <strong>nahi badalta</strong>. Students aksar acceleration ko bhi <span class="m"><span class="vec">a</span>â€² = <span class="vec">a</span> âˆ’ <span class="vec">V</span></span> likh dete hain â€” ye galat hai! <span class="m"><span class="vec">V</span></span> ghata ke velocity milti hai, acceleration ke liye <span class="m"><span class="vec">A</span></span> ghatate hain. Velocity mein <span class="m"><span class="vec">V</span></span>, acceleration mein <span class="m"><span class="vec">A</span></span> â€” ek hi letter, do jagah, do matlab.</p>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Example 1 â€” Lift ka sawal (non-inertial frame ka application)</p>
  <p>Ek lift <span class="m">a</span> = 2 m/sÂ² se upar accelerate kar rahi hai. Uske andar ek banda jiska mass 60 kg hai, floor par khada hai.</p>
  <p><strong>(a) Lab (ground) frame se:</strong> banda upar ki taraf accelerate kar raha hai, to net force upar hona chahiye:
  <span class="m">N âˆ’ mg = ma</span> âŸ¹ <span class="m">N = m(g + a) = 60(9.8 + 2) = 708 N</span>.</p>
  <p><strong>(b) Lift ke frame se:</strong> banda rest par hai, isliye net force zero hona chahiye. To hum ek pseudo force <span class="m">âˆ’m<span class="vec">A</span></span> (neeche ki taraf, magnitude <span class="m">mA</span>) add karte hain:
  <span class="m">N âˆ’ mg âˆ’ mA = 0</span> âŸ¹ <span class="m">N = m(g + a) = 708 N</span>.</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Dono frame answers identical!</strong> Yahi pseudo force ka poora point hai â€” non-inertial frame mein Newton's law ko "use karke" same answer milta hai. (Aapko lift mein zyada bhaari kyun lagta hai, iska reason bhi yahi hai.)</p>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Example 2 â€” Rain aur aadmi (relative velocity ka classic)</p>
  <p>Baarish vertically <span class="m">v_r = 10 m/s</span> se girti hai. Aadmi <span class="m">v_p = 5 m/s</span> se horizontally chal raha hai. Aadmi ko baarish kis angle par girti dikhegi?</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Aadmi ke frame mein baarish ki relative velocity <span class="m"><span class="vec">v</span>â€² = <span class="vec">v</span><sub>rain</sub> âˆ’ <span class="vec">v</span><sub>person</sub></span>.</p>
  <p>Components: vertical <span class="m">10 m/s</span> (neeche), horizontal <span class="m">âˆ’5 m/s</span> (aadmi ke peeche ki taraf).</p>
  <p><span class="m">vâ€² = âˆš(10Â² + 5Â²) = âˆš125 â‰ˆ 11.2 m/s</span>, aur vertical se angle <span class="m">tanÎ¸ = 5/10 = 0.5</span> âŸ¹ <span class="m">Î¸ â‰ˆ 26.6Â°</span>.</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Isliye chalte waqt aapko chhatri thodi aage ki taraf jhukani padti hai! Ye pure relative velocity transformation ka real-life proof hai.</p>
</div>

<div class="box quiz">
<p class="bt" style="color:#3a2f7a">Mini knowledge check 1</p>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q1. Kya Earth ko inertial frame maan sakte hain? Kyun / kyun nahi?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Approximately haan. Earth rotating hai (centripetal acceleration surface par â‰ˆ 0.034 m/sÂ² equator par, aur smaller elsewhere) aur revolution bhi karti hai. Ye accelerations <span class="m">g = 9.8 m/sÂ²</span> ke muqable bahut chhoti hain, isliye normal experiments ke liye Earth lagbhag inertial hai. Lekin long-duration precise experiments (Foucault's pendulum, ballistics, long-range artillery, cyclone direction) mein rotation ka asar clearly dikh jaata hai â€” us waqt Earth ko non-inertial treat karna padta hai.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q2. Ek constant velocity se chalti train inertial frame hai ya non-inertial?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Constant velocity = zero acceleration, isliye ye <strong>inertial</strong> hai. Dhyaan do: train ki velocity zero honi zaroori nahi â€” <em>constant</em> honi chahiye. Aur agar train brake ya accelerate kare (velocity constant na rahe), to frame turant non-inertial ban jaata hai.</p></div>
</details>
</div>

<h3 id="s1-5">1.5 Galilean transformation</h3>

<p>Ab hum upar wale general transformation ko ek <strong>special, most useful case</strong> mein apply karte hain: do inertial frames, jo constant relative velocity se translate kar rahe hain, axes parallel, aur dono clocks <span class="m">t = 0</span> par ek saath start hote hain.</p>

<p>Galileo ke time ka assumption: <strong>time absolute hai</strong> â€” matlab <span class="m">tâ€² = t</span>. (Ye assumption hum Chapter ke end mein "enrichment" box mein challenge karenge, jahan relativity ki taraf rasta khulta hai.)</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Galilean transformation (x-direction along V)</p>
  <p>Frame Sâ€² frame S ke relative <span class="m">x</span>-direction mein velocity <span class="m">V</span> se chal raha hai, aur <span class="m">t = 0</span> par origins coincident the:</p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:10px 0">
    xâ€² = x âˆ’ Vt, &nbsp;&nbsp; yâ€² = y, &nbsp;&nbsp; zâ€² = z, &nbsp;&nbsp; tâ€² = t
    <span class="lbl">Galilean (coordinate) transformation</span>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Inverse transformation (S ko Sâ€² se likhna) sirf <span class="m">V â†’ âˆ’V</span> karke milta hai:
  <span class="m">x = xâ€² + Vt</span>, &nbsp;<span class="m">y = yâ€²</span>, &nbsp;<span class="m">z = zâ€²</span>, &nbsp;<span class="m">t = tâ€²</span>.</p>
</div>

