<h4>Velocity transformation (Galilean)</h4>
<p>Upar wala general result <span class="m"><span class="vec">v</span>â€² = <span class="vec">v</span> âˆ’ <span class="vec">V</span></span> hi Galilean transformation se aata hai. <span class="m">x</span>-component likhne par:</p>
<div class="eq">
  <span class="frac"><span>dxâ€²</span><span>dt</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>d</span><span>dt</span></span>(x âˆ’ Vt) = <span class="frac"><span>dx</span><span>dt</span></span> âˆ’ V &nbsp;&nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp;&nbsp; vâ€²<sub>x</sub> = v<sub>x</sub> âˆ’ V
  <span class="lbl">Galilean velocity addition (classical)</span>
</div>
<p>Aur <span class="m">y, z</span> components: <span class="m">vâ€²<sub>y</sub> = v<sub>y</sub></span>, <span class="m">vâ€²<sub>z</sub> = v<sub>z</sub></span>.</p>

<h4>Acceleration transformation (Galilean)</h4>
<p>Ek aur derivative lo, aur yaad rakho <span class="m">V</span> constant hai (isliye <span class="m">dV/dt = 0</span>):</p>
<div class="eq">
  aâ€²<sub>x</sub> = <span class="frac"><span>dvâ€²<sub>x</sub></span><span>dt</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>dv<sub>x</sub></span><span>dt</span></span> âˆ’ 0 = a<sub>x</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="vec">a</span>â€² = <span class="vec">a</span>
  <span class="lbl">Acceleration is invariant under Galilean transformation</span>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” what changes and what doesn't</p>
  <div class="tw" style="margin:6px 0 0">
  <table style="min-width:420px">
    <thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Galilean transformation ke baad</th><th>Change?</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Position / displacement</td><td><span class="m">xâ€² = x âˆ’ Vt</span></td><td>Badalta hai</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Time interval</td><td><span class="m">tâ€² = t</span> (Î”tâ€² = Î”t)</td><td>Nahi badalta (absolute time)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Velocity</td><td><span class="m">vâ€² = v âˆ’ V</span></td><td>Badalta hai</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Acceleration</td><td><span class="m">aâ€² = a</span></td><td><strong>Nahi badalta</strong> â† yahi asli hero hai</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Force / mass</td><td><span class="m">Fâ€² = F</span>, <span class="m">mâ€² = m</span></td><td>Nahi badalta</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Length of a rod (Î”x)</td><td><span class="m">Î”xâ€² = Î”x</span></td><td>Nahi badalta (Vt cancel ho jaata hai)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
</div>

<h3 id="s1-6">1.6 Invariance of Newton's laws</h3>

<p>"Invariance" ka matlab: <strong>equation ka form dono frames mein same rehta hai.</strong> Ye dikhata hai ki koi bhi inertial frame "special" nahi hai â€” physics har inertial frame mein same language mein bolegi. Isi ko <strong>Galilean relativity (principle of relativity)</strong> kehte hain.</p>

<ul class="deriv">
  <li>Frame S mein Newton's second law: <span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span> = m<span class="vec">a</span></span>
    <span class="whyline">Assume: mass <span class="m">m</span> frame-independent hai (classical mechanics mein mass invariant hai).</span></li>
  <li>Galilean transformation se acceleration invariant: <span class="m"><span class="vec">a</span>â€² = <span class="vec">a</span></span>
    <span class="whyline">Ye humne Section 1.5 mein derive kar liya â€” <span class="m">V</span> constant hone ki wajah se.</span></li>
  <li>Force bhi frame-independent hai: <span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span>â€² = <span class="vec">F</span></span>
    <span class="whyline">Mechanical forces sirf bodies ki relative positions (aur unke derivatives) par depend karte hain â€” jaise spring force <span class="m">âˆ’k(xâ‚‚ âˆ’ xâ‚)</span>, gravity <span class="m">Gmâ‚mâ‚‚/rÂ²</span>. Sab relative quantities hain, to translation se nahi badalte.</span></li>
  <li>Substitute karo: <span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span> = m<span class="vec">a</span></span> âŸ¹ <span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span>â€² = m<span class="vec">a</span>â€²</span>
    <span class="whyline">Yani the form of the law is unchanged. Isi ko <strong>invariance of Newton's laws under Galilean transformation</strong> kehte hain.</span></li>
</ul>

<h4>Teeno laws ke liye ek-ek line</h4>
<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Newton's laws ki Galilean invariance</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Law</th><th>Statement</th><th>Galilean transformation ke under</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td><strong>First law</strong></td>
  <td>Net force zero âŸ¹ acceleration zero (rest ya uniform velocity).</td>
  <td>Invariant. Agar <span class="m"><span class="vec">a</span> = 0</span> frame S mein, to <span class="m"><span class="vec">a</span>â€² = <span class="vec">a</span> = 0</span> frame Sâ€² mein bhi. Yani "inertial frame" hone ki property transform karne par bachi rehti hai. <em>Isi liye kaha jaata hai: saare inertial frames equivalent hain.</em></td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><strong>Second law</strong></td>
  <td><span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span> = m<span class="vec">a</span> = d<span class="vec">p</span>/dt</span></td>
  <td>Invariant, kyunki <span class="m">m</span> aur <span class="m"><span class="vec">a</span></span> dono invariant hain (aur classical mechanics mein <span class="m">m</span> velocity se independent). Momentum: <span class="m"><span class="vec">p</span>â€² = m(<span class="vec">v</span> âˆ’ <span class="vec">V</span>)</span> badalta hai, lekin <span class="m">d<span class="vec">p</span>â€²/dt = d<span class="vec">p</span>/dt = <span class="vec">F</span></span> â€” equation ka form wahi.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><strong>Third law</strong></td>
  <td><span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span>â‚â‚‚ = âˆ’<span class="vec">F</span>â‚‚â‚</span> (action = âˆ’reaction)</td>
  <td>Invariant. Kyunki ye forces particles ke <em>relative</em> position <span class="m">(<span class="vec">r</span>â‚ âˆ’ <span class="vec">r</span>â‚‚)</span> par depend karte hain, aur relative position translation se nahi badalti: <span class="m">(<span class="vec">r</span>â‚â€² âˆ’ <span class="vec">r</span>â‚‚â€²) = (<span class="vec">r</span>â‚ âˆ’ <span class="vec">R</span>) âˆ’ (<span class="vec">r</span>â‚‚ âˆ’ <span class="vec">R</span>) = <span class="vec">r</span>â‚ âˆ’ <span class="vec">r</span>â‚‚</span>.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h4>Invariance ke consequences (exam mein likhne layak points)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Koi experiment <em>sirf apne inertial frame ke andar</em> karke ye nahi bata sakte ki aap "rest mein ho" ya "constant velocity se chal rahe ho". (Isi liye band kamre mein aap ye feel nahi karte ki Earth 30 km/s se ghoom rahi hai.)</li>
  <li>Kisi mechanical experiment ka result inertial frame ke choice par depend nahi karta.</li>
  <li>Isliye "absolute rest" ka koi physical meaning nahi â€” sirf relative motion ka hota hai.</li>
  <li>Conservation laws (momentum, energy, angular momentum) bhi translation ke under form-invariant rehte hain (<span class="m">V</span> constant ho to kinetic energy <span class="m">Â½m(<span class="vec">v</span>â€² + <span class="vec">V</span>)Â²</span> badalti hai, lekin <em>changes</em> aur conservation ki condition same rehti hai â€” Chapter 4 mein ise precisely dekhenge CM frame ke saath).</li>
</ul>

<div class="box supp-box">
  <p class="bt">Enrichment (syllabus se bahar, par useful context)</p>
  <p>Galilean transformation <strong>mechanics mein</strong> perfect kaam karta hai, lekin <strong>electromagnetism par fail</strong> ho jaata hai. Maxwell ke equations se light ki speed <span class="m">c = 3 Ã— 10â¸ m/s</span> milti hai â€” lekin kis frame ke relative? Galilean velocity addition se to light ki speed frame badalne par <span class="m">c âˆ’ V</span> aani chahiye thi. Michelsonâ€“Morley experiment (1887) ne aisa koi difference nahi dekha. Is contradiction ne Einstein ko special relativity tak pahunchaya, jahan Galilean transformation ki jagah Lorentz transformation lagti hai aur time bhi relative ho jaata hai.</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Important:</strong> Ye extra information hai. Aapka Unit I syllabus sirf classical (Galilean) transformation maangta hai â€” par ek achhe answer mein aap ye line likh sakte ho: <em>"Galilean transformation mechanical phenomena ke liye valid hai; non-mechanical (electromagnetic) phenomena ke liye Lorentz transformation ki zaroorat padi."</em></p>
</div>

<div class="qa">
<p class="q">Solved Example 3 â€” Galilean transformation ka calculation</p>
<p>Ek car <span class="m">20 m/s</span> se east ki taraf chal rahi hai. Ek doosri car uske peeche se <span class="m">30 m/s</span> (east) se aa rahi hai. Pehli car ke frame se: (a) doosri car ki velocity kya hai? (b) Agar doosri car 3 s mein 0 se 30 m/s tak accelerate hui ho, to pehli car ke frame mein uski acceleration? (c) 5 s mein relative displacement?</p>
<p class="a"><strong>Solution:</strong> Frames: S = ground, Sâ€² = car-1, jo <span class="m">V = +20 m/s</span> se chalti hai.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>(a)</strong> <span class="m">vâ€²â‚‚ = vâ‚‚ âˆ’ V = 30 âˆ’ 20 = 10 m/s</span> (east). Car-1 ke driver ko car-2 dheere se paas aati dikhegi, 10 m/s par.</li>
  <li><strong>(b)</strong> Acceleration ground frame mein <span class="m">a = Î”v/Î”t = 30/3 = 10 m/sÂ²</span>. Kyunki <span class="m">V</span> constant hai, <span class="m">aâ€² = a = 10 m/sÂ²</span>. <em>Acceleration transform karne par same rehti hai</em> â€” ye dikhane ke liye ki jab car-1 khud uniform velocity se chal rahi hai, wo inertial frame hai.</li>
  <li><strong>(c)</strong> Relative displacement <span class="m">Î”xâ€² = vâ€²â‚‚ t = 10 Ã— 5 = 50 m</span> (car-2 car-1 ke paas 50 m aa jaayegi). Cross-check: ground frame mein car-2: <span class="m">150 m</span>, car-1: <span class="m">100 m</span>, difference <span class="m">= 50 m</span> âœ“.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="box recap">
  <p class="bt">Quick Recap â€” Chapter 1</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Frame of reference = axes + clock; motion hamesha frame ke relative hota hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Inertial frame</strong>: Newton's laws directly valid. <strong>Non-inertial</strong>: accelerating/rotating frame, pseudo force chahiye.</li>
    <li>Translation transformation: <span class="m"><span class="vec">r</span> = <span class="vec">R</span> + <span class="vec">r</span>â€²</span>, <span class="m"><span class="vec">v</span> = <span class="vec">V</span> + <span class="vec">v</span>â€²</span>, <span class="m"><span class="vec">a</span> = <span class="vec">A</span> + <span class="vec">a</span>â€²</span>.</li>
    <li>Constant <span class="m"><span class="vec">V</span></span> âŸ¹ <span class="m"><span class="vec">A</span> = 0</span> âŸ¹ <span class="m"><span class="vec">a</span>â€² = <span class="vec">a</span></span> âŸ¹ Newton's laws invariant.</li>
    <li>Galilean transformation: <span class="m">xâ€² = x âˆ’ Vt, tâ€² = t</span>; velocity add/subtract hoti hai, acceleration nahi.</li>
    <li>Sab inertial frames equivalent hain â€” "absolute rest" à¤•à¤¾ koi physical test nahi hai.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box exam">
  <p class="bt">Exam corner â€” Chapter 1</p>
  <p><strong>Likhiye (short answer):</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Inertial aur non-inertial frame ki definition aur ek-ek example dijiye.</li>
    <li>Galilean transformation equations likhiye aur inki assumptions bataiye.</li>
    <li>Sabit kijiye ki Newton's second law Galilean transformation ke under form-invariant hai.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Detail mein (long answer):</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Translation wale frames ke liye displacement, velocity aur acceleration ka transformation derive kijiye. Dikhaiye ki jab frame constant velocity se chalta ho to acceleration invariant rehti hai.</li>
    <li>"All inertial frames are equivalent" â€” Galilean transformation ke aadhaar par ise explain kijiye aur iske do consequences likhiye.</li>
  </ol>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Numerical practice:</strong> Ek boat river ko cross kar rahi hai; boat ki speed still water mein 5 km/h, river flow 3 km/h. Boat ka net displacement aur time nikaliye dono cases ke liye: (i) boat perpendicular cross karti hai, (ii) boat river ke against-perpendicular angle par jhaanki jaati hai. (Hint: relative velocity transformation <span class="m"><span class="vec">v</span> = <span class="vec">V</span> + <span class="vec">v</span>â€²</span> use kijiye.)</p>
</div>
