<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 2 Â· Unit I (b)</span>Rotating Frames, Pseudo Forces, Coriolis Force &amp; Foucault's Pendulum</h2>

<p class="lead">Ab tak humne wo frames dekhe jo sirf <em>khisakte</em> the (translation). Ab axes <strong>ghoomne</strong> lagenge. Yahi se wo teen famous "extra forces" nikalte hain â€” centrifugal, Coriolis aur Euler. Aur yahi chapter batata hai ki <strong>Earth ki rotation ko hum kaise detect karte hain</strong> (Foucault's pendulum) aur cyclone ghoomte kyun hain.</p>

<h3 id="s2-1">2.1 Pseudo force ka idea (recap + deepening)</h3>

<p>Chapter 1 ka result dobara likhte hain: non-inertial frame mein</p>
<div class="eq"><span class="vec">a</span>â€² = <span class="vec">a</span> âˆ’ <span class="vec">A</span> &nbsp;&nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp;&nbsp; m<span class="vec">a</span>â€² = <span class="vec">F</span> âˆ’ m<span class="vec">A</span></div>

<p>Frame S inertial hai to <span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span> = m<span class="vec">a</span></span>. Frame Sâ€² ke andar kaam karne ke liye hum likhte hain:</p>
<div class="eq">m<span class="vec">a</span>â€² = <span class="vec">F</span><sub>real</sub> + <span class="vec">F</span><sub>pseudo</sub>, &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; jahan <span class="vec">F</span><sub>pseudo</sub> = âˆ’m<span class="vec">A</span></div>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Pseudo force (fictitious / inertial force)</p>
  <p><strong>A pseudo force is an extra apparent force which must be introduced in a non-inertial frame so that Newton's second law can be applied in that frame. It has no physical source (no other body exerts it), it is proportional to the mass of the object, and it vanishes when the frame becomes inertial.</strong></p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Ek line mein: pseudo force = Newton's law ko non-inertial frame mein "repair" karne wala term.</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Pseudo force ki 3 pehchaan (yaad rakho)</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Source (kisne lagaya?):</strong> koi object nahi. Ye frame ki acceleration ki wajah se "aata" hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Magnitude:</strong> always <span class="m">m</span> ke proportional (<span class="m">mA</span>, <span class="m">mÏ‰Â²r</span>, <span class="m">2mÏ‰v</span>, <span class="m">mrÎ±</span>). Isse pata chalta hai ki ye "force" nahi, actually frame ka effect hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Third law:</strong> iska koi "reaction pair" nahi hota. (Real force ke saath reaction hota hai, pseudo force ke saath nahi.)</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h3 id="s2-2">2.2 Rotating frame â€” pehle picture saaf karein</h3>

<p>Ek merry-go-round ya turntable imagine karo jo constant angular velocity <span class="m">Ï‰</span> se ghoom raha hai. Us par ek frame Sâ€² fix hai (jo body ke saath ghoomta hai), aur bahar khada observer frame S mein baitha hai (inertial). Dono frames ka origin same hai (sirf rotation hai), lekin Sâ€² ke axes ghoom rahe hain.</p>

<p><strong>Displacement ka transformation:</strong> Agar koi vector (say position vector <span class="m"><span class="vec">r</span></span>) dono frames mein likhein, to components different honge, jo ek rotation matrix se related hote hain. Simple 2-D case (frame Sâ€² z-axis ke around angle <span class="m">Î¸ = Ï‰t</span> se ghooma hai):</p>
<div class="eq">
  x = xâ€² cos Ï‰t âˆ’ yâ€² sin Ï‰t, &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; y = xâ€² sin Ï‰t + yâ€² cos Ï‰t
  <span class="lbl">Rotation transformation (displacement) â€” 2-D, z-axis ke around</span>
</div>
<p>Aur ulta: <span class="m">xâ€² = x cos Ï‰t + y sin Ï‰t</span>, <span class="m">yâ€² = âˆ’x sin Ï‰t + y cos Ï‰t</span>.</p>
<p><strong>Important insight:</strong> Length nahi badalti â€” <span class="m">xÂ² + yÂ² = xâ€²Â² + yâ€²Â²</span> (rotation length preserve karta hai). Lekin <strong>velocity badal jaati hai</strong>, kyunki component <span class="m">cos Ï‰t</span> time ke saath badalte hain. Isi wajah se rotating frame mein kuch "extra" acceleration terms nikalte hain.</p>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” ek badi confusion yahin chhupi hai</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Agar koi vector rotating frame mein <strong>fix</strong> ho (jaise pendulum ki rod, ya turntable par rakhi pencil), to uske components Sâ€² mein constant honge (<span class="m">d<span class="vec">A</span>â€²/dt = 0</span>), lekin inertial frame mein wo <strong>ghoom raha hai</strong> â€” matlab <span class="m">d<span class="vec">A</span>/dt = <span class="vec">Ï‰</span> Ã— <span class="vec">A</span> â‰  0</span>. Yahi baat agle section ka poora core hai.</p>
</div>

