<html><head><meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><section id="ch3">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 3 Â· Unit I (c)</span>Conservative Forces &amp; Potential Energy Curves</h2>

<p class="lead">Is chapter mein hum force aur energy ka rishta samjhenge. Do sabse kaam ki cheezein: (1) energy conservation â€” jo kaam lamba derivation kiye bina numerical solve karne deta hai, aur (2) <strong>potential energy curve (energy diagram)</strong> â€” ek graph dekh kar poora motion picture samajhna (kahan particle rukti hai, kahan oscillate karti hai, kahan se bhaag jaati hai). Ye graph BSc mein har jagah kaam aata hai â€” mechanics, atomic physics, molecular bonding, sab jagah.</p>

<h3 id="s3-1">3.1 Conservative aur non-conservative forces</h3>

<p>Pehle work ka recall karo: kisi force dwara chhote displacement <span class="m">d<span class="vec">r</span></span> mein kiya gaya work <span class="m">dW = <span class="vec">F</span> Â· d<span class="vec">r</span></span> hota hai, aur poore path ke liye <span class="m">W = âˆ«<sub>path</sub> <span class="vec">F</span> Â· d<span class="vec">r</span></span>.</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Conservative force</p>
  <p><strong>A force is said to be conservative if the work done by it (or against it) in moving a particle between two points depends only on the initial and final positions and not on the path followed; equivalently, the work done by the force around any closed path is zero, and the force can be derived from a scalar potential energy function as <span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span> = âˆ’âˆ‡U</span>.</strong></p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Simple Hinglish: "jo force path ki sochta hi nahi â€” sirf start aur end point dekhta hai."</p>
</div>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Non-conservative force</p>
  <p><strong>A force is non-conservative if the work done by it depends on the path followed (or is dissipative, i.e. it can convert mechanical energy permanently into other forms). Such a force cannot be derived from a potential energy function.</strong></p>
</div>

<h4>Teen tests â€” koi bhi force conservative hai ya nahi, kaise pata karein?</h4>
<ol class="deriv">
  <li>Path test: do alag-alag paths lo. Work same aaya? âŸ¹ conservative. Alag aaya? âŸ¹ non-conservative.
    <span class="whyline">Example: gravity â€” chhoti ladder se upar jao ya lambi slope se, <span class="m">W = mgh</span> hi milega.</span></li>
  <li>Closed-loop test: ek band (closed) path par poora work zero?
    <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">âˆ® <span class="vec">F</span> Â· d<span class="vec">r</span> = 0 &nbsp;âŸº&nbsp; force conservative</div>
    <span class="whyline">Friction ke liye ye zero nahi aata â€” kyunki wo hamesha movement ke opposite hai, to poore loop mein work hamesha negative.</span></li>
  <li>Curl test (3-D): <span class="m">âˆ‡ Ã— <span class="vec">F</span> = 0</span> â€” "curl zero" ho to force conservative hai.
    <span class="whyline">Aur us waqt ek scalar function <span class="m">U(<span class="vec">r</span>)</span> exist karta hai jisse <span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span> = âˆ’âˆ‡U</span>. (Technical detail: simply-connected region mein.)</span></li>
</ol>

<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Conservative vs non-conservative â€” full comparison</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Conservative force</th><th>Non-conservative force</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Work &amp; path</td><td>Path-independent</td><td>Path-dependent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Closed path par work</td><td><span class="m">âˆ®FÂ·dr = 0</span></td><td><span class="m">âˆ®FÂ·dr â‰  0</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Potential energy</td><td>Exist karti hai (<span class="m">F = âˆ’dU/dx</span>)</td><td>Exist nahi karti</td></tr>
