<tr><td>Curl</td><td><span class="m">âˆ‡ Ã— <span class="vec">F</span> = 0</span></td><td><span class="m">âˆ‡ Ã— <span class="vec">F</span> â‰  0</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Gravity, spring force, electrostatic (Coulomb), all central forces <span class="m">F = f(r)<em>rÌ‚</em></span></td><td>Friction, viscous drag, air resistance, normal force kabhi-kabhi (path-dependence), push by hand</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” 3 galtiyan jo har saal hoti hain</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>"Normal force aur tension bhi conservative hain?"</strong> Nahi, inka work normally zero hota hai (kyunki ye displacement ke perpendicular hote hain), lekin wo "conservative" nahi hain â€” unke liye potential energy define nahi hoti.</li>
    <li><strong>"Conservative force ka matlab force constant hai."</strong> Nahi! Conservative â‰  constant magnitude. Gravity <span class="m">1/rÂ²</span> ke saath badalti hai, phir bhi conservative hai. Conservative hone ka matlab sirf path-independence hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Sign ki galti:</strong> <span class="m">F = âˆ’dU/dx</span>. Slope (dU/dx) positive ho to force <strong>negative direction</strong> mein hota hai, yani particle ko "downhill" dhakelta hai. Isi liye particle always lower potential energy ki taraf jaata hai.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h3 id="s3-2">3.2 Workâ€“energy theorem se potential energy tak</h3>
<p>Ek particle par sirf conservative force lagta hai. Energy conservation ka derivation (3 lines, ekdum standard):</p>
<ul class="deriv">
  <li>Equation of motion: <span class="m">m áº = F(x) = âˆ’<span class="frac"><span>dU</span><span>dx</span></span></span></li>
  <li>Dono taraf <span class="m">áº‹</span> se multiply karo: <span class="m">m áº‹ áº = âˆ’<span class="frac"><span>dU</span><span>dx</span></span> áº‹</span>
    <span class="whyline">Ye legal hai â€” koi assumption nahi, sirf ek mathematical step.</span></li>
  <li>Pehchaan: <span class="m">m áº‹ áº = <span class="frac"><span>d</span><span>dt</span></span>(Â½máº‹Â²)</span> aur <span class="m"><span class="frac"><span>dU</span><span>dx</span></span>áº‹ = <span class="frac"><span>dU</span><span>dt</span></span></span> (chain rule).</li>
  <li>To: <span class="m"><span class="frac"><span>d</span><span>dt</span></span>(Â½máº‹Â² + U) = 0</span></li>
</ul>
<div class="eq">
K + U = Â½máº‹Â² + U(x) = E = constant
<span class="lbl">Mechanical energy conservation (sirf conservative forces ke liye)</span>
</div>
<p>Har symbol ka matlab: <span class="m">K</span> = kinetic energy (J), <span class="m">U</span> = potential energy (J), <span class="m">E</span> = total mechanical energy (J). <span class="m">E</span> ko "constant of motion" kehte hain â€” ye initial conditions se decide hoti hai.</p>

<p>Ulta rishta (force from potential energy) â€” 1-D aur 3-D dono forms:</p>
<div class="eq">
F<sub>x</sub> = âˆ’<span class="frac"><span>dU</span><span>dx</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; aur &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="vec">F</span> = âˆ’âˆ‡U = âˆ’(<span class="frac"><span>âˆ‚U</span><span>âˆ‚x</span></span><em>Ã®</em> + <span class="frac"><span>âˆ‚U</span><span>âˆ‚y</span></span><em>Äµ</em> + <span class="frac"><span>âˆ‚U</span><span>âˆ‚z</span></span><em>kÌ‚</em>)
<span class="lbl">Potential energy se force (aur 3-D mein gradient form)</span>
</div>

<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Common potential energies â€” yaad rakhne wali table</caption>
<thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Potential energy <span class="m">U</span></th><th>Force <span class="m">F = âˆ’dU/dx</span></th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
