<tr><td>Spring / SHM</td><td><span class="m">Â½kxÂ²</span></td><td><span class="m">âˆ’kx</span></td><td>mean position par <span class="m">U = 0</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Gravitation (far away)</td><td><span class="m">âˆ’GMm/r</span></td><td><span class="m">âˆ’GMm/rÂ²</span> (attractive)</td><td><span class="m">r = âˆž</span> par <span class="m">U = 0</span> (max)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coulomb (do charges)</td><td><span class="m">k qâ‚qâ‚‚/r</span></td><td><span class="m">k qâ‚qâ‚‚/rÂ²</span></td><td><span class="m">r = âˆž</span> par <span class="m">U = 0</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Molecular interaction (Lennard-Jones type)</td><td><span class="m">a/xÂ¹Â² âˆ’ b/xâ¶</span></td><td>Attraction + repulsion combined</td><td><span class="m">x = âˆž</span> par <span class="m">U = 0</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” gravitational U negative kyun hota hai?</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Kyunki hum reference <span class="m">U = 0</span> ko <span class="m">r = âˆž</span> (infinite separation) par rakhte hain. Do masses ko infinity tak alag karne ke liye <strong>energy deni padti</strong> hai (gravity unhe pakad ke rakhti hai) â€” matlab wo "bound" state hai, energy zero se kam. Isi baat ka test: kisi bound system ki total energy negative hoti hai. (Chapter 6 mein Kepler orbits mein yahi test kaam aayega: <span class="m">E &lt; 0</span> = bound orbit, <span class="m">E â‰¥ 0</span> = escape.)</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s3-3">3.3 Rectilinear motion under conservative forces</h3>

<p><strong>Rectilinear</strong> ka matlab: particle sirf ek straight line (jaise x-axis) par chalti hai â€” 1-D motion. Is case mein sab kuch 1 coordinate se describe hota hai, aur poori machinery simple ho jaati hai:</p>
<div class="eq">
m<span class="frac"><span>dÂ²x</span><span>dtÂ²</span></span> = âˆ’<span class="frac"><span>dU</span><span>dx</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; with &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Â½máº‹Â² + U(x) = E
</div>
<p>In do equations se particle ka poora motion describe hota hai. Kabhi-kabhi time nikalne ke liye ye integral use hota hai (formula ko yaad karne ki zaroorat nahi â€” samajhna kaafi hai):</p>
<div class="eq">
t âˆ’ tâ‚€ = âˆ«<sub>xâ‚€</sub><sup>x</sup> <span class="frac"><span>dxâ€²</span><span><span class="sqrt">2(E âˆ’ U(xâ€²))/m</span></span></span>
<span class="lbl">Motion ka "time" integral â€” sirf tab kaam karta hai jab E âˆ’ U(x) &gt; 0</span>
</div>
<p>Dhyaan do: <span class="m">E âˆ’ U(x) &gt; 0</span> hona <strong>zaroori</strong> hai â€” warna kinetic energy negative ho jaayegi, jo impossible hai. Jahan <span class="m">E âˆ’ U = 0</span> hota hai, wahan particle <strong>ruk jaati hai</strong> aur wapas murti hai. Aise points ko <strong>turning points</strong> kehte hain. Yahi idea aage energy diagram ka poora aadhaar hai.</p>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Solved Example 1 â€” Energy equation se speed nikalna</p>
  <p>Ek block <span class="m">m = 2 kg</span> spring se bandha hai, <span class="m">k = 200 N/m</span>, amplitude <span class="m">A = 0.1 m</span>. (a) Total energy? (b) <span class="m">x = 0.05 m</span> par speed? (c) Mean position par speed?</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>(a)</strong> Extreme position par <span class="m">v = 0</span>: <span class="m">E = U<sub>max</sub> = Â½kAÂ² = Â½(200)(0.1)Â² = 1.0 J</span></li>
    <li><strong>(b)</strong> <span class="m">Â½mvÂ² = E âˆ’ Â½kxÂ² = 1 âˆ’ Â½(200)(0.05Â²) = 1 âˆ’ 0.25 = 0.75 J</span> âŸ¹ <span class="m">v = âˆš(2Ã—0.75/2) = âˆš0.75 = 0.87 m/s</span></li>
    <li><strong>(c)</strong> x = 0 par <span class="m">U = 0</span>, to <span class="m">Â½mvÂ² = 1</span> âŸ¹ <span class="m">v = âˆš(2/2) = 1.0 m/s</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Check: <span class="m">v<sub>max</sub> = Aâˆš(k/m) = 0.1 Ã— âˆš100 = 1.0 m/s</span> âœ“ match.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s3-4">3.4 Potential energy curve (energy diagram) â€” graph padhna seekho</h3>

<p>Ye syllabus ka most useful "skill" wala topic hai. Ek graph: x-axis par position, y-axis par <span class="m">U(x)</span>. Usme ek horizontal line <span class="m">E</span> kheech do. Bas, ab aap poora motion <em>dekh</em> sakte ho â€” bilkul waise jaise ek roller-coaster track ka profile dekh ke pata chal jaata hai ki ball kahan tak jaayegi.</p>

