<h4>Curve padhne ke 5 rules (exam mein sabse zyada poochhe jaate hain)</h4>
<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Potential energy curve â€” reading rules</caption>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Graph par kya dekha jaaye</th><th>Physics ka matlab</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Force <span class="m">= âˆ’</span>(slope of curve)</td><td>Jahan curve girta hai (negative slope), force positive direction mein; jahan chadhta hai, force negative direction mein. Force <strong>always downhill</strong> hota hai.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td><span class="m">dU/dx = 0</span> (curve ka flat point)</td><td><span class="m">F = 0</span> â†’ <strong>equilibrium</strong>. Rest force ka matlab equilibrium; body wahin rest kar sakti hai.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Minimum <span class="m">(dÂ²U/dxÂ² &gt; 0)</span></td><td><strong>Stable equilibrium.</strong> Chhota displacement karo, force use wapas kheenchta hai. (Yehi ball cup ke bottom par rest karti hai.)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Maximum <span class="m">(dÂ²U/dxÂ² &lt; 0)</span></td><td><strong>Unstable equilibrium.</strong> Chhota push do, wo bhaag jaati hai (jaisa pahad ki choti par ball).</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Horizontal line <span class="m">E</span> milti hai jahan <span class="m">E = U</span></td><td><strong>Turning points</strong> â€” wahan <span class="m">K = 0</span>, speed zero, direction ulta. Particle in points ke beech "band" hai (bound motion). Curve ko cross na kar paana = unbound/escape.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” minimum par stable aur maximum par unstable? (Roti hui katori ka example)</p>
  <p>Katori ke bottom par marble rakho â€” chhoti si push do, wo hilti hai lekin wapas bottom ki taraf laut ke aati hai. Kyunki minimum ke aas-paas <span class="m">U</span> badh jaati hai, to force <strong>wapas centre ki taraf</strong> hota hai (restoring force).</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Ulta: katori ko ulta karke uske upar marble rakho â€” wo theoretically rest kar sakta hai, lekin zara sa bhi hile, to force use bahar ki taraf dhakel deta hai. Isi liye maximum par "equilibrium exists but is unstable". <em>Chhote displacement ke baad force ka direction hi decide karta hai</em> â€” yahi stability ka poora criteria hai.</p>
</div>

<h4>Orbit/molecule wala example: <span class="m">U(x) = a/xÂ² âˆ’ b/x</span></h4>
<p>Ye ek bahut important shape hai â€” isme bhi ek well hai (attraction) aur bahut chhote x par bahut zyada repulsion. Curiously, ye exactly wahi functional form hai jo central force mein "effective potential" banata hai (Chapter 6) â€” to aap ise yaad rakh lo.</p>
<ul class="deriv">
  <li>Force: <span class="m">F = âˆ’dU/dx = 2a/xÂ³ âˆ’ b/xÂ²</span>
    <span class="whyline">Chain rule: <span class="m">d(a xâ»Â²)/dx = âˆ’2axâ»Â³</span>, <span class="m">d(âˆ’b xâ»Â¹)/dx = +b xâ»Â²</span>. To <span class="m">F = âˆ’(âˆ’2a/xÂ³ + b/xÂ²) = 2a/xÂ³ âˆ’ b/xÂ²</span> âœ“</span></li>
  <li>Equilibrium: <span class="m">F = 0 âŸ¹ 2a/xÂ³ = b/xÂ² âŸ¹ xâ‚€ = 2a/b</span>
    <span class="whyline">Graph par yahi well ka saaf bottom hai.</span></li>
  <li>Stability: <span class="m">dÂ²U/dxÂ² = 6a/xâ´ âˆ’ 2b/xÂ³</span>; <span class="m">xâ‚€ = 2a/b</span> par ye <span class="m">= bâ´/(8aÂ³) &gt; 0</span> â†’ <strong>stable</strong> âœ“</li>
  <li>Well ki depth: <span class="m">U(xâ‚€) = âˆ’bÂ²/(4a)</span> â€” negative, kyunki bound state hai.</li>
  <li>Chhote oscillations ke liye: <span class="m">xâ‚€</span> ke aas-paas curve ko parabola maan lo (<span class="m">U â‰ˆ U(xâ‚€) + Â½ k<sub>eff</sub>(x âˆ’ xâ‚€)Â²</span>) â†’ simple harmonic motion, jahan <span class="m">k<sub>eff</sub> = dÂ²U/dxÂ²|<sub>xâ‚€</sub></span>.
    <span class="whyline">Ye ek bahut kaam ka general result hai: <strong>"har stable equilibrium ke aas-paas chhote oscillations SHM hote hain"</strong>. Isi se molecules ke vibration, atoms ki lattice vibration, sab model karte hain.</span></li>
</ul>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Solved Example 2 â€” Graph se poora motion batao</p>
  <p>Ek particle ki potential energy curve ka well <span class="m">xâ‚ = 1 m</span> aur <span class="m">xâ‚‚ = 3 m</span> ke beech hai (energy <span class="m">E = 4 J</span> par). Well ka minimum <span class="m">U<sub>min</sub> = 0</span> hai <span class="m">x = 2 m</span> par. Bataiye: (a) turning points, (b) <span class="m">x = 2 m</span> par kinetic energy, (c) <span class="m">x = 1.5 m</span> par speed agar <span class="m">m = 0.5 kg</span> aur <span class="m">U(1.5) = 1.2 J</span> ho, (d) kaunsa point par force zero hai.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>(a)</strong> Turning points <span class="m">x = 1 m</span> aur <span class="m">x = 3 m</span> â€” wahan <span class="m">K = 0</span>.</li>
    <li><strong>(b)</strong> <span class="m">K = E âˆ’ U<sub>min</sub> = 4 âˆ’ 0 = 4 J</span> (maximum speed yahin milegi).</li>
    <li><strong>(c)</strong> <span class="m">K = 4 âˆ’ 1.2 = 2.8 J</span> âŸ¹ <span class="m">v = âˆš(2Ã—2.8/0.5) = âˆš11.2 = 3.35 m/s</span></li>
    <li><strong>(d)</strong> Force zero wahan jahan curve flat ho, yani <span class="m">x = 2 m</span> (minimum) â€” stable equilibrium.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Note karo: kinetic energy maximum equilibrium par hoti hai, turning points par zero. Ye har well ke liye sach hai.</p>
</div>

<div class="box quiz">
<p class="bt" style="color:#3a2f7a">Mini knowledge check 3</p>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q1. Potential energy ki value kabhi negative kyun hoti hai?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Kyunki <span class="m">U</span> ki sirf <strong>differences</strong> ka physical matlab hai; zero-reference hum apni convenience se choose karte hain. Gravitational/Coulomb case mein reference <span class="m">r = âˆž</span> par rakhte hain, to bound states (jahan particle attached hai) negative U par aa jaati hain. Agar aap reference badal do (jaise spring ke liye mean position par zero), to values shift ho jaayengi â€” par force aur energy conservation same rahenge.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q2. <span class="m">U(x) = 5 J</span> constant ho (flat curve). Kya hoga?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a"><span class="m">F = âˆ’dU/dx = 0</span> â€” force zero. Ye <strong>neutral equilibrium</strong> hai (har point par). Particle apni velocity se seedha chalti rahegi â€” koi restoring, koi dhakka nahi. Ye "flat" case stable aur unstable ke beech ka third type hai.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q3. Ek particle ka total energy <span class="m">E</span> potential barrier ke <em>maximum se thoda kam</em> hai. Kya wo doosri taraf ja sakti hai?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Classically <strong>nahi</strong> â€” wo barrier ke pehle turning point par ruk jaayegi aur wapas aa jaayegi (bound). Lekin quantum mechanics mein ek non-zero probability hoti hai ki wo "tunnel" kar jaaye â€” isi ko <strong>quantum tunnelling</strong> kehte hain (alpha decay, tunnelling microscope isi par chalte hain). Ye aapke classical syllabus se bahar hai, par exam mein ek line ke liye achhi "extra" baat hai.</p></div>
</details>
</div>

<div class="box recap">
  <p class="bt">Quick Recap â€” Chapter 3</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Conservative force: work path-independent, <span class="m">âˆ®FÂ·dr = 0</span>, <span class="m">âˆ‡Ã—F = 0</span>, <span class="m">F = âˆ’âˆ‡U</span>, mechanical energy conserved.</li>
    <li>Non-conservative (friction, drag): path-dependent, energy dissipate, U exist nahi karti.</li>
    <li>Rectilinear motion: <span class="m">m áº = âˆ’dU/dx</span> aur <span class="m">Â½máº‹Â² + U(x) = E</span>.</li>
    <li>Energy diagram se pata chalta hai: equilibrium points (flat), stable (min), unstable (max), turning points (<span class="m">K = 0</span>), bound vs unbound motion.</li>
