<section id="ch4">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 4 Â· Unit II</span>Centre of Mass, Collisions, Angular Momentum &amp; Scattering</h2>

<p class="lead">Jab ek se zyada particles hote hain, to har particle ko alag-alag track karna boring aur mushkil ho jaata hai. Solution: ek <strong>representative point</strong> dhoondh lo jahan poora mass ek jagah maan sakte ho â€” <em>centre of mass</em>. Iske baad collisions, explosions, rocket, scattering â€” sab à¤†à¤¸à¤¾à¤¨ ho jaate hain.</p>

<h3 id="s4-1">4.1 Centre of mass â€” concept aur definition</h3>

<p>Ek example se shuru karo. Ek bat (cricket bat ya rod) ko hawa mein uchhal ke uska koi random point pakad ke spin karo â€” wo ek boring, wobbling motion karti hai. Lekin ek special point aisa hota hai jahan se pakad ke spin karo to wo smooth ghoomti hai. Wahi point <strong>centre of mass</strong> hai.</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Centre of mass</p>
  <p><strong>The centre of mass of a system of particles is that point whose position vector is the mass-weighted average of the position vectors of all the particles of the system:</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    <span class="vec">R</span> = <span class="frac"><span>mâ‚<span class="vec">r</span>â‚ + mâ‚‚<span class="vec">r</span>â‚‚ + â€¦ + m<sub>N</sub><span class="vec">r</span><sub>N</sub></span><span>mâ‚ + mâ‚‚ + â€¦ + m<sub>N</sub></span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>1</span><span>M</span></span> Î£ m<sub>i</sub><span class="vec">r</span><sub>i</sub>
    <span class="lbl">Discrete system (N particles, total mass M = Î£m<sub>i</sub>)</span>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Continuous body ke liye summation integral ban jaata hai:</p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    <span class="vec">R</span> = <span class="frac"><span>1</span><span>M</span></span> âˆ« <span class="vec">r</span> dm
    <span class="lbl">dm = Ï dV (ya Î» dl for wire, Ïƒ dA for lamina)</span>
  </div>
</div>

