<h4>Motion of the centre of mass (ye result dil se yaad karo)</h4>
<p>Ab <span class="m"><span class="vec">R</span></span> ka derivative lo â€” poori system ki internal complexity gayab ho jaati hai:</p>
<ul class="deriv">
  <li><span class="m">M<span class="vec">R</span> = Î£ m<sub>i</sub><span class="vec">r</span><sub>i</sub></span> (definition se)</li>
  <li>Time derivative: <span class="m">M<span class="vec">V</span><sub>cm</sub> = Î£ m<sub>i</sub><span class="vec">v</span><sub>i</sub> = <span class="vec">P</span></span> (system ka total momentum)</li>
  <li>Ek aur derivative: <span class="m">M<span class="vec">A</span><sub>cm</sub> = Î£ m<sub>i</sub><span class="vec">a</span><sub>i</sub> = Î£ <span class="vec">F</span><sub>i</sub></span></li>
  <li>Internal forces Newton's third law se pair mein cancel ho jaate hain (<span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span><sub>ij</sub> = âˆ’<span class="vec">F</span><sub>ji</sub></span>), sirf external forces bachte hain.</li>
</ul>
<div class="eq">
M<span class="vec">A</span><sub>cm</sub> = <span class="vec">F</span><sub>ext</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; aur &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="frac"><span>d<span class="vec">P</span></span><span>dt</span></span> = <span class="vec">F</span><sub>ext</sub>
<span class="lbl">Centre of mass ka equation of motion â€” ek particle jaise behave karta hai!</span>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” is ek line ka fayda samjho</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Bomb hawa mein phat jaaye: har fragment ka path complicated, lekin <strong>CM ka path wahi parabola</strong> rehta hai jo bina phatne wale bomb ka hota.</li>
    <li>Dono kharab khelte hue gymnasts ek doosre ko dhakka dein: unka combined CM pehle jaisa hi chalta hai (agar external force zero ho).</li>
    <li>Sun + Earth system: dono Sun (bhaari) ke around ghoomte dikhte hain, lekin CM (jo Sun ke andar hi hai, par centre par nahi) ke around dono orbit karte hain.</li>
    <li>Aur agar <span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span><sub>ext</sub> = 0</span> â†’ <span class="m"><span class="vec">P</span> = constant</span> â†’ <strong>momentum conservation</strong>. Isliye CM frame = "zero-momentum frame".</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Solved Example 1 â€” CM nikalna (simple numbers)</p>
  <p>Teen masses: 1 kg at (0,0), 2 kg at (3,0) m, 3 kg at (0,4) m. CM nikaaliye.</p>
  <p><span class="m">x<sub>CM</sub> = (1Ã—0 + 2Ã—3 + 3Ã—0)/6 = 1.0 m</span>; <span class="m">y<sub>CM</sub> = (1Ã—0 + 2Ã—0 + 3Ã—4)/6 = 2.0 m</span>.</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>CM = (1.0, 2.0) m.</strong> Cross-check: masses ke coordinates se ye point "6 kg ke weighted average" hai â€” heavier mass (3 kg) ki taraf y zyada kheencha gaya. âœ”</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s4-3">4.3 Centre of mass frame aur reduced mass</h3>

<p><strong>CM frame</strong> wo frame hai jismein system ka CM rest par hai. Iske do bahut bade fayde hain: (1) total momentum zero, (2) two-particle problem ek-particle problem ban jaati hai.</p>

<h4>(i) Position vectors ka division</h4>
<p>Two particles ke liye relative vector <span class="m"><span class="vec">r</span> = <span class="vec">r</span>â‚ âˆ’ <span class="vec">r</span>â‚‚</span> define karo. Thoda algebra se (definition <span class="m"><span class="vec">R</span> = (mâ‚<span class="vec">r</span>â‚ + mâ‚‚<span class="vec">r</span>â‚‚)/M</span> solve karke):</p>
<div class="eq">
<span class="vec">r</span>â‚ = <span class="vec">R</span> + <span class="frac"><span>mâ‚‚</span><span>M</span></span><span class="vec">r</span>, &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="vec">r</span>â‚‚ = <span class="vec">R</span> âˆ’ <span class="frac"><span>mâ‚</span><span>M</span></span><span class="vec">r</span>
<span class="lbl">M = mâ‚ + mâ‚‚</span>
</div>
<p>Matlab: CM ke frame mein dekhne par particle 1 CM se <span class="m">(mâ‚‚/M)r</span> door hai aur particle 2 <span class="m">(mâ‚/M)r</span> door â€” bhaari particle CM ke zyada paas, halki zyada door. (Visual: see-saw par bhaari banda centre ke paas baithta hai.)</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Reduced mass</p>
  <p><strong>For a two-particle system the relative motion is equivalent to the motion of a single particle of "reduced mass" <span class="m">Î¼ = mâ‚mâ‚‚/(mâ‚ + mâ‚‚)</span> acted upon by the same mutual force, while the centre of mass moves freely.</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    Î¼ = <span class="frac"><span>mâ‚mâ‚‚</span><span>mâ‚ + mâ‚‚</span></span>, &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Î¼<span class="frac"><span>dÂ²<span class="vec">r</span></span><span>dtÂ²</span></span> = <span class="vec">F</span><sub>1</sub> (relative motion ka equation)
  </div>
</div>

<h4>(ii) Kinetic energy ka decomposition (bahut kaam aata hai)</h4>
<p>Total KE do hisso mein toot jaati hai â€” "CM ki energy" aur "relative (internal) energy":</p>
<div class="eq">
K = Â½M V<sub>cm</sub>Â² &nbsp;+&nbsp; Â½Î¼ v<sub>rel</sub>Â²
<span class="lbl">K<sub>cm</sub> (translation of the whole system) + K<sub>rel</sub> (internal kinetic energy)</span>
</div>
<p>Ye result bahut powerful hai: collisions mein "kitni energy andar available hai" (jaise excitation ya bond tootne ke liye) sirf <span class="m">K<sub>rel</sub></span> hai â€” <span class="m">K<sub>cm</sub></span> kabhi kaam nahi aati, kyunki CM ki velocity conserve rehti hai.</p>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” CM frame is called the "zero-momentum frame"</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">By definition CM frame mein CM rest par hai, to <span class="m"><span class="vec">P</span><sub>cm frame</sub> = M<span class="vec">V</span><sub>cm</sub> = 0</span>. Two-particle case mein iska simple matlab: <span class="m">mâ‚<span class="vec">v</span>â‚â€² = âˆ’mâ‚‚<span class="vec">v</span>â‚‚â€²</span> â€” dono particle ke momenta <strong>equal magnitude, opposite direction</strong>. Isi wajah se CM frame mein collision ke calculation 10 guna simple ho jaate hain (Section 4.4).</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s4-4">4.4 Collision of two particles â€” 1-D (elastic &amp; inelastic)</h3>

<p><strong>Collision</strong> = do bodies ka short-time interaction jismein bahut bade internal forces lagte hain, par bahar se net force ~zero hota hai. Isliye <strong>momentum hamesha conserve</strong> hota hai (elastic aur inelastic dono mein!). Energy sirf <em>elastic</em> mein conserved rehti hai.</p>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why momentum always conserves</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Collision ke dauran lagne wale forces internal hain (dono bodies ek doosre par), aur internal forces Newton's third law se cancel ho jaate hain. Isliye <span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span><sub>ext</sub> â‰ˆ 0</span> â†’ <span class="m">Î”<span class="vec">P</span> = 0</span> â†’ <span class="m">mâ‚<span class="vec">u</span>â‚ + mâ‚‚<span class="vec">u</span>â‚‚ = mâ‚<span class="vec">v</span>â‚ + mâ‚‚<span class="vec">v</span>â‚‚</span>. Ye poori collision theory ka base hai. (Aur <span class="m">Î”t</span> chhota hone ki wajah se external forces jaise gravity ka impulse <span class="m">mgÎ”t</span> negligible rehta hai.)</p>
</div>

<h4>(A) Perfectly inelastic collision (easiest case â€” pehle ise karo)</h4>
<p>Dono bodies chipak jaati hain, common velocity <span class="m">v</span> se chalti hain:</p>
<div class="eq">
mâ‚uâ‚ + mâ‚‚uâ‚‚ = (mâ‚ + mâ‚‚)v âŸ¹ v = <span class="frac"><span>mâ‚uâ‚ + mâ‚‚uâ‚‚</span><span>mâ‚ + mâ‚‚</span></span> = V<sub>cm</sub>
<span class="lbl">Note: common velocity exactly CM ki velocity hoti hai!</span>
</div>
<p>Energy loss (jab <span class="m">uâ‚‚ = 0</span>): <span class="m">Î”K = Â½ <span class="frac"><span>mâ‚mâ‚‚</span><span>mâ‚+mâ‚‚</span></span> uâ‚Â² = Â½Î¼uâ‚Â²</span> â€” yani <strong>poori relative kinetic energy chali jaati hai</strong>. Numerical example: <span class="m">mâ‚ = 2 kg</span>, <span class="m">uâ‚ = 3 m/s</span>, <span class="m">mâ‚‚ = 4 kg</span> rest: <span class="m">v = 6/6 = 1 m/s</span>; <span class="m">K<sub>i</sub> = 9 J</span>, <span class="m">K<sub>f</sub> = Â½(6)(1Â²) = 3 J</span> â†’ loss = 6 J = <span class="m">Â½Î¼uâ‚Â² = Â½Ã—(8/6)Ã—9 = 6 J</span> âœ“</p>

<h4>(B) Elastic collision â€” 1-D derivation</h4>
<p>Two equations: momentum conservation + kinetic energy conservation. Solve karke (standard algebra, ek baar khud se likh lo):</p>
<div class="eq">
vâ‚ = <span class="frac"><span>mâ‚ âˆ’ mâ‚‚</span><span>mâ‚ + mâ‚‚</span></span>uâ‚ + <span class="frac"><span>2mâ‚‚</span><span>mâ‚ + mâ‚‚</span></span>uâ‚‚ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; vâ‚‚ = <span class="frac"><span>2mâ‚</span><span>mâ‚ + mâ‚‚</span></span>uâ‚ + <span class="frac"><span>mâ‚‚ âˆ’ mâ‚</span><span>mâ‚ + mâ‚‚</span></span>uâ‚‚
<span class="lbl">1-D elastic collision â€” general result (v = final, u = initial)</span>
</div>
<p>Agar target rest par hai (<span class="m">uâ‚‚ = 0</span>):</p>
<div class="eq">
vâ‚ = <span class="frac"><span>mâ‚ âˆ’ mâ‚‚</span><span>mâ‚ + mâ‚‚</span></span>uâ‚, &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; vâ‚‚ = <span class="frac"><span>2mâ‚</span><span>mâ‚ + mâ‚‚</span></span>uâ‚, &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; aur relative velocity ulti ho jaati hai: vâ‚‚ âˆ’ vâ‚ = âˆ’(uâ‚‚ âˆ’ uâ‚)
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Derivation (beautiful CM-frame trick â€” exam mein impress karega)</p>
  <p>CM frame mein lo. Wahan <span class="m">mâ‚<span class="vec">v</span>â‚â€² = âˆ’mâ‚‚<span class="vec">v</span>â‚‚â€²</span>, aur elastic hone ka matlab: har particle ki speed CM frame mein <strong>wahi rehti hai</strong>, sirf direction palat jaati hai.</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>CM velocity: <span class="m">V = mâ‚uâ‚/M</span> (jab <span class="m">uâ‚‚ = 0</span>).</li>
    <li>CM frame mein initial: <span class="m">uâ‚â€² = uâ‚ âˆ’ V = mâ‚‚uâ‚/M</span>, <span class="m">uâ‚‚â€² = âˆ’V</span>.</li>
    <li>Elastic hone se final: <span class="m">vâ‚â€² = âˆ’uâ‚â€² = âˆ’mâ‚‚uâ‚/M</span>, <span class="m">vâ‚‚â€² = âˆ’uâ‚‚â€² = +V = mâ‚uâ‚/M</span>.</li>
    <li>Wapas lab frame mein: <span class="m">vâ‚ = V + vâ‚â€² = mâ‚uâ‚/M âˆ’ mâ‚‚uâ‚/M = (mâ‚âˆ’mâ‚‚)uâ‚/M</span> âœ” aur <span class="m">vâ‚‚ = V + vâ‚‚â€² = 2mâ‚uâ‚/M</span> âœ”</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="tw">
