<h3 id="s4-5">4.5 Collision in two dimensions</h3>

<p>Ab collisions plane mein hote hain (jaise billiards, ya Î±-particle ka nucleus se scatter hona). Yahan momentum conservation <strong>do equations</strong> deta hai (x aur y), aur elastic case mein energy conservation teesri equation.</p>

<h4>Setup</h4>
<p>Particle 1 (mass <span class="m">mâ‚</span>, speed <span class="m">uâ‚</span>) x-direction mein aata hai; particle 2 (mass <span class="m">mâ‚‚</span>) rest par hai. Collision ke baad particle 1 angle <span class="m">Î¸â‚</span> par <span class="m">vâ‚</span> se, aur particle 2 angle <span class="m">Î¸â‚‚</span> par <span class="m">vâ‚‚</span> se jaati hai (generally opposite sides mein, kyunki net y-momentum zero tha).</p>
<div class="eq">
mâ‚uâ‚ = mâ‚vâ‚cosÎ¸â‚ + mâ‚‚vâ‚‚cosÎ¸â‚‚ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(x-momentum)<br>
0 = mâ‚vâ‚sinÎ¸â‚ âˆ’ mâ‚‚vâ‚‚sinÎ¸â‚‚ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(y-momentum)<br>
Â½mâ‚uâ‚Â² = Â½mâ‚vâ‚Â² + Â½mâ‚‚vâ‚‚Â² &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(KE â€” sirf elastic ke liye)
</div>

<h4>CM frame se lab frame â€” key relations</h4>
<p>CM frame mein (elastic case): har particle ki speed same rehti hai, direction <span class="m">Î¸*</span> se badal jaati hai. Lab frame mein wapas transform karne par:</p>
<div class="eq">
tan Î¸â‚ = <span class="frac"><span>mâ‚‚ sin Î¸*</span><span>mâ‚ + mâ‚‚ cos Î¸*</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="frac"><span>Eâ‚</span><span>Eâ‚€</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>mâ‚Â² + mâ‚‚Â² + 2mâ‚mâ‚‚cos Î¸*</span><span>(mâ‚ + mâ‚‚)Â²</span></span>
<span class="lbl">Lab-frame scattering angle &amp; final energy in terms of CM-frame angle Î¸*</span>
</div>

<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>2-D elastic collision ke important results</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Result</th><th>Comment</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><span class="m">mâ‚ = mâ‚‚</span></td><td><span class="m">Î¸â‚ = Î¸*/2</span> aur <span class="m">Î¸â‚ + Î¸â‚‚ = 90Â°</span></td><td>Billiards ka famous result: equal masses ki elastic collision ke baad dono <strong>perpendicular</strong> nikalte hain. (Player isliye 90Â° angle par aim karta hai!)</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">mâ‚ &lt; mâ‚‚</span></td><td><span class="m">Î¸â‚</span> 0 se <span class="m">180Â°</span> tak koi bhi ho sakta (0 se Ï€ tak full range)</td><td>Halki particle bhaari target se takraye â€” bounce back bhi ho sakti hai (jaise neutron kisi bhaari nucleus se).</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="m">mâ‚ &gt; mâ‚‚</span></td><td><span class="m">Î¸â‚ &lt; Î¸<sub>max</sub></span> jahan <span class="m">sin Î¸<sub>max</sub> = mâ‚‚/mâ‚</span></td><td>Bhaari particle chhote target ke saath <strong>limited angle</strong> tak hi mud sakti hai (e.g. Î± particle electron se).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Head-on (<span class="m">b = 0</span>)</td><td><span class="m">Î¸* = 180Â°</span>, <span class="m">Î¸â‚ = 0Â°</span></td><td>Maximum energy transfer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grazing (<span class="m">b</span> bada)</td><td><span class="m">Î¸* â†’ 0</span></td><td>Almost no deflection, no energy transfer.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Impact parameter &amp; scattering cross-section (aage kaam aayega)</p>
  <p><strong>Impact parameter <span class="m">b</span>:</strong> the perpendicular distance between the initial line of motion of the incident particle and the parallel line through the centre of the target. Bada <span class="m">b</span> = kam deflection.</p>
  <p><strong>Differential cross-section <span class="m">dÏƒ/dÎ©</span>:</strong> the effective area presented by the target which scatters the particle into a small solid angle <span class="m">dÎ©</span> at angle <span class="m">Î¸</span>. Mathematically <span class="m">dÏƒ/dÎ© = |db/dÎ¸| Â· b / sin Î¸</span>.</p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Ye do concepts Section 4.10 (nucleus se scattering) mein directly use honge. Cross-section ka unit: <span class="m">mÂ²</span> (ya "barn": <span class="m">1 barn = 10â»Â²â¸ mÂ²</span>).</p>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Solved Example 2 â€” 2-D inelastic collision</p>
  <p>Do identical cars (1 kg each â†’ aise lena easy hai) ek intersection par takrayi ruk jaati hain aur chipak kar ek saath chal deti hain. Car A east ki taraf 20 m/s, car B north ki taraf 10 m/s. (a) Final velocity? (b) Energy loss?</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Perfectly inelastic collision ka vector version:</p>
  <p><span class="m"><span class="vec">v</span> = <span class="frac"><span>m<span class="vec">v</span><sub>A</sub> + m<span class="vec">v</span><sub>B</sub></span><span>2m</span></span> = (10, 5) m/s</span> â†’ speed <span class="m">= âˆš(100+25) = 11.2 m/s</span>, direction <span class="m">tanâ»Â¹(0.5) = 26.6Â°</span> north of east.</p>
  <p><span class="m">K<sub>i</sub> = Â½(1)(400) + Â½(1)(100) = 250 J</span>; <span class="m">K<sub>f</sub> = Â½(2)(125) = 125 J</span> â†’ <strong>125 J loss</strong> (50 %).</p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Momentum conserve à¤¹à¥à¤† (vector sum same), kinetic energy nahi (aadhi chali gayi â€” deformation, heat, sound mein).</p>
</div>

<div class="box quiz">
<p class="bt" style="color:#3a2f7a">Mini knowledge check 4</p>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q1. Ek ball wall se <span class="m">e = 1</span> se bounce karti hai. Wall recoil nahi karti dikhti â€” momentum conservation fail?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Nahi, fail nahi hota. Wall bhi (bahut chhoti si) momentum leti hai â€” lekin wall ka mass Earth ke saath jura hua hai (â‰ˆ10Â²â´ kg), to uska recoil velocity practically zero hai. Ball ka momentum exactly ulta ho jaata hai; momentum Earth ke saath share ho jaati hai, aur Earth ke reference frame mein negligible dikhta hai.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q2. Elastic collision mein CM frame se dekhne par kya sabse simple hota hai?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">CM frame mein: (1) total momentum zero, (2) elastic hone par <strong>har particle ki speed unchanged</strong> rehti hai â€” sirf velocity ka direction badalta hai, aur (3) scattering angle <span class="m">Î¸*</span> se poori geometry describe ho jaati hai. Isliye har advanced collision topic (Rutherford scattering included) CM frame mein karta hai.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q3. Do identical balls ki elastic collision ke baad angle 90Â° kyun hota hai?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Equal masses ke liye CM frame mein dono ki speed same hoti hai aur dono diametrically opposite directions mein jaati hain. Lab frame mein CM velocity vector us circle ka centre ban jaata hai â€” yaad karo theorem: <em>circle ke diameter par bana angle 90Â° hota hai</em>. Isi liye <span class="m">Î¸â‚ + Î¸â‚‚ = 90Â°</span>. (Ye billiards ke "cut shot" ka geometric reason hai.)</p></div>
</details>
</div>
</section>
