<h3 id="s4-7">4.7 Slowing down of neutrons in a moderator (nuclear reactor ka physics)</h3>

<p>Ye syllabus ka wo topic hai jo "textbook mechanics" ko real nuclear technology se jodta hai. Kahani simple hai:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Nuclear fission (uranium ke andar) se neutrons nikalte hain jo <strong>fast</strong> hote hain â€” energy <span class="m">â‰ˆ 1â€“2 MeV</span>.</li>
  <li>Lekin further fission karne ke liye <strong>slow (thermal) neutrons</strong> chahiye â€” energy <span class="m">â‰ˆ 0.025 eV</span> (room temperature ke hisaab se).</li>
  <li>To neutrons ko slow karna padta hai â€” aur ye kaam hota hai <strong>elastic collisions</strong> se, ek "moderator" material ke nuclei ke saath.</li>
</ul>
<p>Question: neutron ko kaunse nucleus se takrana chahiye, aur kitni baar? Iska jawab poori tarah Unit II ke collision formulas se nikalta hai.</p>

<h4>Elastic collision mein energy transfer ka analysis</h4>
<p>Neutron (mass <span class="m">m</span>, initial energy <span class="m">Eâ‚€</span>) kisi nucleus (mass <span class="m">M</span>, initially rest) se elastic takrata hai. Section 4.5 ka CM-frame result use karo (jahan <span class="m">mâ‚ = m</span>, <span class="m">mâ‚‚ = M</span>, aur <span class="m">Î¸*</span> = CM-frame scattering angle):</p>
<div class="eq">
<span class="frac"><span>Eâ€²</span><span>Eâ‚€</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>mÂ² + MÂ² + 2mM cos Î¸*</span><span>(m + M)Â²</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp;&nbsp; maximum loss jab <span class="m">cos Î¸* = âˆ’1</span> (head-on)
<span class="lbl">Neutron ki final energy â€” elastic scattering</span>
</div>
<p>Head-on case (<span class="m">Î¸* = 180Â°</span>) mein minimum energy:</p>
<div class="eq">
<span class="frac"><span>Eâ€²<sub>min</sub></span><span>Eâ‚€</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>(M âˆ’ m)Â²</span><span>(M + m)Â²</span></span> â‰¡ Î±
<span class="lbl">Maximum possible fractional energy loss per collision</span>
</div>

