<html><head><meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><section id="ch5">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 5 Â· Unit II (Rigid Body)</span>Rigid Body Dynamics â€” Rotation, Inertia &amp; Spinning Top</h2>

<p class="lead">Ek rigid body = aisi body jismein saare particles ke beech ki distances <strong>fix</strong> rehti hain (koi stretch, koi bend nahi). Aisi body mein do tarah ki motion hoti hai: (1) translation (CM ka movement), aur (2) rotation (CM ke around orientation badalna). Unit II ka pehla half CM ka tha; ab doosra half <strong>rotation</strong> ka hai â€” aur yahin "inertia tensor", "principal axes" aur "spinning top ka precession" jaise concepts aate hain.</p>

<h3 id="s5-1">5.1 Rigid body ke basics</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Rigid body</p>
  <p><strong>A rigid body is a system of particles in which the distances between any two particles remain fixed (invariant) for all time, whatever forces act on it.</strong></p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Real bodies thodi deform hoti hain, lekin agar deformation rotation ki scale se negligible ho, to rigid-body approximation (bilkul valid) hai.</p>
</div>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Degrees of freedom:</strong> 6 â€” 3 translational (CM ki position) + 3 rotational (orientation, jaise Euler angles <span class="m">Ï†, Î¸, Ïˆ</span>). Agar body ek point par fixed ho, to sirf 3 rotational DOF bachte hain.</li>
  <li><strong>Planar motion</strong> (jaise wheel rolling on a road) mein: 2 translational + 1 rotational = 3 DOF.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="s5-2">5.2 Equation of motion of a rotating body</h3>

<p>Chapter 4 ka general result yaad karo: <span class="m">d<span class="vec">L</span>/dt = <span class="vec">Ï„</span><sub>ext</sub></span>. Rigid body ke liye yehi equation rotation ki "Newton's second law" hai.</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Equation of motion of a rotating body (general form)</p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    <span class="vec">Ï„</span><sub>ext</sub> = <span class="frac"><span>d<span class="vec">L</span></span><span>dt</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>d</span><span>dt</span></span>(<span class="vec">ð•€</span> Â· <span class="vec">Ï‰</span>) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="up">(space-fixed frame mein)</span>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Fixed axis ke around rotation ke simple case mein <span class="m">L<sub>axis</sub> = IÏ‰</span> aur:</p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    Ï„<sub>axis</sub> = IÎ± = I <span class="frac"><span>dÏ‰</span><span>dt</span></span>
    <span class="lbl">Fixed axis rotation â€” "rotational Newton's second law" (Î± = angular acceleration)</span>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Lekin dhyaan do: agar <span class="m">L</span> axis ke along nahi hai, to <span class="m">Ï„ = IÎ±</span> jaise simple relation kaafi nahi hota â€” tab humein inertia tensor (<span class="m"><span class="vec">ð•€</span></span>) chahiye, aur hi asli "rigid body dynamics" shuru hoti hai. Agle sections isi par hain.</p>

<h3 id="s5-3">5.3 Inertial coefficients (moments &amp; products of inertia)</h3>

<p>Translation mein mass hota hai â€” "kitna push chahiye acceleration ke liye". Rotation mein uski jagah <strong>inertia coefficients</strong> aate hain â€” "kitna torque chahiye angular acceleration ke liye". Aur rotation mein ye ek single number nahi, ek <em>tensor</em> hota hai (kyunki axis ki direction matter karti hai).</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Inertia tensor (inertial coefficients)</p>
  <p>Ek rigid body (continuous, density <span class="m">Ï</span>) ke liye 9 coefficients define hote hain:</p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    I<sub>xx</sub> = âˆ« Ï(yÂ² + zÂ²)dV, &nbsp; I<sub>yy</sub> = âˆ« Ï(xÂ² + zÂ²)dV, &nbsp; I<sub>zz</sub> = âˆ« Ï(xÂ² + yÂ²)dV
    <span class="lbl">Moments of inertia (diagonal terms)</span>
  </div>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    I<sub>xy</sub> = I<sub>yx</sub> = âˆ’âˆ« Ï x y dV, &nbsp; I<sub>yz</sub> = I<sub>zy</sub> = âˆ’âˆ« Ï y z dV, &nbsp; I<sub>zx</sub> = I<sub>xz</sub> = âˆ’âˆ« Ï z x dV
    <span class="lbl">Products of inertia (off-diagonal terms)</span>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">In sab ko mila kar matrix form mein likhte hain (symmetric tensor):</p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    <span class="vec">ð•€</span> = [ I<sub>xx</sub> &nbsp; I<sub>xy</sub> &nbsp; I<sub>xz</sub><br>
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; I<sub>yx</sub> &nbsp; I<sub>yy</sub> &nbsp; I<sub>yz</sub><br>
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; I<sub>zx</sub> &nbsp; I<sub>zy</sub> &nbsp; I<sub>zz</sub> ] &nbsp;&nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="vec">L</span> = <span class="vec">ð•€</span><span class="vec">Ï‰</span>, &nbsp; i.e. L<sub>i</sub> = Î£<sub>j</sub> I<sub>ij</sub>Ï‰<sub>j</sub>
  </div>
</div>

