</div>
<p class="small"><strong>Radius of gyration:</strong> <span class="m">k</span> aisa distance hai ki <span class="m">I = MkÂ²</span> â€” iska matlab: poora mass ek ring par <span class="m">k</span> radius par rakh do, to same inertia milegi.</p>

<h3 id="s5-4">5.4 Case of <span class="m">J</span> (angular momentum) NOT parallel to <span class="m">Ï‰</span></h3>

<p>Ye syllabus ka sabse important aur sabse "confusing" concept hai â€” isliye ise dheere se samjhein. (Note: kai kitaabein angular momentum ko <span class="m">J</span> likhti hain, aur angular velocity ko <span class="m">Ï‰</span>; yahan hum <span class="m">L â‰¡ J</span> use kar rahe hain.)</p>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Pehle intuition: <span class="m">L</span> aur <span class="m">Ï‰</span> parallel kab hote hain?</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Simple case (school wala):</strong> symmetric body (disc, ring, sphere, cylinder) apni symmetry axis ke around ghoom rahi ho. Tab <span class="m">L = IÏ‰</span> aur <span class="m">L âˆ¥ Ï‰</span> â€” sab simple.</li>
    <li><strong>General case (BSc wala):</strong> body ka shape kisi bhi axis ke around asymmetric ho, ya rotation axis symmetry axis na ho. Tab mass ka distribution har direction mein different hai â€” to har point ka <span class="m">r Ã— mv</span> contribution alag-alag direction mein hota hai, aur unka vector sum <span class="m">L</span> âŸ‚ <span class="m">Ï‰</span> se alag direction mein nikal jaata hai!</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<p>Mathematically iska reason tensor equation hai: <span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span> = <span class="vec">ð•€</span><span class="vec">Ï‰</span></span>. Matrix se multiply karne par direction generally badal jaati hai â€” sirf tab nahi badalti jab <span class="m"><span class="vec">Ï‰</span></span> "eigenvector" ho. Us special direction ko hi <strong>principal axis</strong> kehte hain (Section 5.6).</p>

<h4>Concrete example: rectangular lamina ka diagonal axis</h4>
<p>Ek rectangular lamina (mass <span class="m">m</span>, sides <span class="m">a</span> aur <span class="m">b</span>, <span class="m">a = 2b</span>) xy-plane mein rakhi hai; x, y axes lamina ki symmetry axes hain. Phir:</p>
<div class="eq">
I<sub>xx</sub> = <span class="frac"><span>mbÂ²</span><span>12</span></span>, &nbsp;&nbsp; I<sub>yy</sub> = <span class="frac"><span>maÂ²</span><span>12</span></span> = 4I<sub>xx</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp;(kyunki <span class="m">a = 2b</span>), &nbsp;&nbsp; I<sub>xy</sub> = 0 (symmetry se)
</div>
<p>Ab body ko lamina ke plane ke andar, x-axis se <span class="m">45Â°</span> par ghoomaiye: <span class="m"><span class="vec">Ï‰</span> = Ï‰(cos45Â°, sin45Â°, 0) = (Ï‰/âˆš2)(1, 1, 0)</span>. To:</p>
<div class="eq">
<span class="vec">L</span> = <span class="vec">ð•€</span><span class="vec">Ï‰</span> = ( I<sub>xx</sub>Ï‰/âˆš2, &nbsp; I<sub>yy</sub>Ï‰/âˆš2, &nbsp; 0 ) = <span class="frac"><span>Ï‰</span><span>âˆš2</span></span>( I<sub>xx</sub>, &nbsp; 4I<sub>xx</sub>, &nbsp; 0 )
</div>
<ul>
  <li><span class="m"><span class="vec">Ï‰</span></span> ka x-axis se angle = <strong>45Â°</strong>.</li>
  <li><span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span></span> ka x-axis se angle = <span class="m">tanâ»Â¹(4I<sub>xx</sub>/I<sub>xx</sub>) = tanâ»Â¹(4) = <strong>76Â°</strong></span>.</li>
  <li>Yani <span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span></span> aur <span class="m"><span class="vec">Ï‰</span></span> mein <span class="m">â‰ˆ 31Â°</span> ka farq hai â€” <strong>L parallel nahi hai Ï‰ ke!</strong> âœ”</li>
</ul>

