<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” iska practical matlab (bahut important!)</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Jab <span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span> âˆ¦ <span class="vec">Ï‰</span></span> ho, to <span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span></span> space mein direction badalta rehta hai <em>chahe body constant <span class="m">Ï‰</span></em> se ghoom rahi ho. Iske liye <span class="m"><span class="vec">Ï„</span> = d<span class="vec">L</span>/dt â‰  0</span> chahiye.
      <ul>
        <li>Agar axis bearings se fix hai â†’ bearings ko periodically badalta hua <strong>torque</strong> dena padta hai (warna wo toot/fail ho jaayenge).</li>
        <li>Agar axis free hai â†’ body <strong>wobble</strong> karegi (axis hilta rahega).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Isliye</strong> car ke tyres, washing machine ke drums, engine ke crankshafts, sab <em>dynamically balanced</em> kiye jaate hain â€” mass itna adjust karte hain ki rotation axis ek principal axis ban jaaye, taaki <span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span> âˆ¥ <span class="vec">Ï‰</span></span> ho aur bearings par koi vibrating torque na aaye.</li>
    <li>Formula jo yaad rakhna chahiye (lamina, in-plane axis): <strong><span class="m">L<sub>âŠ¥</sub> = Ï‰ sinÎ¸ cosÎ¸ (I<sub>yy</sub> âˆ’ I<sub>xx</sub>)</span></strong> â€” Î¸ = Ï‰ aur principal axis ke beech ka angle. Isi se torque ka magnitude <span class="m">Ï„ = Ï‰ L<sub>âŠ¥</sub> = Ï‰Â² sinÎ¸cosÎ¸ (I<sub>yy</sub> âˆ’ I<sub>xx</sub>)</span>.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Solved Example 1 â€” dynamic imbalance ka torque</p>
  <p>Upar wali lamina: <span class="m">m = 1 kg</span>, <span class="m">b = 0.2 m</span>, <span class="m">a = 0.4 m</span>. To <span class="m">I<sub>xx</sub> = (1)(0.04)/12 = 3.33 Ã— 10â»Â³ kg mÂ²</span> aur <span class="m">I<sub>yy</sub> = 4I<sub>xx</sub> = 1.33 Ã— 10â»Â² kg mÂ²</span>. Body <span class="m">Ï‰ = 100 rad/s</span> par diagonal axis (Î¸ = 45Â°) ke around ghoom rahi hai. Bearing ko kitna torque dena padega?</p>
  <p><span class="m">Ï„ = Ï‰Â² sinÎ¸ cosÎ¸ (I<sub>yy</sub> âˆ’ I<sub>xx</sub>) = (100)Â² Ã— (0.5) Ã— (1.33Ã—10â»Â² âˆ’ 3.33Ã—10â»Â³) = 10000 Ã— 0.5 Ã— 1.0Ã—10â»Â² = 50 NÂ·m</span></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Ye ek bahut bada torque hai â€” ek 1 kg ki chhoti lamina ke liye! Isse samajh aata hai ki unbalanced rotating machines itni vibration kyun karti hain. Torque <span class="m">Ï‰Â²</span> ke saath badhta hai â€” speed double, torque 4 guna.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s5-5">5.5 Kinetic energy of rotation</h3>
<p>Figure 5.1 se: element <span class="m">dm</span> jiski distance <span class="m">Ï</span> hai, uski speed <span class="m">v = Ï‰Ï</span> hai, to uski kinetic energy <span class="m">dK = Â½(dm)(Ï‰Ï)Â²</span>. Poori body ke liye integrate:</p>
<div class="eq">
K = âˆ« Â½ ÏÂ² Ï‰Â² dm = Â½Ï‰Â² âˆ« ÏÂ² dm = Â½ I Ï‰Â²
<span class="lbl">Kinetic energy for rotation about a fixed axis</span>
</div>
<p>General (3-D, kisi bhi axis ke around) form:</p>
<div class="eq">
K = Â½ <span class="vec">Ï‰</span> Â· <span class="vec">L</span> = Â½ Î£<sub>i,j</sub> I<sub>ij</sub> Ï‰<sub>i</sub> Ï‰<sub>j</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp;&nbsp; (principal axes) &nbsp; K = Â½( Iâ‚Ï‰â‚Â² + Iâ‚‚Ï‰â‚‚Â² + Iâ‚ƒÏ‰â‚ƒÂ² )
<span class="lbl">General rotational KE â€” principal axes mein simple form</span>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” <span class="m">K = Â½<span class="vec">Ï‰</span>Â·<span class="vec">L</span></span> ka intuition</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><span class="m">K = Â½IÏ‰Â²</span> mein <span class="m">IÏ‰ = L</span> substitute karo (jab <span class="m">L âˆ¥ Ï‰</span>): <span class="m">K = Â½Ï‰L = Â½<span class="vec">Ï‰</span>Â·<span class="vec">L</span></span>. Translation ke <span class="m">K = Â½<span class="vec">v</span>Â·<span class="vec">p</span></span> ka bilkul analogue âœ“. Aur jab <span class="m">L âˆ¦ Ï‰</span> ho, tab bhi <span class="m">K = Â½<span class="vec">Ï‰</span>Â·<span class="vec">L</span></span> valid rehta hai â€” par <span class="m">L</span> ka sirf <span class="m">Ï‰</span> ke along component hi kaam karta hai (kyunki dot product hai).</p>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Solved Example 2 â€” rolling vs sliding (combined motion)</p>
  <p>Ek solid sphere <span class="m">M = 2 kg</span>, <span class="m">R = 0.1 m</span> rolling without slipping <span class="m">v = 2 m/s</span> par. Total KE?</p>
  <p>Rolling without slipping: <span class="m">v = Ï‰R âŸ¹ Ï‰ = 20 rad/s</span>. Sphere ke liye <span class="m">I = 2MRÂ²/5</span>.</p>
  <p><span class="m">K<sub>trans</sub> = Â½MvÂ² = Â½(2)(4) = 4 J</span>; &nbsp; <span class="m">K<sub>rot</sub> = Â½IÏ‰Â² = Â½ Ã— (2Ã—2Ã—0.01/5) Ã— 400 = Â½ Ã— 0.008 Ã— 400 = 1.6 J</span></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Total = 5.6 J</strong>, jismein <span class="m">4/5.6 â‰ˆ 71%</span> translation, <span class="m">29%</span> rotation. Interesant baat: rolling sphere ke liye <span class="m">K<sub>rot</sub>/K<sub>trans</sub> = 2/5</span> â€” ye ratio shape (I) par depend karta hai, mass par nahi. Isliye race mein hollow sphere solid sphere se dheera hota hai (uska <span class="m">I</span> bada hai).</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s5-6">5.6 Principal axes (aur principal moments of inertia)</h3>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Principal axes</p>
  <p><strong>Principal axes of a rigid body (about a given point O) are those mutually perpendicular axes for which the products of inertia vanish and the angular momentum is parallel to the angular velocity, i.e. <span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span> = I<span class="vec">Ï‰</span></span>. The corresponding moments of inertia are called the principal moments of inertia.</strong></p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Mathematically: principal axes = the eigenvectors of the inertia tensor; principal moments = uske eigenvalues (Iâ‚, Iâ‚‚, Iâ‚ƒ). Inertia tensor symmetric hai, isliye eigenvalues real hote hain aur eigenvectors perpendicular â€” isi liye principal axes "exist" karte hain.</p>
</div>

<h4>Principal axes kaise pehchane (kaam ke rules)</h4>
<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Symmetry se principal axes</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Body</th><th>Principal axes</th><th>Principal moments</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Koi bhi symmetry axis</strong></td><td>Ek principal axis hai (zaroor)</td><td>â€”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sphere (ya cube)</td><td>Koi bhi 3 mutually perpendicular axes (sab equivalent)</td><td><span class="m">Iâ‚ = Iâ‚‚ = Iâ‚ƒ</span> (spherical top)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Circular disc, cylinder, rod-like symmetric top</td><td>Symmetry axis + uske perpendicular koi bhi do</td><td><span class="m">Iâ‚ = Iâ‚‚ â‰  Iâ‚ƒ</span> (symmetric top)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rectangular box / lamina (a â‰  b â‰  c)</td><td>Teen symmetry axes hi principal axes</td><td><span class="m">Iâ‚ â‰  Iâ‚‚ â‰  Iâ‚ƒ</span> (asymmetric top)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kisi bhi body ke liye</td><td>Inertia tensor ke eigenvector nikaal kar (det(I âˆ’ Î»1) = 0 solve karke)</td><td>Eigenvalues</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” principal axis par <span class="m">L âˆ¥ Ï‰</span> kyun ho jaata hai?</p>
  <p>Principal axes wale coordinate system mein inertia tensor <strong>diagonal</strong> ho jaata hai (products of inertia zero). Tab:</p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0"><span class="vec">L</span> = <span class="vec">ð•€</span><span class="vec">Ï‰</span> = (Iâ‚Ï‰â‚, Iâ‚‚Ï‰â‚‚, Iâ‚ƒÏ‰â‚ƒ)</div>
  <p>Agar <span class="m"><span class="vec">Ï‰</span></span> sirf ek principal axis ke along ho (jaise <span class="m"><span class="vec">Ï‰</span> = (Ï‰, 0, 0)</span>), to <span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span> = (Iâ‚Ï‰, 0, 0)</span> â€” <strong>same direction</strong> âœ“ bas magnitude <span class="m">Iâ‚</span> guna. Isliye principal axis ke around freely ghoomti hui body stabil hoti hai: <span class="m">L</span> aur <span class="m">Ï‰</span> dono same direction mein rehte hain, koi time-varying <span class="m">L</span> nahi, to <span class="m">Ï„ = 0</span> kaafi hai â€” steady rotation possible hai bina kisi external torque ke.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s5-7">5.7 Euler's equations aur free rotation ki stability</h3>
<p>Ab hum chapter ka sabse important set of equations derive karte hain â€” body-frame mein rotation ka equation of motion. Ye <span class="m">Ï„ = d<span class="vec">L</span>/dt</span> ka body-frame version hai.</p>
<ul class="deriv">
  <li>Start: space frame mein <span class="m"><span class="vec">Ï„</span> = (<span class="frac"><span>d<span class="vec">L</span></span><span>dt</span></span>)<sub>space</sub></span></li>
  <li>Transport theorem lagao (Chapter 2 ka result â€” yahan <span class="m"><span class="vec">Ï‰</span></span> body ki angular velocity hai):
    <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">(<span class="frac"><span>d<span class="vec">L</span></span><span>dt</span></span>)<sub>space</sub> = (<span class="frac"><span>d<span class="vec">L</span></span><span>dt</span></span>)<sub>body</sub> + <span class="vec">Ï‰</span> Ã— <span class="vec">L</span></div></li>
  <li>Body ke principal axes use karo: <span class="m">Lâ‚ = Iâ‚Ï‰â‚, Lâ‚‚ = Iâ‚‚Ï‰â‚‚, Lâ‚ƒ = Iâ‚ƒÏ‰â‚ƒ</span>, aur body frame mein <span class="m">Iâ‚, Iâ‚‚, Iâ‚ƒ</span> constant hain.</li>
  <li>Component-wise likho (e.g., 1-component): <span class="m">Ï„â‚ = Iâ‚Ï‰Ì‡â‚ + (Ï‰â‚‚Lâ‚ƒ âˆ’ Ï‰â‚ƒLâ‚‚) = Iâ‚Ï‰Ì‡â‚ + Ï‰â‚‚Ï‰â‚ƒ(Iâ‚ƒ âˆ’ Iâ‚‚)</span></li>
</ul>
<div class="eq">
Iâ‚Ï‰Ì‡â‚ âˆ’ (Iâ‚‚ âˆ’ Iâ‚ƒ)Ï‰â‚‚Ï‰â‚ƒ = Ï„â‚<br>
Iâ‚‚Ï‰Ì‡â‚‚ âˆ’ (Iâ‚ƒ âˆ’ Iâ‚)Ï‰â‚ƒÏ‰â‚ = Ï„â‚‚<br>
Iâ‚ƒÏ‰Ì‡â‚ƒ âˆ’ (Iâ‚ âˆ’ Iâ‚‚)Ï‰â‚Ï‰â‚‚ = Ï„â‚ƒ
<span class="lbl">Euler's equations of motion (principal axes, body frame)</span>
</div>
<p>Free rotation (<span class="m">Ï„ = 0</span>) ke liye RHS zero ho jaata hai â€” aur phir equations couple ho jaate hain (har <span class="m">Ï‰Ì‡</span> doosre components par depend karta hai). Isi coupling se "<span class="m">Ï‰</span> ka precession" aur "stability/instability" nikalta hai.</p>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” free rotation ke 3 cases (asymmetric top <span class="m">Iâ‚ &lt; Iâ‚‚ &lt; Iâ‚ƒ</span>)</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Sabse chhote <span class="m">I</span> ke around (long axis, jaise pencil ka tip se spin):</strong> stable âœ“ â€” chhoti disturbance oscillations deti hai, body spin karti rehti hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Sabse bade <span class="m">I</span> ke around (jaise flat plate ka normal):</strong> stable âœ“</li>
    <li><strong>Middle <span class="m">I</span> ke around:</strong> <strong>unstable</strong> âœ— â€” chhoti si disturbance exponentially badh jaati hai; body flip karne lagti hai. Isi ko <strong>intermediate axis theorem</strong> (ya <em>tennis-racket theorem</em>) kehte hain.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box fact">
  <p class="bt">Did you know? â€” Dzhanibekov effect</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">1985 mein Soviet cosmonaut Vladimir Dzhanibekov ne space station (microgravity, virtually zero torque) mein ek wing-nut ko spin kiya. Usne dekha: nut waqt-waqt par apni axis palat deta tha (flip), bina kisi torque ke! Ye intermediate axis theorem ka live demonstration tha. Isliye ise "Dzhanibekov effect" bhi kehte hain. Ek tennis racket ko hawa mein uchhal ke middle axis ke around spin karo â€” wo bhi palat jaayega âœ“ (aap ghar par test kar sakte hain).</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s5-8">5.8 Precessional motion of the spinning top</h3>

