<section id="ch6">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 6 Â· Supplementary</span>Motion Under a Central Force &amp; Kepler's Laws</h2>

<h3 id="s6-1">6.1 Central force kya hai?</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Central force</p>
  <p><strong>A central force is a force whose line of action always passes through a fixed point (the centre of force) and whose magnitude depends only on the distance <span class="m">r</span> of the particle from that centre:</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0"><span class="vec">F</span> = f(r) <em>rÌ‚</em> &nbsp;&nbsp;(ya <span class="m">f(r)</span> negative ho to attractive)</div>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Gravitation (<span class="m">F = âˆ’GMm/rÂ²</span>), Coulomb force (<span class="m">F = kqâ‚qâ‚‚/rÂ²</span>), spring force ek fixed centre se (<span class="m">F = âˆ’kr</span>) â€” sab central forces hain.</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” central force ki 3 golden properties (yaad kar lo, exam mein sabse zyada poochhi jaati hain)</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Torque zero:</strong> <span class="m"><span class="vec">Ï„</span> = <span class="vec">r</span> Ã— <span class="vec">F</span> = <span class="vec">r</span> Ã— f(r)<em>rÌ‚</em> = 0</span> (kyunki <span class="m"><span class="vec">r</span> âˆ¥ <em>rÌ‚</em></span>) âŸ¹ <strong>angular momentum <span class="m">L</span> conserved</strong>. Yahi Kepler's second law ka source hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Motion ek plane mein:</strong> kyunki <span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span></span> ki direction fix hai, aur <span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span> = <span class="vec">r</span> Ã— <span class="vec">p</span></span> hamesha <span class="m"><span class="vec">r</span></span> aur <span class="m"><span class="vec">p</span></span> dono ke perpendicular hai â€” to particle us plane mein hi rehta hai jo <span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span></span> ke perpendicular hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Conservative force:</strong> central force hamesha conservative hota hai (curl zero), isliye potential energy <span class="m">V(r)</span> exist karti hai aur total energy conserve hoti hai. Isliye hum energy diagram bana sakte hain (Chapter 3 yaad karo!).</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h4>Areal velocity (Kepler's 2nd law) â€” direct result</h4>
<p>Plane polar coordinates <span class="m">(r, Î¸)</span> mein chhota area element <span class="m">dA = Â½rÂ²dÎ¸</span> hota hai (triangle ka area). To:</p>
<div class="eq">
<span class="frac"><span>dA</span><span>dt</span></span> = Â½ rÂ²<span class="frac"><span>dÎ¸</span><span>dt</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>L</span><span>2m</span></span> = constant
<span class="lbl">Areal velocity constant hai âŸ¹ equal areas in equal times</span>
</div>
<p>Ye ek line ka proof hai â€” koi long derivation nahi chahiye, kyunki <span class="m">L = mrÂ²Î¸Ì‡</span> conserved hai.</p>

