<h3 id="s6-4">6.4 Orbit ka equation (Binet's formula) aur Kepler's 1st law</h3>
<p>Orbit ki <em>shape</em> nikalne ke liye ek standard trick use hoti hai: <span class="m">u = 1/r</span> ko variable banao. Central force ke liye <strong>Binet's formula</strong>:</p>
<div class="eq">
<span class="frac"><span>dÂ²u</span><span>dÎ¸Â²</span></span> + u = âˆ’<span class="frac"><span>F(1/u)</span><span>m hÂ² uÂ²</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; where <span class="m">h = L/m = rÂ²Î¸Ì‡</span> (specific angular momentum)
<span class="lbl">Binet's formula â€” orbit equation</span>
</div>
<p>Inverse-square attractive force <span class="m">F = âˆ’GMm/rÂ² = âˆ’GMm uÂ²</span> substitute karo:</p>
<ul class="deriv">
  <li><span class="m"><span class="frac"><span>dÂ²u</span><span>dÎ¸Â²</span></span> + u = <span class="frac"><span>GMm</span><span>m hÂ²</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>GM</span><span>hÂ²</span></span></span> (constant)</li>
  <li>Ye ek simple harmonic-type equation hai; uska solution: <span class="m">u = A cos(Î¸ âˆ’ Î¸â‚€) + <span class="frac"><span>GM</span><span>hÂ²</span></span></span></li>
  <li><span class="m">Î¸â‚€ = 0</span> choose karke (axis ki choice), <span class="m">r = 1/u</span> likho:
    <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">r = <span class="frac"><span>â„“</span><span>1 + e cos Î¸</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp; with &nbsp; â„“ = <span class="frac"><span>hÂ²</span><span>GM</span></span>, &nbsp; e = <span class="frac"><span>A hÂ²</span><span>GM</span></span></div>
    <span class="whyline">Ye <strong>conic section</strong> ka polar equation hai (semi-latus rectum â„“, eccentricity e) â€” bilkul Kepler's 1st law! âœ“</span></li>
</ul>

<div class="tw">
