<h3 id="s6-5">6.5 Kepler's three laws</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Kepler's laws (statements â€” exam ke liye)</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Law of orbits:</strong> Har planet Sun ke around ek <em>ellipse</em> mein ghoomta hai, jismein Sun ek focus par hota hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Law of areas:</strong> Planet ko Sun se jodne wali line equal intervals of time mein equal areas sweep karti hai (yani areal velocity constant).</li>
    <li><strong>Law of periods:</strong> <span class="m">TÂ² âˆ aÂ³</span> â€” orbital period ka square semi-major axis ke cube ke proportional hota hai.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h4>Third law ka derivation (ye exam mein aksar poochha jaata hai)</h4>
<ul class="deriv">
  <li>Ellipse ka area: <span class="m">A = Ï€ab</span> (a = semi-major, b = semi-minor axis)</li>
  <li>Areal velocity (2nd law se): <span class="m">dA/dt = L/(2m) = h/2</span></li>
  <li>Period = poora area Ã· areal velocity: <span class="m">T = <span class="frac"><span>Ï€ab</span><span>h/2</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>2Ï€ab</span><span>h</span></span></span></li>
  <li>Do geometric relations use karo: <span class="m">â„“ = bÂ²/a</span> aur (orbit equation se) <span class="m">â„“ = hÂ²/(GM)</span> âŸ¹ <span class="m">bÂ² = aâ„“ = ahÂ²/(GM)</span></li>
  <li>Ab <span class="m">TÂ²</span> nikalo:
    <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">TÂ² = <span class="frac"><span>4Ï€Â²aÂ²bÂ²</span><span>hÂ²</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>4Ï€Â²aÂ²</span><span>hÂ²</span></span> Â· <span class="frac"><span>a hÂ²</span><span>GM</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span>4Ï€Â²</span><span>GM</span></span> aÂ³</div>
    <span class="whyline">Bas! <span class="m">TÂ² = (4Ï€Â²/GM)aÂ³</span> â€” yani <span class="m">TÂ² âˆ aÂ³</span> âœ“ Kepler's 3rd law derived from Newton's gravity.</span></li>
  <li>Do bodies ki finite masses ke liye exact form: <span class="m">TÂ² = <span class="frac"><span>4Ï€Â²aÂ³</span><span>G(M + m)</span></span></span></li>
</ul>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Solved Example 1 â€” Kepler's third law ka real test (Mars)</p>
  <p>Mars ka orbital period <span class="m">T = 687 days = 1.881 yr</span> hai aur Sun se average distance <span class="m">a = 1.524 AU</span>. Third law check karo (Earth ke liye <span class="m">T = 1 yr</span>, <span class="m">a = 1 AU</span>).</p>
  <p><span class="m">aÂ³ = (1.524)Â³ = 3.54</span>; &nbsp; <span class="m">TÂ² = (1.881)Â² = 3.54</span> &nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp; <strong><span class="m">TÂ²/aÂ³ = 1.00</span></strong> (Earth ke same units mein) âœ“</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Yani Mars bilkul Kepler's third law follow karta hai (deviation &lt; 1% â€” Sun ke around other planets ki perturbations aur data precision ke level par). Isi law se hum <em>kisi bhi exoplanet ka semi-major axis</em> ya <em>period</em> nikal sakte hain!</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s6-6">6.6 Energy, escape velocity aur satellites</h3>
<p>Bound orbit ke liye total energy ka bahut elegant form hai (derive karne ke liye orbit equation + vis-viva use karo):</p>
<div class="eq">
E = âˆ’<span class="frac"><span>GMm</span><span>2a</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp; (bound orbit), &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; vÂ² = GM( <span class="frac"><span>2</span><span>r</span></span> âˆ’ <span class="frac"><span>1</span><span>a</span></span> )
<span class="lbl">Total energy aur vis-viva equation (v = orbital speed)</span>
</div>
<p><strong>Escape velocity:</strong> object ko gravity se hamesha ke liye nikalne ke liye <span class="m">E â‰¥ 0</span> chahiye; surface se launch karte hue:</p>
<div class="eq">
Â½mv<sub>esc</sub>Â² âˆ’ <span class="frac"><span>GMm</span><span>R</span></span> = 0 âŸ¹ v<sub>esc</sub> = <span class="sqrt"><span>2GM</span>/<span>R</span></span> = âˆš(2gR)
<span class="lbl">Escape velocity â€” Earth ke liye â‰ˆ 11.2 km/s (mass se independent!)</span>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Solved Example 2 â€” Geostationary orbit (CLO-level application)</p>
  <p>Geostationary satellite wo hai jo Earth ke saath hi ghoomta hai (period <span class="m">T = 1</span> sidereal day <span class="m">= 86164 s</span>). Uska orbital radius nikalo.</p>
  <p><span class="m">TÂ² = 4Ï€Â²rÂ³/(GM) âŸ¹ r = (GMTÂ²/4Ï€Â²)<sup>1/3</sup></span></p>
  <p><span class="m">GM = 3.986 Ã— 10Â¹â´ mÂ³/sÂ²</span>, <span class="m">TÂ² = (86164)Â² = 7.42 Ã— 10â¹ sÂ²</span></p>
  <p><span class="m">rÂ³ = (3.986Ã—10Â¹â´ Ã— 7.42Ã—10â¹)/(4Ï€Â²) = 7.49 Ã— 10Â²Â² mÂ³ âŸ¹ r â‰ˆ 4.22 Ã— 10â· m = 42,200 km</span></p>
  <p><strong>Altitude = r âˆ’ R<sub>E</sub> = 42,200 âˆ’ 6,371 â‰ˆ 35,800 km</strong> (yahi standard value hai).</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Bonus insight: geostationary orbit <strong>sirf equator ke upar</strong> possible hai â€” kyunki satellite ke orbit ka plane Earth ke centre se guzarta hai, aur "stationary" dikhne ke liye us plane mein Earth ki rotation axis bhi honi chahiye.</p>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Solved Example 3 â€” energy signs ka matlab</p>
  <p>Ek satellite <span class="m">a = 2R<sub>E</sub></span> par circular orbit mein hai (<span class="m">R<sub>E</sub> = 6.4Ã—10â¶ m</span>, <span class="m">g = 9.8 m/sÂ²</span>). Uska speed aur total energy (per unit mass) nikalo; aur minimum speed jo dene par wo escape kar jaaye.</p>
  <p>Circular orbit ke liye: <span class="m">v = âˆš(GM/r) = âˆš(gR<sub>E</sub>Â²/r) = âˆš(9.8 Ã— (6.4Ã—10â¶)Â²/(1.28Ã—10â·)) = âˆš(3.14Ã—10â·) â‰ˆ 5.6 km/s</span></p>
  <p>Energy per unit mass: <span class="m">Îµ = âˆ’GM/(2a) = âˆ’(9.8 Ã— 4.1Ã—10Â¹Â³)/(2 Ã— 1.28Ã—10â·) = âˆ’1.57Ã—10â· J/kg</span> (negative = bound âœ“)</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Escape ke liye: <span class="m">v<sub>esc</sub> = âˆš2 v<sub>circ</sub> = 1.414 Ã— 5.6 â‰ˆ 7.9 km/s</span> â€” dhyan do, <strong>escape speed = âˆš2 Ã— circular orbit speed</strong> usi radius par. Ye ek beautiful general result hai âœ“</p>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” common galtiyan (central force / Kepler)</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>"Aphelion par planet ki speed zero hoti hai?"</strong> Nahi â€” speed minimum hoti hai, zero nahi. Angular momentum ki wajah se wo kabhi rukta nahi.</li>
    <li><strong>"Escape velocity mass par depend karti hai?"</strong> Nahi â€” <span class="m">v<sub>esc</sub> = âˆš(2GM/R)</span> mein object ka mass hi nahi aata. Bhaari ya halki body, dono ko same escape speed chahiye (vacuity mein).</li>
    <li><strong>"Satellite ko upar rakhne ke liye centrifugal force gravity ko balance karta hai"</strong> â€” inertial frame se ye galat hai. Asli statement: gravity hi centripetal force provide karta hai (<span class="m">GMm/rÂ² = mvÂ²/r</span>). Centrifugal force rotating frame ki language hai (Chapter 2) â€” dono descriptions correct hain, par mix na karo.</li>
    <li><strong>"Sun ek focus par hai, matlab Earth Sun se sabse door tab hai jab..."</strong> â€” Dhyan: seasons <em>distance</em> se nahi, axial tilt se hote hain (northern summer mein Earth actually Sun se sabse door hoti hai â€” July aphelion!). Ye exam mein "conceptual trap" ban sakta hai.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="box quiz">
<p class="bt" style="color:#3a2f7a">Mini knowledge check 6</p>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q1. Central force ke liye angular momentum conserved kyun hota hai?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Kyunki torque <span class="m"><span class="vec">Ï„</span> = <span class="vec">r</span> Ã— <span class="vec">F</span> = 0</span> â€” force hamesha position vector ke along (<span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span> = f(r)<em>rÌ‚</em></span>) hota hai, to cross product zero. Aur <span class="m">d<span class="vec">L</span>/dt = <span class="vec">Ï„</span> = 0</span> âŸ¹ <span class="m"><span class="vec">L</span></span> constant. Iska direct consequence: motion plane mein rehta hai aur areal velocity constant hoti hai (Kepler's 2nd law).</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q2. Effective potential mein centrifugal term <span class="m">LÂ²/2mrÂ²</span> chhote r par bahut bada kyun ho jaata hai? Physical matlab?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Ye term angular momentum ki "fees" hai: chhote <span class="m">r</span> par particle ko tez ghoomna padta hai (<span class="m">v<sub>Î¸</sub> = L/mr</span>), to kinetic energy <span class="m">LÂ²/(2mrÂ²)</span> mehenga ho jaata hai. Ye ek <strong>repulsive barrier</strong> ki tarah kaam karta hai â€” particle centre tak pahunch hi nahi sakta (jab tak <span class="m">L â‰  0</span>). Isi wajah se planets Sun mein gir nahi jaate, aur electrons nuclei mein nahi girte (quantum version mein bhi yehi term hai).</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q3. Agar kisi comet ka <span class="m">e = 1.0</span> ho, to uska future kya hoga?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a"><span class="m">e = 1</span> ka matlab parabolic orbit aur <span class="m">E = 0</span> (exactly escape energy). Wo Sun ke paas aayega (perihelion par tez ghoomega) aur phir hamesha ke liye door chala jaayega â€” wapas nahi aayega. Practically <span class="m">e</span> itni exactly 1 nahi hoti; thoda sa &lt; 1 ho to ellipse (periodic comet, jaise Halley), &gt; 1 ho to hyperbola (ek hi visit).</p></div>
</details>
</div>

<div class="box recap">
  <p class="bt">Quick Recap â€” Chapter 6</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Central force <span class="m"><span class="vec">F</span> = f(r)<em>rÌ‚</em></span>: torque zero âŸ¹ <span class="m">L</span> conserved, motion planar, force conservative (energy conserved).</li>
    <li>Areal velocity <span class="m">dA/dt = L/2m = constant</span> (Kepler's 2nd law).</li>
    <li>Two-body â†’ one-body: <span class="m">Î¼<span class="vec">r</span>Ìˆ = <span class="vec">F</span>(r)<em>rÌ‚</em></span>, <span class="m">Î¼ = mâ‚mâ‚‚/(mâ‚+mâ‚‚)</span>.</li>
    <li>Effective potential <span class="m">V<sub>eff</sub> = V(r) + LÂ²/2mrÂ²</span>; minimum = circular orbit; <span class="m">E &lt; 0</span> bound, <span class="m">E â‰¥ 0</span> unbound.</li>
    <li>Binet's formula se orbit: <span class="m">r = â„“/(1 + e cosÎ¸)</span> â€” conic; Kepler's 1st law.</li>
    <li>Kepler's 3rd: <span class="m">TÂ² = 4Ï€Â²aÂ³/(G(M+m))</span>; energy <span class="m">E = âˆ’GMm/2a</span>; vis-viva <span class="m">vÂ² = GM(2/r âˆ’ 1/a)</span>.</li>
    <li>Escape speed <span class="m">v<sub>esc</sub> = âˆš(2GM/R) = âˆš2 v<sub>circ</sub></span>; Earth par 11.2 km/s; geostationary radius â‰ˆ 42,200 km.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box exam">
  <p class="bt">Exam corner â€” Chapter 6</p>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> (i) Central force define kijiye aur 3 properties likhiye. (ii) Areal velocity aur angular momentum ka relation likhiye. (iii) Escape velocity ka expression derive kijiye. (iv) Geostationary orbit sirf equator par kyun?</p>
  <p><strong>Long answer:</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Two-body central force problem ko one-body problem mein reduce kijiye (reduced mass ke saath).</li>
    <li>Effective potential ka concept samjhaiye aur bound/unbound orbits ko energy diagram se explain kijiye.</li>
    <li>Binet's formula ki sahayata se inverse-square force ke liye conic-section orbit derive kijiye.</li>
    <li>Kepler's teen laws likhiye aur teeno ko Newton's law of gravitation se derive kijiye.</li>
  </ol>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Numerical:</strong> (i) Mars ka period 687 days hai, uska <span class="m">a</span> nikaliye AU mein. (ii) 400 km altitude par circular orbit ka speed aur period nikaliye. (iii) <span class="m">a = 3R<sub>E</sub></span> wale satellite ki total energy per unit mass nikaliye aur bataiye wo bound hai ya nahi.</p>
</div>
