<section id="ch1">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 1 Â· Unit I</span>Periodicity, Effective Nuclear Charge &amp; Slater's Rules</h2>

<h3 id="s1-1">1.1 Periodicity ka concept â€” sabse pehle "kyun"</h3>
<p>Periodic table itni kaam ki kyun hai? Kyunki elements ke properties <strong>random nahi</strong> hote â€” wo unke <strong>electronic configuration</strong> se seedha nikalte hain, aur configuration har period mein dohraata hai.</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Periodic property</p>
  <p><strong>A periodic property is a property of an element which varies in a regular, repeating manner with increasing atomic number, because the outer electronic configuration of the elements repeats periodically.</strong></p>
</div>

<p>Do "families" ko yaad rakho:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Group (vertical column):</strong> same valence-shell configuration (e.g., sab alkali metals ka <span class="m">nsÂ¹</span>), isliye <strong>chemical properties similar</strong>, par size badhne se trend gradually badalta hai.</li>
  <li><strong>Period (horizontal row):</strong> same <span class="m">n</span>, electrons usi shell mein bharte hain (e.g., <span class="m">2s â†’ 2p</span>), isliye <strong>properties steadily badalti hain</strong> â€” metal â†’ metalloid â†’ non-metal.</li>
</ul>

<p>Aur poore periodic table ka "engine" ek hi idea hai: <strong>nucleus ka positive charge badhta hai, lekin electrons ek doosre ko shield karte hain</strong>. Net charge jo outermost electron feel karta hai, wahi saari periodic properties decide karta hai. Isi ko <em>effective nuclear charge</em> kehte hain.</p>

<h3 id="s1-2">1.2 Effective nuclear charge (Z<sub>eff</sub>) aur screening effect</h3>

<p>Ek simple sawal: Na atom (Z = 11) mein nucleus par +11 charge hai aur 11 electrons hain. To outermost 3s electron nucleus se poori <strong>11 units</strong> ki attraction feel karta hai?</p>
<p><strong>Nahi.</strong> Kyunki uske aur nucleus ke beech mein 10 electrons (1sÂ², 2sÂ², 2pâ¶) maujood hain, aur wo bhi negative hain. Ye beech ke electrons nucleus ki attraction ko "block" (screen) kar dete hain â€” jaise bheed mein aap teacher ko theek se nahi dekh paate.</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Effective nuclear charge &amp; screening</p>
  <p><strong>Effective nuclear charge (Z<sub>eff</sub>) is the net positive charge experienced by an electron in a multi-electron atom; it is less than the actual nuclear charge (Z) because the inner electrons shield (screen) the outer electron from the full nuclear attraction:</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    Z<sub>eff</sub> = Z âˆ’ S
    <span class="lbl">Z = actual nuclear charge (atomic number) Â· S = screening (shielding) constant</span>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Screening (shielding) effect:</strong> the reduction in the nuclear attraction felt by an electron due to the repulsive effect of other electrons, particularly those closer to the nucleus.</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” inner electrons zyada shield karte hain, outer kam</p>
  <p>Shielding ka achha measure <strong>penetration</strong> hai â€” matlab electron ka nucleus ke paas pair hone ka "chance".</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>s-orbital</strong> nucleus ke sabse paas pahunchta hai (radial probability finite hoti hai r = 0 par) â†’ sabse achha penetrator, par ye bhi doosron ko shield karta hai kam.</li>
    <li><strong>p</strong> usse kam, <strong>d</strong> usse bhi kam, <strong>f</strong> sabse kharaab penetrator.</li>
    <li>Isliye same shell mein: <span class="m">s &gt; p &gt; d &gt; f</span> (energy order bhi yahi hota hai).</li>
    <li>Aur screening power ka order ulta: <strong><span class="m">f &gt; d &gt; p &gt; s</span></strong> â€” yani f electrons (nucleus ke paas hone ke bawajood shape ki wajah se) doosre electrons ko theek se screen nahi karte. <strong>Yahi lanthanide contraction ka reason hai</strong> (Chapter 2).</li>
  </ul>
</div>

