</tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="s1-4">1.4 Slater's rules â€” complete method with worked examples</h3>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Slater's rules (J. C. Slater, 1930)</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Electrons ko groups mein likho (energy order mein): <strong>[1s] [2s,2p] [3s,3p] [3d] [4s,4p] [4d] [4f] [5s,5p] â€¦</strong> â€” dhyaan do, <span class="m">3d</span> aur <span class="m">4s</span> alag groups hain, aur <span class="m">s</span>â€“<span class="m">p</span> ek hi group banate hain.</li>
    <li>Jis electron ka Z<sub>eff</sub> chahiye, uske <strong>right</strong> (yani bahar) wale electrons S mein <strong>zero</strong> contribute karte hain.</li>
    <li><strong>Same group</strong> ke baaki electrons: har ek <strong>0.35</strong> (sirf 1s ke liye 0.30).</li>
    <li>Agar electron <strong><span class="m">ns</span> ya <span class="m">np</span></strong> hai: turant neeche wale shell (nâˆ’1) ke har electron ka <strong>0.85</strong>, aur usse andar ke saare electrons ka <strong>1.00</strong>.</li>
    <li>Agar electron <strong><span class="m">nd</span> ya <span class="m">nf</span></strong> hai: uske group ke left ke <em>saare</em> electrons ka <strong>1.00</strong> (same n ke s/p bhi included).</li>
    <li>Bottle mein dalo: <span class="m">Z<sub>eff</sub> = Z âˆ’ S</span>.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Example 1 â€” Sodium (Z = 11) ka 3s electron</p>
  <p>Config: <span class="m">1sÂ² 2sÂ² 2pâ¶ 3sÂ¹</span></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Same group (3sÂ¹): electron ke alava 0 â†’ <span class="m">0 Ã— 0.35 = 0</span></li>
    <li>nâˆ’1 shell (2sÂ²2pâ¶ = 8 electrons): <span class="m">8 Ã— 0.85 = 6.80</span></li>
    <li>Andar ke shells (1sÂ² = 2): <span class="m">2 Ã— 1.00 = 2.00</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="m">S = 0 + 6.80 + 2.00 = 8.80</span> â†’ <strong><span class="m">Z<sub>eff</sub> = 11 âˆ’ 8.80 = 2.20</span></strong></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Matlab: Na ka outermost electron sirf 2.2 units ki attraction feel karta hai â€” isliye Na ka ionization enthalpy sirf 496 kJ/mol hai aur wo aasaani se Naâº ban jaata hai.</p>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Example 2 â€” Potassium (Z = 19) ka 4s electron</p>
  <p>Config: <span class="m">1sÂ² 2sÂ² 2pâ¶ 3sÂ² 3pâ¶ 4sÂ¹</span></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Same group (4sÂ¹): 0</li>
    <li>nâˆ’1 shell (3sÂ²3pâ¶ = 8): <span class="m">8 Ã— 0.85 = 6.80</span></li>
    <li>Andar (1sÂ²2sÂ²2pâ¶ = 10): <span class="m">10 Ã— 1.00 = 10.00</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong><span class="m">S = 16.80 â†’ Z<sub>eff</sub> = 19 âˆ’ 16.80 = 2.20</span></strong></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Interesting! Na aur K dono ka outermost electron <span class="m">Z<sub>eff</sub> = 2.20</span> feel karta hai â€” kyunki group ke saath nuclear charge (Z) aur number of inner electrons dono badhte hain, aur lagbhag cancel ho jaate hain. Lekin K ka electron <span class="m">n = 4</span> par hai (nucleus se zyada door), isliye K ka IE kam hai (419 kJ/mol vs Na 496). <strong>Ye baat yaad rakho: Z<sub>eff</sub> same, par size badhne se attraction kam.</strong></p>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Example 3 â€” Chlorine (Z = 17) ka 3p electron aur Zn (Z = 30) ka 3d electron</p>
  <p><strong>Chlorine, 3p:</strong> same group (3sÂ²3pâµ = 7 electrons, khud ko chhod ke 6): <span class="m">6 Ã— 0.35 = 2.10</span>; nâˆ’1 (2sÂ²2pâ¶ = 8): <span class="m">8 Ã— 0.85 = 6.80</span>; andar (1sÂ²): <span class="m">2 Ã— 1.00 = 2.00</span>.<br>
  <span class="m">S = 10.90</span> â†’ <strong><span class="m">Z<sub>eff</sub> = 6.10</span></strong> (yahi value Allredâ€“Rochow scale mein chahiye hogi, Chapter 5).</p>
  <p><strong>Zinc, 3d:</strong> 3d electron ke liye rule alag hai â€” same group (3dÂ¹â°, khud ko chhod ke 9): <span class="m">9 Ã— 0.35 = 3.15</span>; aur <em>left ke saare</em> electrons (1sÂ²2sÂ²2pâ¶3sÂ²3pâ¶ = 18) ka <span class="m">18 Ã— 1.00 = 18.00</span>.<br>
  <span class="m">S = 21.15</span> â†’ <strong><span class="m">Z<sub>eff</sub> = 8.85</span></strong> (d electrons ko kaafi zyada attraction feel hoti hai â€” isliye d-block elements ke radii chhote hote hain.)</p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Shortcut mantrane: "0.35 apne group mein, 0.85 ek shell andar, 1.00 uske andar â€” aur d/f ke liye sab left wale 1.00."</p>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” Slater's rules mein 4 common galtiyan</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Jis electron ka Z<sub>eff</sub> nikal rahe ho, uske <strong>right wale electrons ko ginna hi nahi</strong> hai (unka contribution zero hai).</li>
    <li><span class="m">3d</span> aur <span class="m">4s</span> ko ek hi group nahi maanna â€” Slater ki list mein <span class="m">4s,4p</span> ka group <strong>3d ke baad</strong> aata hai (bhari atoms mein energy order yahi hai).</li>
    <li>nâˆ’1 ke electrons ke liye <strong>0.85</strong> use karo, 1.00 nahi. 1.00 sirf <em>nâˆ’2 aur usse andar</em> ke liye hai (s/p electrons ke case mein).</li>
    <li>Same group ke 0.35 mein <strong>khud ko include mat karo</strong> (e.g., 3sÂ²3pâµ ke liye 6, na ki 7).</li>
  </ol>
</div>

