<html><head><meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><section id="ch2">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 2 Â· Unit I</span>Atomic, Covalent, Ionic &amp; Crystal Radii</h2>

<p class="lead">"Atom ka size kitna hai?" â€” ye simple sawal hai, par jawab tricky hai. Kyunki electron cloud ki koi fixed boundary nahi hoti. Isliye hum radius ko <strong>measurement ke method ke hisaab se</strong> define karte hain â€” aur isi wajah se ek hi element ke ek se zyada "radius" hote hain. Ye chapter poora isi confusion ko clear karta hai, kyunki syllabus mein chaaron (atomic/vdW, covalent octahedral-tetrahedral, ionic, crystal) maange gaye hain.</p>

<h3 id="s2-1">2.1 Atomic radii â€” concept aur van der Waals radius</h3>

<p>Do atoms ko jab tak bond na ho, unki distance measure karna mushkil hai. Lekin <strong>solid state</strong> mein atoms ki positions fix ho jaati hain (X-ray diffraction se measure hoti hain). Us distance ko hum "radius" ke roop mein use karte hain â€” par kis situation ki distance, ye decide karta hai ki kaunsa radius milega.</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” van der Waals radius</p>
  <p><strong>The van der Waals radius of an atom is one-half of the distance between the nuclei of two identical, non-bonded atoms of the same element which are in their closest possible approach in the solid (or a molecular crystal).</strong></p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Yani wo radius jo atom apne <em>non-bonded</em> padosi se minimum doori par rakhta hai â€” bilkul jaise do log ek bheed mein ek doosre se "touch" kar ke khade hote hain, bina haath milaye.</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” vdW radius sabse bada hota hai</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Kyunki ye <strong>non-bonded</strong> contact hai â€” do electron clouds zyada se zyada paas aa sakte hain bas. Bonded cases mein nuclei zyada paas aa jaate hain (bond banana hi overlap hai). Isliye: <strong><span class="m">r<sub>vdW</sub> &gt; r<sub>metallic</sub> &gt; r<sub>covalent</sub></span></strong>. Rough rule: <span class="m">r<sub>vdW</sub> â‰ˆ 2 Ã— r<sub>covalent</sub></span> (approximate).</p>
</div>

