</table>
</div>

<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Pauling's covalent radii â€” octahedral (CN = 6) set</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Element</th><th class="num">S</th><th class="num">Se</th><th class="num">Te</th><th class="num">Cl</th><th class="num">Br</th><th class="num">I</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>Octahedral covalent radius (Ã…)</td><td class="num">1.04</td><td class="num">1.17</td><td class="num">1.37</td><td class="num">0.99</td><td class="num">1.14</td><td class="num">1.33</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why do sets â€” octahedral vs tetrahedral</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Pauling ne crystal structures ke interatomic distances se radii nikale. Jab kisi element ke compounds mein coordination <strong>4 (tetrahedral)</strong> hoti hai (jaise CHâ‚„, SiClâ‚„, diamond), tab ek set kaam aata hai; jab <strong>6 (octahedral)</strong> hoti hai (jaise S in sulfates, halogens in many ionic/covalent crystals), tab doosra set.</li>
    <li>Generally <strong>CN badhne par radius thoda bada</strong> ho jaata hai (zyada padosi atoms electron cloud ko faila dete hain).</li>
    <li>Exam mein: agar question mein coordination batayi ho, to <strong>wahi set</strong> use karo. Agar na batayi ho, to standard single-bond covalent radius (H 0.37, C 0.77, Cl 0.99, Br 1.14, I 1.33 Ã…) use karo â€” ye wahi classic values hain jo maximum textbooks mein hain.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” "radius" ka confusion</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Ek hi element ke vdW, covalent, ionic (aur metallic) radii <strong>alag-alag hote hain</strong>. Isliye numerical question mein pehle check karo: (i) kaunsa radius maanga hai, (ii) kaunsi scale (Pauling/Shannon/Goldschmidt), (iii) kaunsa coordination number. Bina ye check kiye numbers add karna sabse common galti hai.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s2-3">2.3 Ionic radii â€” problem kya hai?</h3>

<p>Ionic crystal mein cation aur anion touch karte hain. X-ray se humein <strong>sum</strong> milta hai:</p>
<div class="eq">r<sub>cation</sub> + r<sub>anion</sub> = d (internuclear distance)</div>
<p>Lekin <strong>ek equation, do unknowns</strong> â€” isliye radius ko do hisson mein baantne ke liye ek <em>reference</em> chahiye. Wahi se different "scales" paida hoti hain:</p>

<div class="tw">
