<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 3 Â· Unit I</span>Ionization Enthalpy</h2>

<h3 id="s3-1">3.1 Definition aur basic idea</h3>

<p>Atom se electron nikalna â€” ismein energy <strong>deni padti</strong> hai (kyunki nucleus electron ko pakde hue hai). Kitni energy? Ye depend karta hai ki electron kaise "bandha" hua hai.</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Ionization enthalpy</p>
  <p><strong>Ionization enthalpy (I.E.) of an element is the enthalpy change accompanying the removal of one electron from an isolated gaseous atom (or ion) in its ground state to form a positively charged ion, at absolute zero:</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    M(g) âŸ¶ Mâº(g) + eâ» &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Î”<sub>i</sub>HÂ° = +ve (endothermic)
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>First ionization enthalpy:</strong> <span class="m">M(g) â†’ Mâº(g) + eâ»</span>. Unit: <strong>kJ molâ»Â¹</strong> (purane books mein eV atomâ»Â¹ bhi milta hai: 1 eV = 96.5 kJ molâ»Â¹).</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” "enthalpy" aur "energy" mein chhota sa farq</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Purane naam "ionization energy/potential" the; aaj IUPAC "enthalpy" recommend karta hai kyunki hum constant pressure par kaam karte hain, aur <span class="m">Î”H = Î”E + PÎ”V</span>. Values mein farq bahut chhota hai (â‰ˆ <span class="m">RT</span> ke order ka), isliye exam mein dono terms use ho sakti hain â€” par definition mein "enthalpy change" likhna better hai.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s3-2">3.2 Factors affecting ionization energy (yaad rakho â€” chaar factors)</h3>
<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Ionization energy ko kaun-kaun affect karta hai</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Asar</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>1. Atomic size</strong></td><td>Size â†‘ âŸ¹ IE â†“</td><td>Outermost electron nucleus se door hota hai, attraction kam (<span class="m">F âˆ 1/rÂ²</span>).</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>2. Nuclear charge (Z<sub>eff</sub>)</strong></td><td>Z<sub>eff</sub> â†‘ âŸ¹ IE â†‘</td><td>Zyada net nuclear attraction, electron tightly held (period mein IE badhta hai).</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>3. Screening / shielding</strong></td><td>Shielding â†‘ âŸ¹ IE â†“</td><td>Inner electrons nucleus ka pull kam kar dete hain; s &gt; p &gt; d &gt; f penetration bhi matter karti hai.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>4. Electronic configuration</strong></td><td>Half-filled aur fully-filled shells âŸ¹ IE â†‘</td><td>Extra stability (exchange energy, symmetry) se electron nikalna mushkil (Be, N, Ne).</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p class="small">Bonus factors (conceptual questions mein kaam aate hain): <strong>charge on ion</strong> (cation ka IE &gt;&gt; neutral atom ka), aur <strong>relativistic effects</strong> bahut bhaari elements mein.</p>

