<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” trends ka logic</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Period mein left â†’ right:</strong> EGE <strong>zyada favourable</strong> hota jaata hai (Cl sabse achhe), kyunki Z<sub>eff</sub> badhta hai â†’ naya electron zyada strongly pakda jaata hai. Lekin noble gas tak pahunchte hi sign palat jaata hai (filled shell).</li>
    <li><strong>Group mein top â†’ bottom (halogens):</strong> <span class="m">F (âˆ’328) &lt; Cl (âˆ’349)</span> phir Br (âˆ’325), I (âˆ’295) â€” matlab first decrease nahi, F par thoda <em>kam</em> favourable hai (anomaly!), uske baad normal trend (size badhta hai â†’ EGE kam favourable).</li>
    <li><strong>Chalcogens:</strong> O (âˆ’141) &lt; S (âˆ’200) â€” same reason (O chhota hai, electron-electron repulsion zyada).</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” F ka EGE Cl se kam kyun? (Famous anomaly, must-know)</p>
  <p>Do reasons, dono likhna:</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> F atom bahut chhota hai (covalent radius 0.72 Ã… vs Cl 0.99 Ã…). Naya electron 2p shell mein aata hai jo already compact hai â€” to <strong>electron-electron repulsion</strong> zyada lagti hai, jo released energy ko kam kar deti hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Resonance/hydration-type secondary effects bhi</strong> isi taraf point karte hain; aur F ke ionization enthalpy/electronegativity sabse zyada hone ke baawajood EGE Cl se kam rehta hai. Isliye "F sabse strong oxidising agent" (jo thermodynamic + Fâ‚‚ ki weak Fâ€“F bond se aata hai) aur "Cl ka EGE sabse favourable" â€” dono statements alag-alag sach hain.</li>
  </ol>
