<section id="ch5">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 5 Â· Unit I</span>Electronegativity â€” Pauling, Mulliken, Allredâ€“Rochow, Mullikenâ€“JaffÃ©, Sanderson</h2>

<p class="lead">Ionization enthalpy = "electron dene ki aasani". Electron gain enthalpy = "electron lene ka fayda". Elektronegativity = <strong>"bond ke andar electron ko apni taraf kheenchne ki ability"</strong>. Aur yahin syllabus ka sabse "scales-heavy" topic hai â€” isliye har scale ka method + formula + worked example + limitation, sab denge.</p>

<h3 id="s5-1">5.1 Concept â€” electronegativity kya hai (aur kya naheen)</h3>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Electronegativity</p>
  <p><strong>Electronegativity is the tendency (relative power) of an atom in a molecule to attract the shared electron pair of a covalent bond towards itself.</strong></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Dhyaan:</strong> ye <em>bonded atom</em> ki property hai (isolated atom ki nahi) â€” isliye ye ek <em>relative, dimensionless</em> quantity hai aur <strong>direcly measure nahi ki ja sakti</strong> (isliye itne saare scales hain!).</p>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” electronegativity vs electron gain enthalpy</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Donon "electron attraction" jaisi lagti hain, par <strong>EGE isolated gaseous atom ki measurably measurable enthalpy hai; electronegativity ek bonded atom ki relative tendency hai</strong> (koi unit nahi). Isliye F ka EGE Cl se kam hai, par electronegativity F ki sabse zyada (3.98) hai â€” ye contradiction nahi, do bilkul alag quantities hain.</p>
</div>

<div class="chips">
  <span class="chip">Pauling (1932) â€” thermochemical, sabse common</span>
  <span class="chip">Mulliken (1934) â€” IE + EA se</span>
  <span class="chip">Allredâ€“Rochow (1958) â€” electrostatic, Z<sub>eff</sub>/rÂ²</span>
  <span class="chip">Mullikenâ€“JaffÃ© â€” valence state (hybridization)</span>
  <span class="chip">Sanderson (1955) â€” electron density ratio</span>
</div>

<h3 id="s5-2">5.2 Pauling's scale (thermochemical)</h3>
<p>Pauling ka idea: agar Aâ€“B bond <strong>purely covalent</strong> hota, to uski bond energy, Aâ€“A aur Bâ€“B ki bond energies ka geometric mean hoti. Par asli bond usse <strong>zyada strong</strong> hoti hai, kyunki usme kuch "ionic character" bhi aa jaata hai. Ye extra energy <span class="m">Î”</span> electronegativity difference ka measure hai:</p>

<div class="eq">
Î” = D(Aâ€“B) âˆ’ <span class="sqrt">D(Aâ€“A) Ã— D(Bâ€“B)</span> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(kcal molâ»Â¹ mein)<br>
|X<sub>A</sub> âˆ’ X<sub>B</sub>| = 0.208 <span class="sqrt">Î”</span> &nbsp;&nbsp;(Î” in kcal/mol) &nbsp;&nbsp;âŸº&nbsp;&nbsp; = 0.102 <span class="sqrt">Î”</span> &nbsp;(Î” in kJ/mol)
<span class="lbl">Pauling's equation â€” correlation factor 0.208 (kcal) ya 0.102 (kJ) commonly used</span>
</div>
<p>Reference (arbitrary scale): <strong>Hydrogen ka X = 2.1</strong> (Pauling ne H par fix kiya).</p>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example â€” H aur Cl ki electronegativity (classic)</p>
  <p><strong>Data:</strong> D(Hâ€“H) = 436 kJ/mol, D(Clâ€“Cl) = 243 kJ/mol, D(Hâ€“Cl) = 431 kJ/mol; H ka X = 2.20 lijiye.</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>Geometric mean: <span class="m">âˆš(436 Ã— 243) = âˆš105,948 = 325.5 kJ/mol</span></li>
    <li>Extra energy: <span class="m">Î” = 431 âˆ’ 325.5 = 105.5 kJ/mol</span>
      <span class="whyline">Note: kai books 91.7 kJ/mol likhti hain â€” wo tab aata hai jab "bond dissociation energy" ki jagah slightly different data use ho. Isliye apni data clearly likhna.</span></li>
    <li>Formula: <span class="m">Î”X = 0.102âˆš105.5 = 0.102 Ã— 10.27 = 1.05</span></li>
    <li>To <span class="m">X<sub>Cl</sub> = 2.20 + 1.05 = <strong>3.25</strong></span> (actual Pauling value 3.16 â€” method approximate hai, isliye ye level ka farq normal hai)</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Standard Pauling values: H 2.20, C 2.55, N 3.04, O 3.44, F 3.98, Na 0.93, Cl 3.16, Br 2.96, I 2.66, K 0.82.</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” Pauling's scale ki limitations (exam mein poochhi jaati hain)</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Sirf <strong>approximate</strong> hai â€” bond energies ka data different sources se different hota hai (jaise upar dekhia).</li>
    <li>D(Aâ€“B) nikalne ke liye Aâ€“A aur Bâ€“B bonds chalne chahiye â€” kai pairs ke liye data hi nahi hota.</li>
    <li>Value <strong>bond ke environment/hybridization</strong> se badal jaati hai (jaise carbon sp vs spÂ³ alag), jabki ek "fixed" number deta hai â€” isi gap ko Mullikenâ€“JaffÃ© ne bharaa.</li>
    <li>Sign convention bhi arbitrary hai (H = 2.1 par fix).</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h3 id="s5-3">5.3 Mulliken's scale (IE aur EA se)</h3>
<p>Mulliken ne kaha: agar koi atom electron <strong>kheenchta</strong> hai, to uska measure ye hona chahiye ki wo (i) apna electron kitna mushkil se deta hai (IE) aur (ii) naya electron kitna aasaani se leta hai (EA). Dono ka average:</p>
<div class="eq">
Ï‡<sub>M</sub> = <span class="frac"><span>IE + EA</span><span>2</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp;(eV mein) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; aur &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ï‡<sub>Pauling</sub> â‰ˆ <span class="frac"><span>Ï‡<sub>M</sub></span><span>2.8</span></span>
<span class="lbl">Mulliken electronegativity aur Pauling scale mein conversion (factor â‰ˆ 2.8, kai books 3.0 bhi likhti hain)</span>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example â€” F aur Cl ke liye Mulliken scale</p>
  <p><strong>Fluorine:</strong> IE = 17.42 eV, EA = 3.40 eV.<br>
  <span class="m">Ï‡<sub>M</sub> = (17.42 + 3.40)/2 = 10.41 eV</span>; &nbsp;<span class="m">Ï‡<sub>Pa</sub> â‰ˆ 10.41/2.8 = 3.72</span> &nbsp;(actual Pauling F = 3.98 â€” close âœ“)</p>
  <p><strong>Chlorine:</strong> IE = 12.97 eV, EA = 3.61 eV.<br>
  <span class="m">Ï‡<sub>M</sub> = 16.58/2 = 8.29 eV</span>; &nbsp;<span class="m">Ï‡<sub>Pa</sub> â‰ˆ 8.29/2.8 = 2.96</span> &nbsp;(actual 3.16 â€” roughly ok âœ“)</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Observation:</strong> dono scales trend mein match karte hain (F &gt; Cl) par exact numbers thode different hain â€” kyunki har scale ek different physical model use karti hai. Exam mein trend + reason likhna zyada important hai.</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” Mulliken scale ka fayda aur kamzori</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Fayda:</strong> ye kisi bond data ki zaroorat nahi, sirf atom ke data (IE, EA) se mil jaata hai â€” isliye noble gases ke liye bhi value nikal sakti hai. <strong>Kamzori:</strong> EA ki values reliable nahi hoti (aur noble gases ki EA to nahi hoti), aur ye atom ka <em>valence state</em> ignore karta hai â€” jo Mullikenâ€“JaffÃ© ne sudhara.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s5-4">5.4 Allredâ€“Rochow's scale (electrostatic)</h3>
<p>Allred aur Rochow ne sabse simple electrostatic model diya: electron kheenchne ki taqat = nucleus ka <strong>Z<sub>eff</sub></strong> divided by covalent radius ka square (Coulomb's law jaise, <span class="m">F âˆ Z<sub>eff</sub>/rÂ²</span>):</p>
<div class="eq">
Ï‡<sub>AR</sub> = 0.359 <span class="frac"><span>Z<sub>eff</sub></span><span>rÂ²</span></span> + 0.744 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(r in Ã…) &nbsp;&nbsp;âŸº&nbsp;&nbsp; Ï‡<sub>AR</sub> = 3590 <span class="frac"><span>Z<sub>eff</sub></span><span>rÂ²</span></span> + 0.744 &nbsp;&nbsp;(r in pm)
<span class="lbl">Allredâ€“Rochow electronegativity â€” Z<sub>eff</sub> Slater's rules se nikaalte hain</span>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example â€” Chlorine ka Allredâ€“Rochow electronegativity</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Slater's rules se Z<sub>eff</sub> (3p electron, Cl Z = 17) = <strong>6.10</strong> (Chapter 1, Example 3).<br>
  <strong>Step 2:</strong> Covalent radius r = 0.99 Ã….<br>
  <strong>Step 3:</strong> <span class="m">Ï‡<sub>AR</sub> = 0.359 Ã— (6.10/0.99Â²) + 0.744 = 0.359 Ã— 6.223 + 0.744 = 2.234 + 0.744 = <strong>2.98</strong></span></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Pauling value 3.16 â€” achha match âœ“. Isliye ye scale bhi Pauling ke saath correlation dikhata hai, par sirf <strong>atomic data</strong> se calculate ho jaata hai.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s5-5">5.5 Mullikenâ€“JaffÃ© scale â€” valence state ka concept</h3>
<p>Mullikenâ€“JaffÃ© (Hinzeâ€“JaffÃ©) ne observe kiya ki ek hi element ki electronegativity <strong>hybridization aur charge</strong> ke saath badalti hai. Isliye unhone IE aur EA ki jagah <strong>valence-state ionization potential (IP<sub>v</sub>)</strong> aur <strong>valence-state electron affinity (EA<sub>v</sub>)</strong> use kiye, aur ek weighted equation di:</p>
<div class="eq">
Ï‡<sub>MJ</sub> = a Â· IP<sub>v</sub> + b Â· EA<sub>v</sub> + c
<span class="lbl">a, b, c coefficients valence state par depend karte hain (hybridization + charge) â€” isliye "same element, different EN" possible ho jaata hai</span>
</div>
<p>Iska seedha fayda: carbon ka electronegativity <span class="m">CHâ‚„</span>, <span class="m">Câ‚‚Hâ‚„</span> aur <span class="m">HCâ‰¡CH</span> mein different nikalta hai â€” jo s-character se seedha juda hai. Yahi syllabus ka point hai: <strong>"variation of electronegativity with bond order / hybridization"</strong>.</p>

<h3 id="s5-6">5.6 Hybridization, s-character aur bond order ka asar</h3>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” sp carbon sabse electronegative carbon hai</p>
  <p>Kyunki <strong>s-orbital nucleus ke bahut paas hota hai</strong> (achha penetrator â€” Chapter 1). Jismein s ka hissa zyada, wo electrons nucleus ke zyada paas honge, matlab us atom ki electron kheenchne ki ability zyada:</p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">sp (50 % s) &gt; spÂ² (33 % s) &gt; spÂ³ (25 % s)</div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Isliye Câ€“H bond ki acidity bhi wahi order follow karti hai: HCâ‰¡CH (pK<sub>a</sub> â‰ˆ 25) &gt; Hâ‚‚C=CHâ‚‚ (â‰ˆ 44) &gt; CHâ‚„ (â‰ˆ 50) â€” kyunki sp carbon apna electron zyada kheenchta hai, to H usse door chala jaata hai aur aasaani se Hâº ban jaata hai. (Ye organic chemistry ka bhi favourite question hai.)</p>
</div>

