<p class="small">Same trend other elements ke liye bhi hai â€” e.g., nitrogen sp &gt; spÂ² &gt; spÂ³. Yahi wajah hai ki nitrile aur alkyne acidic hote hain aur amines basic.</p>

<h3 id="s5-7">5.7 Group electronegativity</h3>
<p>Molecule mein kabhi ek <strong>group</strong> ko atom ki tarah treat karna hota hai (jaise â€“CHâ‚ƒ, â€“CFâ‚ƒ, â€“OH, â€“NOâ‚‚). Us group ki net electron-kheenchne ki ability ko <strong>group electronegativity</strong> kehte hain.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Approximate "commonly quoted" values: <strong>CHâ‚ƒ â‰ˆ 2.3</strong>, <strong>CFâ‚ƒ â‰ˆ 3.3</strong>, CClâ‚ƒ â‰ˆ 2.6, NHâ‚‚ â‰ˆ 3.35, OH â‰ˆ 3.5, NOâ‚‚ â‰ˆ 3.4 (values method ke saath thodi badalti hain â€” isliye trend par focus karo: <strong>CHâ‚ƒ &lt; CClâ‚ƒ &lt; CFâ‚ƒ</strong>).</li>
  <li><strong>Why CFâ‚ƒ 3.3 tak pahunch jaata hai?</strong> Teen F atoms highly electronegative hain, aur wo carbon se electrons kheench kar usko "electron-poor" bana dete hain (inductive effect). Isliye CFâ‚ƒ group ka net EN carbon (2.55) se bahut zyada ho jaata hai.</li>
  <li><strong>Practical proof:</strong> CFâ‚ƒCOOH (pK<sub>a</sub> â‰ˆ 0.2) acetic acid (â‰ˆ4.8) se lakhon guna strong acid hai â€” kyunki CFâ‚ƒ group electron density kheenchke Oâ€“H bond ko todhna aasan bana deta hai. Yahi connection group electronegativity aur acidity ka hai.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="s5-8">5.8 Partial charge (% ionic character) â€” bond polarity ka measurement</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Partial charge &amp; dipole moment se % ionic character</p>
  <p><strong>Partial charge (Î´) is the fractional charge developed on atoms of a polar covalent bond; it is estimated from the observed dipole moment of the bond compared to the dipole moment the bond would have if the electron were completely transferred (100 % ionic).</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    % ionic character = <span class="frac"><span>Observed dipole moment (Î¼<sub>obs</sub>)</span><span>Calculated 100 % ionic dipole moment (Î¼<sub>ionic</sub> = e Ã— d)</span></span> Ã— 100
    <span class="lbl">e = 4.8 Ã— 10â»Â¹â° esu Â· d = bond length (cm) â†’ Î¼ in Debye units</span>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example â€” HCl ka % ionic character aur partial charge</p>
  <p>Data: <span class="m">Î¼<sub>obs</sub>(HCl) = 1.08 D</span>, bond length <span class="m">d = 1.27 Ã… = 1.27 Ã— 10â»â¸ cm</span>.</p>
