    <li>100 % ionic (ek full electron transfer, HâºClâ») ka dipole: <span class="m">Î¼<sub>ionic</sub> = e Ã— d = (4.8 Ã— 10â»Â¹â°)(1.27 Ã— 10â»â¸) = 6.10 Ã— 10â»Â¹â¸ esuÂ·cm = 6.10 D</span></li>
    <li>% ionic character = <span class="m">(1.08/6.10) Ã— 100 = <strong>17.7 %</strong></span></li>
    <li>Partial charge: <span class="m">Î´ = 0.177 e â‰ˆ Â±0.18 e</span> âŸ¹ likho H<sup>Î´+</sup>â€”Cl<sup>Î´âˆ’</sup></li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Isi method se HF ka <span class="m">Î¼ = 1.91 D</span>, d = 0.917 Ã… â†’ Î¼<sub>ionic</sub> = 4.40 D â†’ <strong>43 %</strong> ionic character. Matlab HF HCl se kaafi zyada polar hai (EN difference bhi zyada: 1.78 vs 0.98) âœ“ â€” ye dikhata hai ki % ionic character EN difference ke saath kaise correlate karta hai.</p>
</div>

<p><strong>Useful empirical result (Hannayâ€“Smith):</strong> <span class="m">% ionic character = 16(Î”X) + 3.5(Î”X)Â²</span> â€” jahan <span class="m">Î”X</span> electronegativity difference hai. Check: HCl ke liye <span class="m">Î”X = 0.98 â†’ 16(0.98) + 3.5(0.96) = 15.7 + 3.4 = 19 %</span> â‰ˆ 17.7 % (direct calculation) â€” kaafi close âœ“</p>

<h3 id="s5-9">5.9 Sanderson's electron density ratio (stability ratio)</h3>
<p>Sanderson ka idea ekdum different angle se aaya: electronegativity = atom ke andar <strong>electron density kitni "compressed" hai</strong>.</p>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Sanderson's method â€” formulae</p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    Average electron density: &nbsp; D = <span class="frac"><span>3Z</span><span>4Ï€rÂ³</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp;(Z = atomic number, r = radius)
  </div>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    Stability ratio: &nbsp; SR = <span class="frac"><span>D<sub>atom</sub></span><span>D<sub>i</sub></span></span>
    <span class="lbl">D<sub>i</sub> = usi number ke electrons wale "hypothetical inert (noble-gas-like) atom" ki interpolated electron density</span>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">SR = 1 hone ka matlab atom "ideal/inert" jaisa stable hai. SR &gt; 1 â†’ electron density zyada (atom electron kheenchne mein active). Isi SR ko ek scaling ke through Pauling-comparable electronegativity value mein convert kiya jaata hai, aur molecule mein <strong>electronegativity equalization</strong> ka use karke partial charges nikale jaate hain.</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” Sanderson ka electron density approach useful kyun hai?</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Ye <strong>size + charge dono</strong> ko ek hi expression (<span class="m">Z/rÂ³</span>) mein pakadta hai â€” isliye periodic trends ismein saaf dikhte hain (chhota aur bhaari atom = high density = high EN).</li>
    <li><strong>Electronegativity equalization</strong> ka concept bhi isi se aata hai: jab do atoms bond karte hain, unki electronegativities ek "average" par pahunchti hain, aur us difference se partial charges (Î´âº/Î´â») estimate hote hain. Isliye Sanderson ka method <strong>partial charge</strong> se seedha juda hai (syllabus mein dono ek hi line mein hain).</li>
    <li>Values absolute nahi, relative hote hain â€” isliye exam mein <em>method</em> likhna important hai, number ko ratta nahi maarna.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

