<h3 id="s6-4">6.4 Diagonal relationship â€” concept, examples, aur "why"</h3>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Diagonal relationship</p>
  <p><strong>Diagonal relationship is the similarity in chemical properties shown by certain elements placed diagonally to each other in the periodic table â€” i.e., between an element of a group and the element one period below and one group to the right (a "diagonal bridge" relationship).</strong></p>
</div>

<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Diagonal pairs aur unki similarities (yaad rakhne wali table)</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Main similarities (examples)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td><strong>Li (Gp 1) â†” Mg (Gp 2)</strong></td>
  <td>Dono paani se dheere react karte hain (Na se kam); dono ke <strong>nitrides</strong> direct bante hain (Liâ‚ƒN, Mgâ‚ƒNâ‚‚); hydroxides weak bases; carbonates heat par decompose (Liâ‚‚COâ‚ƒ â†’ Liâ‚‚O + COâ‚‚); dono organic solvents mein covalent character dikhate hain; dono ke oxides dono acidic+basic (amphoteric-ish) nature ke.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><strong>Be (Gp 2) â†” Al (Gp 13)</strong></td>
  <td>Dono ke oxides/hydroxides <strong>amphoteric</strong>; dono strong Lewis acids aur complex ions banate hain; chlorides covalent (electron-deficient, bridge structure); both react with NaOH dene ke liye beryllate/aluminate; carbide-like behaviour; both show +2 oxidation state.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><strong>B (Gp 13) â†” Si (Gp 14)</strong></td>
  <td>Dono <strong>metalloids</strong>, dono ke oxides acidic (Bâ‚‚Oâ‚ƒ acidic-ish, SiOâ‚‚ weakly acidic); dono <strong>volatile hydrides</strong> banate hain (diborane, silanes); both form glass-type network structures; both oxidise par high temperature require karte hain.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” diagonal relationship hoti kyun hai? (Do reasons â€” exam mein dono likho)</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Size aur charge density ka compensation:</strong> Diagonal par jaane se do effects ulta kaam karte hain: group mein neeche jaane par size badhta hai (+), aur period mein right jaane par Z<sub>eff</sub> badhkar size ghatta hai (âˆ’). <strong>Net result: diagonal elements ka ionic/atomic size aur charge density lagbhag same nikal aata hai</strong> â€” isliye unki polarising power (aur hence chemistry) milti-julti hoti hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Similar electronegativity / polarising power:</strong> Diagonal pairs ki EN values close hoti hain (Li 0.98 vs Mg 1.31 â€” dono low; Be 1.57 vs Al 1.61 â€” lagbhag same!). Yani "electron kheenchne ki taqat" same â†’ compounds ki polarising power, covalent character, hydrolysis behaviour same.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h3 id="s6-5">6.5 Catenation</h3>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Catenation</p>
  <p><strong>Catenation is the ability of an element to form chains (and rings) of its own atoms by forming bonds with itself â€” i.e. self-linking.</strong></p>
</div>

<p>Carbon is the champion: wo millions of compounds banata hai (diamond, graphite, fullerenes, millions of organic molecules). Kyun? Teen reasons:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Câ€“C bond enthalpy high hai (347 kJ/mol)</strong> â€” strong single bonds, isliye lambi chains stable rah sakti hain.</li>
  <li><strong>C ka size chhota hai</strong> â€” isse uske orbitals theek se overlap karte hain aur chain ke saath-saath <strong>double/triple bonds (pÏ€â€“pÏ€)</strong> bhi ban sakte hain (C=C, Câ‰¡C).</li>
  <li><strong>Catentaton ke saath tetra-valency (4) aur catenation ki versatility</strong> â€” C apne aap se aur H, O, N, halogens, S etc. se bonds bana kar vast variety deta hai.</li>
</ol>

