<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Bonding in diborane â€” 3câ€“2e (banana) bonds</p>
  <p><strong>In diborane, the four terminal Bâ€“H bonds are ordinary two-centre two-electron (2câ€“2e) bonds, while each bridging hydrogen is held by a three-centre two-electron (3câ€“2e) bond involving the two boron atoms and the bridging hydrogen. Because only two electrons are shared over three atoms, these are called "banana" or "electron-deficient" bonds.</strong></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Count check: valence electrons = 2(3) + 6(1) = 12. Terminal bonds use 8 eâ»; bache hue 4 eâ» do bridge bonds mein â€” <span class="m">2 eâ» per bridge</span>. Isliye boron ka octet complete nahi hota (ye "electron deficiency" hai).</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s8-3">8.3 Diborane â€” preparation, properties aur reactions (exam ka favourite section)</h3>

