<h3 id="s9-3">9.3 Fluorocarbons</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Fluorocarbons</p>
  <p><strong>Fluorocarbons are compounds (usually derivatives of hydrocarbons) in which all or most hydrogen atoms are replaced by fluorine â€” i.e. they contain strong Câ€“F bonds, often along with Câ€“C bonds.</strong></p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Example: tetrafluoroethene (CFâ‚‚=CFâ‚‚) ka polymer â†’ polytetrafluoroethene (PTFE, "Teflon"), aur chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs, jaise CClâ‚‚Fâ‚‚) jo fluorocarbons ke derivate hain.</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” Câ€“F bond itni special hai (aur fluorocarbons itne inert)</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Câ€“F bond enthalpy bahut high hai (~485 kJ/mol)</strong> â€” compare karo Câ€“H (414 kJ/mol). Isliye Câ€“F bond todo na mushkil: fluorocarbons <strong>thermally stable</strong> (high temperature par bhi), chemical attack se safe, aur non-flammable.</li>
    <li><strong>Fluorine bahut electronegative hai</strong> â€” Câ€“F bond strongly polar, isliye molecule ka "surface" bahut low-energy (non-stick) ho jaata hai; paani aur oil se repel karta hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Fluorine ka size chhota (1.47 Ã… vdW)</strong> â€” wo carbon ke around tight "sleeve" banata hai, jisse enzyme/reagent us core tak pahunch hi nahi paata. Isliye PTFE ko body bhi tolerate karta hai (implant-grade).</li>
    <li>Isi wajah se fluorocarbons ki <strong>surface tension bahut low</strong> hoti hai â€” non-stick pans, water-repellent coatings, lubricants.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

