  <p><strong>Silicates are built from SiOâ‚„â´â» tetrahedra in which each silicon is tetrahedrally bonded to four oxygen atoms. Polymerisation (joining) happens by "corner sharing" â€” i.e. bridging oxygens shared between two tetrahedra form Siâ€“Oâ€“Si links. As the number of shared oxygens per tetrahedron increases from 0 to 4, the structure changes progressively from discrete anions â†’ rings â†’ chains â†’ sheets â†’ three-dimensional frameworks.</strong></p>
