<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q2. CaCâ‚‚ paani se reaction karne par kya deta hai, aur ye kis type ka carbide hai?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">CaCâ‚‚ + 2Hâ‚‚O â†’ Ca(OH)â‚‚ + <strong>Câ‚‚Hâ‚‚ (acetylene)</strong>. Ye ek <strong>ionic (saline) carbide</strong> hai jismein <span class="m">Câ‚‚Â²â» (acetylide)</span> anion hai (Câ‰¡C bond ~1.20 Ã…). Ionic carbide ke do sub-types hote hain: acetylides (Câ‚‚Â²â» â†’ acetylene) aur methanides (Câ´â» â†’ methane, jaise Alâ‚„Câ‚ƒ).</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q3. Talc bahut soft hai par quartz bahut hard â€” silicate structure se kaise explain karoge?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Talc ek <strong>sheet silicate</strong> hai: uske Siâ‚‚Oâ‚… sheets ke beech sirf weak van der Waals forces/Mgâ€“O interactions hain, isliye sheets aasaani se slip kar jaate hain (Mohs 1 â€” sabse soft). Quartz ek <strong>3-D framework</strong> hai jismein har oxygen shared hai â†’ poora solid ek giant covalent network, todhna mushkil (Mohs 7). Yani <strong>sharing badhne = hardness badhti hai, cleavage ghatta hai</strong>. Yahi silicate structural principle ka core hai.</p></div>
</details>
</div>

