<tr><td><strong>Brâ‚ƒâ»</strong></td><td>Linear (Br ka bada size)</td><td>CsBrâ‚ƒ, [N(Bu)â‚„]Brâ‚ƒ</td><td>Iâ‚ƒâ» se kam stable</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>IClâ‚‚â», IBrâ‚‚â»</strong></td><td>Linear (central I, 3 lone pairs)</td><td>KIClâ‚‚, KIBrâ‚‚</td><td>Interhalogen + halide ion se bante hain (ICl + Clâ» â†’ IClâ‚‚â»)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” polyhalides stable hone ke liye "bada cation" kyun chahiye?</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Polyhalide ions bade aur "floppy" (polarisable) hote hain. Agar cation chhota ho (jaise Naâº, Kâº), to large anion ke saath lattice energy kam milti hai aur ion decompose ho jaata hai. Bade cations (Csâº, quaternary ammonium jaise [N(CHâ‚ƒ)â‚„]âº) ke saath <strong>lattice packing aur polarisation effects</strong> itne favourable ho jaate hain ki polyhalide stable rehta hai. Isi tarah Clâ‚ƒâ» (chhota ion) bahut unstable hai â€” isliye Cl ke polyhalides practically nahi milte; bade halogens (I, Br) ke bante hain.</p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Stability order:</strong> Iâ‚ƒâ» &gt; Brâ‚ƒâ» &gt; Clâ‚ƒâ» (bade halogen ke saath zyada stable), aur anion mein jitne atoms zyada honge, utna bada cation chahiye.</p>
</div>

<div class="box quiz">
<p class="bt" style="color:#3a2f7a">Mini knowledge check 11</p>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q1. ClFâ‚ƒ T-shaped kyun hai?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Cl ke around 3 bond pairs (Clâ€“F) aur <strong>2 lone pairs</strong> hain â€” total 5 electron pairs â†’ spÂ³d, trigonal bipyramidal arrangement. Lone pairs <strong>equatorial</strong> positions lete hain (kyunki wahan 90Â° ke kam repulsions hote hain). Isliye bache hue 3 bonds ek "T" banate hain: 1 axial + 2 equatorial. Yahoo VSEPR ka standard "AXâ‚ƒEâ‚‚ â†’ T-shaped" case hai.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q2. Interhalogens halogens se zyada reactive kyun hote hain?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">(i) Xâ€“Y bond Xâ€“X ya Yâ€“Y bond se <strong>weaker</strong> hota hai (dono halogens ke orbitals ka mismatch) â†’ todhna aasan; (ii) electron-negativity difference ki wajah se bond <strong>polar</strong> hota hai, jo electrophilic attack ke liye favourable hai; (iii) interhalogens mein central atom ke paas "expanded octet" (10, 12, 14 electrons) hota hai jo reactive behaviour deta hai. Isliye ICl, Iâ‚‚ se zyada reactive hai aur ClFâ‚ƒ, Clâ‚‚ se bahut zyada.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q3. KIâ‚ƒ (iodine + KI) titration mein use kyun hota hai, sirf iodine kyun nahi?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Kyunki Iâ‚‚ paani mein bahut kam soluble (aur volatile â€” sublimation se loss) hota hai. KI ke saath milene par <span class="m">Iâ‚‚ + Iâ» â‡Œ Iâ‚ƒâ»</span> equilibrium se <strong>triiodide</strong> banta hai, jo soluble aur less volatile hai. Isliye titrations mein standard iodine solution KIâ‚ƒ form mein tayar karte hain, aur starch indicator se endpoint (blue-black) dekhte hain.</p></div>
</details>
</div>

<div class="box recap">
  <p class="bt">Quick Recap â€” Chapter 11</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Interhalogen = do different halogens ka binary compound, XYâ‚™ (n = 1, 3, 5, 7); central atom = larger, less electronegative halogen.</li>
    <li>Types: AX (linear: ClF, ICl), AXâ‚ƒ (T-shaped: ClFâ‚ƒ, BrFâ‚ƒ), AXâ‚… (square pyramidal: BrFâ‚…, IFâ‚…), AXâ‚‡ (pentagonal bipyramidal: IFâ‚‡ â€” sirf I).</li>
    <li>Preparation: direct combination in controlled ratio; reactivity halogens se zyada (weak polar Xâ€“Y bond); hydrolysis + fluorinating uses (ClFâ‚ƒ, BrFâ‚ƒ), ICl for iodination.</li>
    <li>Polyhalides: Iâ‚ƒâ» (linear, spÂ³d, KIâ‚ƒ for titration), Brâ‚ƒâ», Iâ‚…â», Iâ‚‡â», IClâ‚‚â»; bade cations se stabilise hote hain; stability Iâ‚ƒâ» &gt; Brâ‚ƒâ» &gt; Clâ‚ƒâ».</li>
  </ul>
</div>

