<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” noble gases kam reactive kyun hote hain?</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Completely filled valence shell (nsÂ²npâ¶)</strong> â€” octet complete, koi "place" nahi jahan electron accept kiya jaaye.</li>
    <li><strong>Bahut high ionization enthalpy</strong> â€” electron dena bhi mushkil (He 2372 kJ/mol).</li>
    <li><strong>Electron gain enthalpy strongly positive</strong> (unfavourable) â€” anion banane ka koi fayda nahi.</li>
    <li><strong>Bade "n" wale noble gases ke liye exception:</strong> neeche jaane par IE ghatta hai (<strong>Xe 1170 kJ/mol</strong>) aur size badhta hai, isliye Xe (aur thoda Kr) hi actual compounds banate hain â€” aur woh bhi sirf <strong>highly electronegative elements (F, O) ya strong oxidising agents (PtFâ‚†)</strong> ke saath.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="box fact">
  <p class="bt">Historical fact (verified) â€” "inert" se "noble" tak</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>1962</strong> mein Canadian chemist <strong>Neil Bartlett</strong> (University of British Columbia) ne dekha ki <span class="m">PtFâ‚†</span> oxygen ko oxidise karke <span class="m">Oâ‚‚âº[PtFâ‚†]â»</span> banata hai. Usne notice kiya ki Oâ‚‚ ka ionization enthalpy (1175 kJ/mol) aur Xe ka IE (1170 kJ/mol) <strong>lagbhag barabar</strong> hain. To usne Xe aur PtFâ‚† ko mix kiya â€” aur turant ek orange-yellow solid <strong>XePtFâ‚†</strong> ban gaya! Yahi pehla noble gas compound tha. (Iske baad yeh pata chala ki asli product XeFâº jaisi species ka mixture/fluoride bhi involved tha â€” par discovery ka point yahi hai.) Isliye aaj "inert gases" ki jagah "noble gases" kehte hain.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s12-2">12.2 Clathrates â€” noble gases "trap" ho sakte hain</h3>
<p>Xe aur Kr mostly chemical bonds nahi banate (bina F/O ke), par wo <strong>clathrate hydrates</strong> mein physically trap ho sakte hain:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Jaise <strong>8ArÂ·46Hâ‚‚O</strong>, <strong>XeÂ·5.75Hâ‚‚O</strong>, kr clathrate â€” yahan noble gas cage mein phansa hota hai, <em>koi chemical bond nahi</em> banta. Isliye ye "compounds" nahi, "clathrates" kehlate hain.</li>
  <li><strong>Fayda:</strong> isi principle se noble gases ko store/stabilise kiya jaata hai, aur helium/argon ki "trapping" ka study geology mein hota hai.</li>
  <li><strong>Distinguish:</strong> clathrate = physical trapping (host lattice); compound = chemical bonding (XeFâ‚‚, XeOâ‚ƒ). Exam mein ye farq likhna important hai.</li>
</ul>

