    XeFâ‚‚ â†’ spÂ³d (5 pairs: 2 bond + 3 lone) â†’ linear<br>
    XeFâ‚„ â†’ spÂ³dÂ² (6 pairs: 4 bond + 2 lone) â†’ square planar<br>
    XeFâ‚† â†’ spÂ³dÂ³ (7 pairs: 6 bond + 1 lone) â†’ distorted octahedral
  </div>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Ye "expanded octet" ka model hai â€” schools/exams mein yahi likhna hota hai, aur structural predictions (shapes + bond angles) bilkul sahi deta hai.</p>
</div>

<div class="box supp-box">
  <p class="bt">Modern view (enrichment) â€” 3câ€“4e bonding aur d-orbital debate</p>
  <p>Advanced treatment (aur modern computational chemistry) mein ye dikhaaya jaata hai ki:</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>XeFâ‚‚ ko "three-centre four-electron" (3câ€“4e) bond</strong> se explain karte hain (Rundleâ€“Pimentel model): teen collinear atoms (Fâ€“Xeâ€“F) par 4 electrons â€” do bonding-type orbitals bhare hue, jo ek bond order â‰ˆ 0.5 per Xeâ€“F dete hain. Isliye Xeâ€“F bond lengths (XeFâ‚‚ mein â‰ˆ 2.00 Ã…) XeFâ‚„ (â‰ˆ 1.95 Ã…) aur XeFâ‚† (â‰ˆ 1.89 Ã…) se longer hote hain â€” bond order ghatne se lamba bond!</li>
    <li><strong>d-orbital participation par debate:</strong> modern studies suggest karte hain ki "expanded octet" ka literal interpretation (d-orbitals mein electrons daalna) physically accurate nahi hai â€” bonding mein 3câ€“4e/MO descriptions better hain. Isliye exams mein VBT (spÂ³dâ€¦) likhna expected hai, par samajhna ye chahiye ki shapes "electron-pair repulsion" se hi samjhaaya ja sakta hai.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

