<p class="small"><strong>Note:</strong> Proton aur neutron dono ko <strong>nucleons</strong> kehte hain. Modern view: proton aur neutron khud quarks (uud aur udd) se bane hue hain, aur nucleus ke andar unhe pi-meson (pion) exchange se "bandha hua" dekha jaata hai (Yukawa ka meson theory, 1935) â€” ye extra context hai, exam ke liye nuclear force ki properties likhni hain (neohe dekho).</p>

<h3 id="s13-2">13.2 Nuclides aur unka representation</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Nuclide</p>
  <p><strong>A nuclide is a specific atomic species characterised by a definite atomic number (Z) and mass number (A) â€” i.e. a particular nucleus with a specified number of protons and neutrons.</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    <sup>A</sup><sub>Z</sub>X &nbsp;&nbsp;where &nbsp; A = mass number (protons + neutrons = Z + N), &nbsp; Z = atomic number (protons)
    <span class="lbl">Notation: á´¬ZX, e.g., Â¹â¶â‚ˆO, Â²Â³âµâ‚‰â‚‚U, â¶â°â‚‚â‚‡Co, Â¹â´â‚†C</span>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Saath mein: <strong>neutron number N = A âˆ’ Z</strong>. Kisi bhi nuclide ko poori tarah define karne ke liye (A, Z) donon chahiye â€” aur stability ke liye N bahut important hai.</p>
</div>

