<h3 id="s13-4">13.4 Nuclear forces â€” nâ€“n, pâ€“p aur nâ€“p</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Nuclear force ki properties (exam ke liye poori list)</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Strong aur short-range:</strong> nuclear force Coulomb aur gravitational force se bahut strong hai, lekin sirf ~1â€“3 fm (10â»Â¹âµ m) tak kaam karta hai. Isse aage practically zero.</li>
    <li><strong>Charge-independent:</strong> nâ€“n, pâ€“p aur nâ€“p ke beech ka force (attraction) <strong>lagbhag same</strong> hota hai (charge ki parwah nahi karta). Isi liye protonâ€“proton jaise charge wale particles bhi bandhe rehte hain â€” Coulomb repulsion ke bawajood.</li>
    <li><strong>Saturating:</strong> ek nucleon sirf apne padosi nucleons ke saath strong interaction rakhta hai (jaise covalent bond saturation) â€” isliye nucleon ka BE per nucleon ~ constant (~8 MeV) rehta hai, aur nucleus ka density bhi constant (~2.3 Ã— 10Â¹â· kg/mÂ³ â€” jo bahut zyada hai).</li>
    <li><strong>Attractive + repulsive (hard core):</strong> ~0.5 fm se kam distance par force <strong>repulsive</strong> ho jaata hai (hard core) â€” isliye nucleus collapse nahi hota. 1â€“3 fm par strongly attractive.</li>
    <li><strong>Exchange nature:</strong> ye forces pi-meson (pion) exchange se aate hain (Yukawa) â€” jaise chemical bond electrons share karke banta hai, waise hi nucleons mesons exchange karte hain. Also spin/tensor-dependent ("non-central") hote hain â€” isliye deuteron ellipsoidal hota hai.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h3 id="s13-5">13.5 Stability of nucleus â€” n/p ratio</h3>
<p>Stability ka sabse simple predictor: <strong>neutron-to-proton ratio (n/p)</strong>.</p>
<div class="tw">
