<p class="lead">Ye Unit II ka sabse bada aur sabse calculation-heavy chapter hai. Ismein hai: radioactivity ka discovery, decay ke laws (Soddyâ€“Fajans displacement law), half-life aur average life ki derivations, mass defect aur binding energy ka calculation, nuclear reactions, spallation, fission, fusion, atom bomb aur nuclear reactor. Har formula ke saath ek worked numerical diya hai â€” kyunki exam mein yahin se questions aate hain.</p>

<h3 id="s14-1">14.1 Radioactivity â€” discovery aur definition</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Radioactivity</p>
  <p><strong>Radioactivity is the spontaneous disintegration of unstable atomic nuclei with the emission of Î±-particles, Î²-particles and/or Î³-rays, resulting in the formation of a different nucleus. It is a nuclear (not electronic) property and is unaffected by temperature, pressure or chemical state.</strong></p>
</div>

<div class="box fact">
  <p class="bt">History (verified timeline)</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>1896:</strong> Henri Becquerel ne accidentally discover kiya ki uranium salts apne aap "rays" nikaalte hain jo photographic plate ko expose karte hain (urs ko pehle "Becquerel rays" kaha gaya).</li>
    <li><strong>1898:</strong> Marie aur Pierre Curie ne polonium aur radium isolate kiya â€” "radioactivity" term unhone hi popular kiya. (Nobel Prize: Physics 1903 Becquerel + Curies; Chemistry 1911 Marie Curie â€” wo pehli insaan jinke do Nobel mile.)</li>
    <li><strong>1899â€“1900s:</strong> Rutherford ne rays ko Î± aur Î² mein classify kiya (aur baad mein Villard ne Î³).</li>
    <li><strong>1911â€“1913:</strong> Soddy aur Fajans ne "group displacement law" di (Î± aur Î² emission ke liye group shift ka rule).</li>
    <li><strong>1934:</strong> IrÃ¨ne Joliot-Curie aur FrÃ©dÃ©ric Joliot ne <strong>artificial radioactivity</strong> discover ki (aluminium ko Î±-particles se bombard karke Â³â°P bana, jo positron emit karta hai) â€” Nobel 1935.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Natural vs artificial radioactivity</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Natural radioactivity:</strong> unstable nuclei (jo nature mein milte hain, jaise Â²Â³â¸U, Â²Â³Â²Th, â´â°K) <strong>spontaneously</strong> disintegrate karte hain â€” koi external trigger nahi. Ye bade Z wale elements mein common hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Artificial (induced) radioactivity:</strong> stable nuclei ko <strong>projectile (Î±, p, n, Î³) se bombard karke</strong> unstable nuclide banaya jaata hai. Example: <span class="m">Â²â·Al + â´He â†’ Â³â°P + n</span>, phir <span class="m">Â³â°P â†’ Â³â°Si + eâº + Î½</span> (tÂ½ â‰ˆ 2.5 min). Isse medically useful isotopes banaate hain (â¶â°Co, Â¹Â³Â¹I, â¹â¹áµTc).</li>
  </ul>
</div>

