<h3 id="s14-9">14.9 Spallation</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Spallation</p>
  <p><strong>Spallation is a nuclear reaction in which a high-energy projectile (typically protons or neutrons of GeV energy, or cosmic rays) strikes a heavy nucleus and knocks out (chips off) several nucleons or small fragments, leaving a lighter product nucleus â€” as if pieces had been "spalled" off.</strong></p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Farq yaad rakho: <strong>fission</strong> = 2 roughly equal bade pieces (low-energy neutron se bhi); <strong>spallation</strong> = bahut se chhote pieces/nucleons (very high energy projectile chahiye).</p>
</div>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Example (general):</strong> GeV-energy proton + heavy nucleus (jaise Â²â°â¸Pb) â†’ kai nucleons (p, n) aur chhote fragments, aur lighter residual nucleus. (Products ki poori list statistical distribution hoti hai.)</li>
  <li><strong>Uses:</strong> <strong>spallation neutron sources</strong> (SNS/laboratory neutron beams â€” research ke liye), radioactive isotope production, aur <strong>ADS (accelerator-driven systems)</strong> â€” nuclear waste transmutation ke liye research.</li>
  <li><strong>Natural spallation:</strong> cosmic rays atmosphere mein spallation karke <strong>Â¹â´C, Â¹â°Be, Â³H, Â²â¶Al</strong> jaise cosmogenic isotopes banate hain â€” yahi Â¹â´C dating ka base hai (aur geology mein cosmogenic dating).</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="s14-10">14.10 Nuclear fission</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Fission</p>
  <p><strong>Nuclear fission is the splitting of a heavy nucleus into two (or more) lighter nuclei of comparable mass, accompanied by the release of a large amount of energy (~200 MeV per event) and, usually, 2â€“3 neutrons.</strong></p>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Fission ka classic example aur energy calculation</p>
  <p><strong>Reaction (Meitnerâ€“Frisch wala famous example):</strong> <br>
  <span class="m">Â²Â³âµU + n â†’ Â²Â³â¶U* â†’ Â¹â´Â¹Ba + â¹Â²Kr + 3n</span></p>
  <p><strong>Mass check:</strong> 235 + 1 = 236 = 141 + 92 + 3 âœ“</p>
  <p><strong>Energy calc:</strong> masses: Â²Â³âµU = 235.0439, n = 1.00867 â†’ reactants 236.0526 u. Products: Â¹â´Â¹Ba = 140.9144, â¹Â²Kr = 91.9262, 3n = 3.0260 â†’ 235.8666 u.</p>
  <p><span class="m">Î”m = 236.0526 âˆ’ 235.8666 = 0.1860 u âŸ¹ E = 0.1860 Ã— 931.5 = <strong>â‰ˆ 173 MeV</strong></span></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Note:</strong> Ye "immediate" energy hai. Fission products khud radioactive hote hain aur unke Î²â»/Î³ decay se ~25 MeV aur nikalta hai, plus neutrinos ~10 MeV â€” total <strong>â‰ˆ 200 MeV per fission</strong> (jo standard value hai). Compare karo: ek carbon atom ke jalaane se ~4 eV milte hain â€” yani fission lakho guna zyada energy deta hai!</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” chain reaction aur critical mass</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Ek fission se 2â€“3 <strong>neutrons</strong> nikalte hain, jo aage aur fission kara sakte hain â†’ <strong>chain reaction</strong>. Multiplication factor <span class="m">k</span> define karte hain (average neutrons jo next generation mein fission karate hain): <span class="m">k &lt; 1</span> subcritical (reaction ruk jaati hai), <span class="m">k = 1</span> critical (steady power â€” reactor), <span class="m">k &gt; 1</span> supercritical (explosive growth â€” bomb).</li>
    <li><strong>Critical mass:</strong> minimum mass jismein neutrons ka loss (escape) production se kam ho â€” taaki chain reaction chale. Approximate values (bare sphere): <strong>Â²Â³âµU â‰ˆ 50 kg, Â²Â³â¹Pu â‰ˆ 10 kg</strong>; reflector (beryllium/graphite) ya compression se ye kam ho jaata hai. Isliye bomb ya reactor ke liye enriched material + geometry dono important hain.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h3 id="s14-11">14.11 Nuclear fusion</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Fusion</p>
  <p><strong>Nuclear fusion is the joining (fusion) of two light nuclei to form a heavier nucleus with the release of a large amount of energy, because the product nucleus has a higher binding energy per nucleon.</strong></p>
</div>
<div class="eq">
D + T âŸ¶ â´He + n + 17.6 MeV &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(Â²H + Â³H â†’ â´He + n)
<span class="lbl">Dâ€“T reaction â€” fusion reactor/thermonuclear research ka main reaction (lowest ignition temperature)</span>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 5 â€” Dâ€“T fusion ki energy</p>
  <p>Masses: Â²H = 2.014102 u, Â³H = 3.016049 u, â´He = 4.002603 u, n = 1.008665 u.</p>
  <p>Reactants = 5.030151 u; products = 5.011268 u; <span class="m">Î”m = 0.018883 u</span>.</p>
