<h3 id="s14-15">14.15 Hydrogen bomb (thermonuclear bomb)</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Hydrogen bomb</p>
  <p><strong>A hydrogen (thermonuclear / fusion) bomb uses a fission bomb as a trigger to create the enormous temperature and pressure needed to fuse light nuclei (deuteriumâ€“tritium), giving a much larger energy release than a fission bomb alone.</strong></p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Fusion fuel:</strong> deuterium (Â²H) aur tritium (Â³H), often lithium deuteride (LiD) ke roop mein â€” kyunki neutrons lithium se tritium banate hain: <span class="m">â¶Li + n â†’ Â³H + â´He</span>, phir <span class="m">D + T â†’ â´He + n + 17.6 MeV</span>.</li>
    <li><strong>Trigger:</strong> pehle fission bomb phatta hai (temperature ~10â·â€“10â¸ K), jo fusion ignition deta hai â€” isliye ise "two-stage" bomb kehte hain.</li>
    <li><strong>Energy scale:</strong> megatons of TNT (fission bombs kilotons mein hote hain) â€” yani ~1000Ã— zyada.</li>
    <li><strong>History:</strong> pehla test <strong>"Ivy Mike" (USA, 1952)</strong> (liquid-deuterium design). Baad mein solid-fuel (LiD) designs bane. (India ne 1998 Pokhran-II mein thermonuclear device ka test kiya.)</li>
    <li><strong>Note (typo flag):</strong> Aapke syllabus mein "â€¦atom bomb, Nuclear reactor and Hydrogen atom" likha hai. Isi list ke context mein ye <strong>"Hydrogen bomb"</strong> hona chahiye tha â€” maine wahi padhaya hai. Agar aapke syllabus mein iska matlab "hydrogen atom/isotopes" tha, to wahi bhi cover ho gaya hai: fusion fuel ke roop mein <strong>hydrogen ke isotopes (D aur T)</strong> ka poora discussion upar hai.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box quiz">
<p class="bt" style="color:#3a2f7a">Mini knowledge check 14</p>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q1. 1 g Â²Â³â¸U ka poora decay ho jaaye to kitna â´He banega (Î± particles)?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Â²Â³â¸U â†’ Â²â°â¶Pb ke series mein <strong>8 Î± particles</strong> nikalte hain. To 1 mol Â²Â³â¸U (= 238 g) se 8 mol He banta hai. 1 g ke liye: <span class="m">(1/238) Ã— 8 = 0.0336 mol He = 0.0336 Ã— 6.022 Ã— 10Â²Â³ = 2.0 Ã— 10Â²Â² helium atoms</span> (yani ~1.35 mL He at STP). Ye "series arithmetic" ka achha numerical hai.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q2. Half-life 10 din wale sample ki activity 30 din baad kitni bachegi (initially 100 %)?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">30 din = 3 half-lives â†’ <span class="m">(1/2)Â³ = 1/8 â†’ 12.5 %</span>. Ya decay law se: <span class="m">A = Aâ‚€e<sup>âˆ’Î»t</sup></span>, jahan <span class="m">Î» = 0.693/10 = 0.0693 dinâ»Â¹</span>; <span class="m">A = 100 Ã— e^(âˆ’0.0693Ã—30) = 100 Ã— e^(âˆ’2.079) = 12.5 %</span> âœ“ (dono method same answer dete hain).</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q3. Fusion mein per kg zyada energy kyun milti hai jabki per event energy kam (17.6 vs 200 MeV) hai?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Kyunki energy <strong>per unit mass</strong> compare karni chahiye. Fission: 200 MeV per 235 u â‰ˆ 0.85 MeV/nucleon. Fusion (Dâ€“T): 17.6 MeV per ~5 u â‰ˆ 3.5 MeV/nucleon â€” yani <strong>fusion mein per nucleon zyada energy release</strong> hoti hai, kyunki Dâ€“T nuclei halke hain aur unka B.E./A jump bahut bada hota hai (1.1 â†’ 7.07 MeV He ke liye). Isliye fusion ~4Ã— zyada energy deta hai per kg.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q4. Critical mass kya hai aur kaunse do factors ise kam karte hain?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Critical mass = minimum fissile mass jismein neutron production (fission se) neutron loss (escape + absorption) se zyada ho, taaki chain reaction sustain ho. Isko kam karne ke do factors: <strong>(i) reflector</strong> (neutrons ko wapas core mein bhejta hai â€” beryllium/graphite), aur <strong>(ii) compression/density increase</strong> (implosion) â€” high density se neutron escape ki probability kam hoti hai. Isi wajah se implosion designs kam material mein bhi supercritical ho jaate hain.</p></div>
</details>
</div>

