<section id="ch1">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 1 Â· Unit I</span>Relations on a Set</h2>

<p class="lead">Poora Unit I isi ek idea par bana hai: <strong>do elements ke beech "sambandh" (relation)</strong>. Equivalence relation, partial order, lattice, Boolean algebra â€” sab relations hi hain, bas unki properties alag hain. Isliye ye chapter sabse dhyan se padho.</p>

<h3 id="s1-1">1.1 Sets aur Cartesian product â€” 30-second recap</h3>
<p>Ek <strong>set</strong> sirf objects ka collection hai; <em>element</em> ka matlab hai us collection ka member. Ab agar aap do sets <span class="m">A</span> aur <span class="m">B</span> lo, to unka <strong>Cartesian product</strong> ye hota hai:</p>
<div class="eq">
A Ã— B = { (a, b) : a âˆˆ A, b âˆˆ B }
<span class="lbl">Ordered pairs ka set â€” (a, b) â‰  (b, a) (order matter karta hai!)</span>
</div>
<p>Jaise <span class="m">A = {1, 2}</span>, <span class="m">B = {x, y}</span> â†’ <span class="m">A Ã— B = {(1,x), (1,y), (2,x), (2,y)}</span>. Ginti yaad rakho:</p>
<div class="eq">|A Ã— B| = |A| Ã— |B| &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; aur &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |A Ã— A| = |A|Â²</div>

<div class="box mnemonic" style="display:block">
  <strong>Yaad rakho:</strong> Cartesian product <em>saare possible pairs</em> banata hai (chahe koi "meaningful" rishta ho ya na ho). Relation usme se <em>chuni hui</em> pairs ka subset hota hai. Isliye: <strong>relation âŠ‚ A Ã— B</strong>.
</div>

<h3 id="s1-2">1.2 Relation â€” definition aur notation</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Binary relation</p>
  <p><strong>A binary relation R from a set A to a set B is a subset of A Ã— B. Agar (a, b) âˆˆ R, to kehte hain "a is related to b" aur likhte hain a R b. Jab A = B ho, to R ko "a relation on the set A" kehte hain (yaani R âŠ† A Ã— A).</strong></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Domain</strong> = {a âˆˆ A : kisi b ke liye (a, b) âˆˆ R}, &nbsp; <strong>Range</strong> = {b âˆˆ B : kisi a ke liye (a, b) âˆˆ R}, &nbsp; <strong>Codomain</strong> = B.</p>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 1 â€” relation banana aur domain/range nikalna</p>
  <p><span class="m">A = {1, 2, 3, 4}</span> par relation <span class="m">R = {(a, b) : a divides b}</span> (a | b) define karo.</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>Pairs check karo: 1 har number ko divide karta hai â†’ (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4).</li>
    <li>2 divides 2, 4 â†’ (2,2),(2,4). &nbsp; 3 divides 3 â†’ (3,3). &nbsp; 4 divides 4 â†’ (4,4).</li>
    <li>Isliye <span class="m">R = {(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,2),(2,4),(3,3),(4,4)}</span> â†’ <strong>8 pairs</strong>.</li>
    <li>Domain = Range = A = {1,2,3,4} (kyunki 1 har jagah hai aur har element khud ko divide karta hai).</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Note:</strong> <span class="m">|A Ã— A| = 16</span> pairs possible the; humne unmein se 8 chune â€” kyunki relation sirf ek <em>subset</em> hai.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s1-3">1.3 Relation ko represent karna â€” matrix aur digraph</h3>
<p>Chhote finite sets par relation ko do tarike se dikhate hain (dono exam mein poochhe jaate hain):</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Zero-one matrix (relation matrix):</strong> rows = A ke elements, columns = B ke elements; agar <span class="m">(a<sub>i</sub>, b<sub>j</sub>) âˆˆ R</span> ho to entry <strong>1</strong>, warna <strong>0</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>Directed graph (digraph):</strong> har element ek <em>vertex</em> (gol); agar <span class="m">(a, b) âˆˆ R</span> ho to <span class="m">a â†’ b</span> ek <em>arrow</em> (edge). Agar <span class="m">(a, a) âˆˆ R</span> ho to us vertex par <strong>loop</strong> banta hai.</li>
</ol>

