<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 2 â€” properties check karna (exam favourite)</p>
  <p><span class="m">A = {1, 2, 3}</span> aur <span class="m">R = {(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,2),(2,1)}</span>. Saari properties check karo.</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li><strong>Reflexive?</strong> (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) sab present âœ“ â†’ <strong>haan</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Symmetric?</strong> (1,2) hai aur (2,1) bhi hai âœ“; baaki self-pairs khud symmetric hain â†’ <strong>haan</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Antisymmetric?</strong> (1,2) aur (2,1) dono hain par 1 â‰  2 â†’ antisymmetric ki condition toot gayi â†’ <strong>nahi</strong>. (Important: symmetric aur antisymmetric ek saath sirf tab ho sakte hain jab do alag elements ke beech koi pair na ho.)</li>
    <li><strong>Transitive?</strong> (1,2) aur (2,1) hain â†’ (1,1) chahiye âœ“ present; (2,1) aur (1,2) â†’ (2,2) âœ“; baaki chains self-pairs se banti hain jo already present hain â†’ <strong>haan</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Ye relation <strong>equivalence relation</strong> hai (reflexive + symmetric + transitive) â€” Ch 2 mein isi ko detail mein karenge.</p>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” 3 sabse common galtiyan</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>"Symmetric ka matlab antisymmetric ka ulta hai"</strong> â€” galat. Symmetric = dono arrows hone chahiye; antisymmetric = dono arrows kabhi na ho (sirf a = b ke case mein allowed). Ek relation dono ho sakta hai (jaise equality), aur ek bhi nahi (jaise R = {(1,2),(2,3)}).</li>
    <li><strong>"Transitive check karne mein sirf ek chain dekh li"</strong> â€” galat. <em>Har</em> possible pair of pairs check karna hai. Exam mein short-cut: sab relations ki list banao jahan pehla element doosre ke second-element se match kare.</li>
    <li><strong>Reflexive = "kuch loops hain"</strong> â€” galat. <em>Saare</em> elements par loop chahiye. Ek bhi missing hua to reflexive nahi.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h3 id="s1-5">1.5 Composition aur inverse relation</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definitions</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Inverse relation:</strong> <span class="m">R<sup>âˆ’1</sup> = {(b, a) : (a, b) âˆˆ R}</span> â€” bas har pair ulta kar do. Matrix mein iska matlab <em>transpose</em> hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Composition:</strong> agar <span class="m">R âŠ† A Ã— B</span> aur <span class="m">S âŠ† B Ã— C</span>, to <span class="m">S âˆ˜ R = {(a, c) : koi b âˆˆ B ho jisme (a, b) âˆˆ R aur (b, c) âˆˆ S}</span>. (Note: <span class="m">S âˆ˜ R</span> mein pehle R lagta hai â€” "right to left" padho.)</li>
    <li><strong>Power:</strong> <span class="m">RÂ² = R âˆ˜ R</span>, <span class="m">RÂ³ = RÂ² âˆ˜ R</span>, aur <span class="m">R<sup>n</sup></span> mein "n steps" ki reachability hoti hai.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 3 â€” composition</p>
  <p><span class="m">R = {(1,2),(2,3),(3,1)}</span> aur <span class="m">S = {(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(2,3)}</span> dono {1,2,3} par.</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>S âˆ˜ R ke liye: R ka second element â†’ S ka first element match karo.</li>
    <li>(1,2) âˆˆ R aur (2,2) âˆˆ S â†’ (1,2) âˆˆ S âˆ˜ R; &nbsp;(1,2) âˆˆ R aur (2,3) âˆˆ S â†’ (1,3) âˆˆ S âˆ˜ R.</li>
    <li>(2,3) âˆˆ R aur (3,3) âˆˆ S â†’ (2,3) âˆˆ S âˆ˜ R; &nbsp;(3,1) âˆˆ R, par S mein 1 se shuru hone wala pair nahi (except (1,1)) â†’ check karo (1,1) âˆˆ S hai! â†’ (3,1) âˆˆ S âˆ˜ R.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>S âˆ˜ R = {(1,2), (1,3), (2,3), (3,1)}</strong>. R âˆ˜ S nikaalne par jawab <em>alag</em> aayega â€” isliye composition mein order maayne rakhta hai (non-commutative).</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s1-6">1.6 Closures â€” relation ko "theek" karna</h3>
<p>Aksar ek relation kuch properties <em>almost</em> satisfy karta hai. Us relation mein <strong>sabse chhoti (minimum) additions</strong> karke jo naya relation banta hai jisme wo property aa jaaye, use us property ka <strong>closure</strong> kehte hain.</p>
<div class="tw">
<table>
