<thead><tr><th>Closure</th><th>Rule</th><th>Example (A = {1,2,3}, R = {(1,2),(2,3)})</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Reflexive closure</strong></td><td>R âˆª {(a,a) : a âˆˆ A}</td><td>{(1,2),(2,3),(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)}</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Symmetric closure</strong></td><td>R âˆª R<sup>âˆ’1</sup></td><td>{(1,2),(2,1),(2,3),(3,2)}</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Transitive closure</strong></td><td>R âˆª RÂ² âˆª RÂ³ âˆª â€¦ âˆª R<sup>n</sup> (n = |A|)</td><td>{(1,2),(2,3),<strong>(1,3)</strong>} â€” sirf transitively-implied pair add hua</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="box supp-box">
  <p class="bt">Beyond syllabus Â· Warshall's algorithm (transitive closure ka fast tarika)</p>
  <p>Transitive closure nikaalne ka efficient tarika Boolean matrix par kaam karta hai (O(nÂ³)): matrix se shuru karke har k ke liye rule lagao â€” <em>agar i se k rasta hai aur k se j rasta hai, to (i, j) entry bhi 1 kar do</em> (<span class="m">M<sub>ij</sub> = M<sub>ij</sub> âˆ¨ (M<sub>ik</sub> âˆ§ M<sub>kj</sub>)</span>). Ye Dijkstra/Floyd ke saath hi "algorithms" wale questions mein kaam aata hai.</p>
  <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:0">Chhote sets par "paths ki list" banakar transitive closure haath se bhi nikaal sakte ho â€” exam mein wahi likhna theek hai; Warshall ko algorithm ka naam likh dena bonus hai.</p>
</div>

