<h3 id="s2-2">2.2 Equivalence class â€” definition aur intuition</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Equivalence class</p>
  <p><strong>Let R be an equivalence relation on A and a âˆˆ A. The equivalence class of a, written [a], is the set of all elements of A that are related to a:</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">[a] = { x âˆˆ A : (a, x) âˆˆ R }</div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Equivalence classes ko <span class="m">aÌ…</span> ya <span class="m">[a]<sub>R</sub></span> bhi likhte hain. Jo element class ko "name" deta hai use <strong>representative</strong> kehte hain.</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” "class" ka matlab kya hai (rozz-marra analogy)</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Socho aap 100 students ki class mein "same birthday month" ke hisaab se groups banao. Ye relation (same month) reflexive, symmetric aur transitive hai â€” yaani equivalence. Ab har group ek <strong>equivalence class</strong> hai (December-wale, July-waleâ€¦), koi student kisi do groups mein nahi ja sakta, aur saare students kisi na kisi group mein hain. Yahi idea maths mein <strong>partition</strong> kehlata hai â€” aur ye itna powerful hai ki isi se "modular arithmetic" aur "quotient structures" bante hain.</p>
</div>

