<h3 id="s2-3">2.3 Equivalence relation âŸº partition (theorem)</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Theorem (aur uski definition)</p>
  <p><strong>Theorem:</strong> Ek equivalence relation R on A ka set of all equivalence classes ek <strong>partition</strong> of A banata hai. Ulta bhi sach hai: A ka koi bhi partition ek equivalence relation deta hai (do elements related hain âŸº wo ek hi block mein hain).</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Partition ka matlab:</strong> A ke non-empty subsets ka collection {Aâ‚, Aâ‚‚, â€¦} aisa ho ki (i) koi do blocks overlap na karein <span class="m">(A<sub>i</sub> âˆ© A<sub>j</sub> = âˆ…)</span>, aur (ii) saare blocks ka union poora A ho.</p>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Proof â€” 4 zaroori steps (exam mein poora likhna hai)</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li><strong>Step 1 (Har class non-empty hai):</strong> R reflexive hai, isliye <span class="m">(a, a) âˆˆ R</span> âŸ¹ <span class="m">a âˆˆ [a]</span>. Yaani koi class khaali nahi.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 2 (Do classes ya identical hain ya disjoint):</strong> Maan lo <span class="m">[a] âˆ© [b] â‰  âˆ…</span>, to koi <span class="m">c âˆˆ [a] âˆ© [b]</span> hai. Iska matlab <span class="m">(a, c) âˆˆ R</span> aur <span class="m">(b, c) âˆˆ R</span>. Symmetry se <span class="m">(c, b) âˆˆ R</span>, phir transitivity se <span class="m">(a, b) âˆˆ R</span>. Ab kisi bhi <span class="m">x âˆˆ [a]</span> ke liye: <span class="m">(a,x) âˆˆ R</span> aur <span class="m">(a,b) âˆˆ R</span> âŸ¹â€¦ (transitivity + symmetry jodkar) <span class="m">(b, x) âˆˆ R</span> âŸ¹ <span class="m">x âˆˆ [b]</span>. Isliye <span class="m">[a] âŠ† [b]</span>, aur same tarah <span class="m">[b] âŠ† [a]</span> â†’ <strong>[a] = [b]</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 3 (Union poora A hai):</strong> Har <span class="m">a âˆˆ A</span> apni hi class <span class="m">[a]</span> mein hai (Step 1), isliye saari classes ka union A hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 4 (Ulta):</strong> Partition diya ho to relation <span class="m">a R b âŸº a aur b ek hi block mein</span> reflexive, symmetric aur transitive hai (har property block ki definition se seedha nikal aati hai) â†’ equivalence relation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Isliye:</strong> "equivalence relations count karna" aur "partitions count karna" ek hi cheez hai â€” dono ki ginti <strong>Bell number</strong> B<sub>n</sub> se hoti hai: Bâ‚ = 1, Bâ‚‚ = 2, Bâ‚ƒ = 5, Bâ‚„ = 15, Bâ‚… = 52. (Aur "exactly k blocks" ki ginti <strong>Stirling number of second kind</strong> S(n, k) â€” supplementary.)</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s2-4">2.4 Congruence mod m â€” sabse important example</h3>
<div class="eq">
a â‰¡ b (mod m) &nbsp;âŸº&nbsp; m divides (a âˆ’ b) &nbsp;âŸº&nbsp; a âˆ’ b = km for some k âˆˆ â„¤
<span class="lbl">m â‰¥ 1; ise "a congruent to b modulo m" padhte hain</span>
</div>
<p>Is relation ke <strong>exactly m equivalence classes</strong> hote hain: <span class="m">[0], [1], â€¦, [mâˆ’1]</span> (remainders). Isliye quotient set <span class="m">â„¤/R = â„¤<sub>m</sub></span> ko hum "mod m integers" kehte hain â€” aur isi par clock arithmetic, check digits, aur cryptography banti hai.</p>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example â€” congruence ke class nikaalna</p>
  <p>m = 4 ke liye, numbers 0 se 11 ke classes likho.</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>[0] = {0, 4, 8} (remainder 0), &nbsp; [1] = {1, 5, 9}, &nbsp; [2] = {2, 6, 10}, &nbsp; [3] = {3, 7, 11}.</li>
    <li>Check karo: 7 âˆ’ 3 = 4 jo 4 se divisible hai â†’ 7 â‰¡ 3 (mod 4) âœ“, isliye dono ek hi class [3] mein.</li>
    <li>Total 4 classes â†’ <span class="m">â„¤â‚„ = {[0],[1],[2],[3]}</span>; class [5] bhi [1] hi hai (5 â‰¡ 1 mod 4) â€” isliye likhte hain <strong>[5] = [1]</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Clock analogy:</strong> 12-ghante ki ghadi mod 12 hai; 15:00 â†’ 3:00 (15 â‰¡ 3 mod 12). Rahul, real life.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s2-5">2.5 0/1 matrix se equivalence check karna (fast method)</h3>
<p>Chhote sets par exam mein teen properties matrix se 10 second mein verify ho jaati hain:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Reflexive:</strong> main diagonal (top-left se bottom-right) ki saari entries <strong>1</strong> hon.</li>
  <li><strong>Symmetric:</strong> matrix apne transpose ke barabar ho (<span class="m">M = Máµ—</span>) â€” matlab matrix diagonal ke aas-paas mirror ho.</li>
  <li><strong>Transitive:</strong> <span class="m">MÂ²</span> ki non-zero entries, M ki non-zero entries ke "andar" hon â€” yaani jo pair MÂ² mein 1 hai wo M mein bhi 1 ho (<span class="m">MÂ² â‰¤ M</span>, entry-wise).</li>
</ul>
<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” class aur representative par</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Class ke naam se confuse mat ho: <span class="m">[3]</span> aur <span class="m">[7]</span> mod 4 mein <strong>wahi class</strong> hai. Isliye "class ke kitne elements hain" aur "kitni classes hain" â€” ye do alag sawaal hain. Aur ek equivalence class ka koi bhi element us class ka <em>representative</em> ban sakta hai; isi liye partition theorem mein "classes" ki baat hoti hai, "representatives" ki nahi.</p>
</div>

<div class="box quiz">
<p class="bt" style="color:#123a6b">Mini knowledge check 2</p>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q1. A = {1,2,3,4} par R = {(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4),(1,2),(2,1)} â€” equivalence classes likhiye.</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">R equivalence hai: [1] = {1,2} (kyunki 1 R 2), [2] = {1,2} (same class!), [3] = {3}, [4] = {4}. Isliye partition hai <strong>{{1,2}, {3}, {4}}</strong> â€” kuch students galti se [1] aur [2] ko alag likh dete hain, jabki wo ek hi class hai.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q2. Ek 3-element set par kitne equivalence relations bante hain?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Bell number Bâ‚ƒ = <strong>5</strong>. Enumerate karo (partitions se): {1,2,3} ek hi block; {1,2}|{3}; {1,3}|{2}; {2,3}|{1}; {1}|{2}|{3}. Bas 5 â€” isliye 3-element set par 5 equivalence relations hote hain (jabki total relations 512 the!).</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q3. "a âˆ’ b divisible by 5" ka relation â„¤ par â€” kitni equivalence classes? Kaunsi?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Ye congruence mod 5 hai â†’ <strong>5 classes</strong>: [0],[1],[2],[3],[4] â€” yaani remainder ke hisaab se. Har class infinitely badi hai (â€¦, âˆ’5, 0, 5, 10, â€¦ jaise). Yehi â„¤â‚… banta hai, aur isi par "mod 5 arithmetic" chalti hai.</p></div>
</details>
</div>

<div class="box recap">
  <p class="bt">Quick Recap â€” Chapter 2</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Equivalence relation = reflexive + symmetric + transitive.</li>
    <li>Equivalence class <span class="m">[a] = {x : (a, x) âˆˆ R}</span> â€” khaali nahi hoti, do classes ya identical hoti hain ya disjoint.</li>
    <li><strong>Fundamental theorem:</strong> equivalence relations âŸº partitions (proof ke 4 steps yaad rakho).</li>
    <li>Congruence mod m: m classes <span class="m">[0]â€¦[mâˆ’1]</span>; â„¤â‚˜ quotient set; clock arithmetic ka base.</li>
    <li>Counting: total = Bell number Bâ‚™; exactly k blocks = Stirling number S(n,k) (supplementary).</li>
    <li>Matrix shortcut: diagonal sab 1 â†’ reflexive; M = Máµ— â†’ symmetric; MÂ² â‰¤ M â†’ transitive.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box exam">
  <p class="bt">Exam corner â€” Chapter 2</p>
  <p><strong>Short:</strong> (i) Equivalence relation define kijiye. (ii) Equivalence class ka definition. (iii) Partition define kijiye. (iv) 3-element set par kitne equivalence relations? (v) â„¤ par "a â‰¡ b (mod 7)" ke kitne classes honge?</p>
  <p><strong>Long:</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Dikhaiye ki ek set A par equivalence relation, A ka partition banata hai (poora proof â€” 4 steps).</li>
    <li>A = {1,2,3,4,5} par relation R = {(a,b) : a âˆ’ b, 3 se divisible} ke liye dikhaiye ki R equivalence hai aur uske equivalence classes likhiye. <span class="small">[Classes: {3}, {1,4}, {2,5}]</span></li>
    <li>"Equivalence relation" aur "partition" ke beech ka oneâ€“one correspondence example ke saath samjhaiye. 4-element set ke saare partitions bhi likhiye. <span class="small">[Bâ‚„ = 15]</span></li>
  </ol>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Conceptual:</strong> "a â‰¤ b" â„¤ par equivalence relation kyun nahi hai â€” kaunsi property tootti hai? <span class="small">[Symmetric nahi hai: 2 â‰¤ 5 sach hai par 5 â‰¤ 2 nahi. Isliye ye partial order hai, equivalence nahi (Ch 3 dekho).]</span></p>
</div>
