<html><head><meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><section id="ch3">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 3 Â· Unit I</span>Partial Order Relations, Hasse Diagrams, Chains &amp; Anti-chains</h2>

<p class="lead">Pichhle chapter mein humne wo relations dekhe jo "sab kuch barabar" kar dete hain (equivalence). Ab hum ulta kaam karte hain: <strong>hierarchy</strong> wale relations â€” jo batate hain "kaun chhota hai, kaun bada", "kaun pehle aata hai", "kaun kis ke andar hai". Yehi partial order hai, aur iske diagrams (Hasse diagrams) Unit I ke sabse scoring questions hain.</p>

<h3 id="s3-1">3.1 Partial order relation aur poset</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Partial order relation (poset)</p>
  <p><strong>A relation R on a set A is a partial order relation (or partial ordering) if it is reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive. The pair (A, R) is then called a partially ordered set, or poset.</strong></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Standard notation: partial order ko aksar <span class="m">â‰¼</span> ya <span class="m">â‰¤</span> se likhte hain. Note karo: itna hi nahi kaha jaata ki <em>har</em> do elements compare ho sakte hain â€” isliye naam "partial" hai.</p>
</div>
<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Classic posets (ye table yaad rakho â€” poore unit mein inhi examples ka use hota hai)</caption>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Set</th><th>Relation</th><th>Reflexive</th><th>Antisym.</th><th>Transitive</th><th>Poset?</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>â„¤ (ya â„)</td><td>a â‰¤ b</td><td>âœ“</td><td>âœ“</td><td>âœ“</td><td><strong>Haan</strong> (total order bhi)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>â„¤âº</td><td>a divides b (a | b)</td><td>âœ“</td><td>âœ“</td><td>âœ“</td><td><strong>Haan</strong> (classic "divisibility poset")</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>P(S) (power set)</td><td>A âŠ† B</td><td>âœ“</td><td>âœ“</td><td>âœ“</td><td><strong>Haan</strong> (inclusion poset)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Students</td><td>"x ki roll number â‰¤ y ki"</td><td>âœ“</td><td>âœ“</td><td>âœ“</td><td>Haan (total order)</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Strings</td><td>Dictionary (lexicographic) order</td><td>âœ“</td><td>âœ“</td><td>âœ“</td><td>Haan</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>â„¤</td><td>a &lt; b</td><td>âœ—</td><td>âœ“</td><td>âœ“</td><td><strong>Nahi</strong> (reflexive nahi â€” "strict" order)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Poset mein do elements <span class="m">a, b</span> ko <strong>comparable</strong> kehte hain agar <span class="m">a â‰¼ b</span> ya <span class="m">b â‰¼ a</span> ho; warna wo <strong>incomparable</strong> hain (likhte hain <span class="m">a âˆ¥ b</span>). Jaise divisibility poset mein 2 | 6 (comparable), par 2 aur 3 <strong>incomparable</strong> hain â€” kyunki na 2 | 3, na 3 | 2. Isi "incomparable" hone ki wajah se "partial" kehte hain.</p>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” partial order vs equivalence (sabse common confusion)</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Dono mein <strong>reflexive + transitive</strong> common hain. Farq sirf teesri property ka hai: equivalence mein <strong>symmetric</strong>, partial order mein <strong>antisymmetric</strong>. Isliye "a divides b" partial order hai, "a â‰¡ b (mod m)" equivalence hai. Aur <strong>strict</strong> orders (jaise &lt;) ko agar poset banana hai to <span class="m">a â‰º b âŸº (a â‰¤ b aur a â‰  b)</span> likhkar samjhao â€” &lt; khud reflexive nahi hota.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s3-2">3.2 Hasse diagram â€” poset ko kaagaz par dikhane ka tarika</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Hasse diagram banane ke 4 rules (exam mein likhne hain)</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Poset ko ek graph samjho jisme har element ek <em>vertex</em> hai aur <span class="m">a â‰º b</span> ek <em>edge</em> hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Loops hata do</strong> (reflexive hone ki wajah se har jagah loop hota hai â€” likhne ka matlab nahi).</li>
    <li><strong>Transitively implied edges hata do</strong>: agar <span class="m">a â‰º b â‰º c</span> ho, to <span class="m">a â‰º c</span> wala edge mat banao (wo apne aap samajh aata hai).</li>
    <li><strong>"Bada" element upar aur "chhota" neeche</strong> rakho, aur edge ko bas line (arrow ke bina) se jodo â€” kyunki direction "upar" se pata chal jaati hai.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

