<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” chains aur anti-chains itne important kyun hain?</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Kyunki ye poset ki "do extreme sochein" hain: <strong>chain</strong> = maximum structure (sab kuch compare ho sakta hai â€” jaise marks ka order), <strong>anti-chain</strong> = bilkul structure-free (koi comparison nahi â€” jaise {2, 3, 5} divisors). Asli duniya ke posets inke beech hote hain, aur yahi baat <strong>Dilworth's theorem</strong> kehti hai (neeche).</p>
</div>

<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Dilworth's theorem (statement â€” application ke saath)</p>
  <p><strong>Theorem (Dilworth, 1950):</strong> Kisi bhi finite poset mein, <em>minimum</em> number of chains jo poore poset ko cover karti hain = us poset ki <em>maximum</em> anti-chain ka size (yaani width).</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Example (Dâ‚ƒâ‚€):</strong> width = 3 hai, isliye Dâ‚ƒâ‚€ ko <strong>3 chains</strong> se cover kiya ja sakta hai: <span class="m">{1, 2, 6, 30}</span>, <span class="m">{3, 15}</span>, <span class="m">{5, 10}</span> â€” dekho, teeno chains mila kar saare 8 elements aa gaye, aur 3 se kam chains se kaam nahi chalega. Yahi Dilworth ka "min = max" wala point hai.</p>
</div>

