<h3 id="s4-2">4.2 Lattice ki doosri definition â€” algebraic structure</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Lattice as an algebra</p>
  <p><strong>A lattice is a non-empty set L with two binary operations âˆ§ (meet) and âˆ¨ (join) satisfying, for all a, b, c âˆˆ L:</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    (1) Idempotent: a âˆ§ a = a, &nbsp; a âˆ¨ a = a<br>
    (2) Commutative: a âˆ§ b = b âˆ§ a, &nbsp; a âˆ¨ b = b âˆ¨ a<br>
    (3) Associative: (a âˆ§ b) âˆ§ c = a âˆ§ (b âˆ§ c), &nbsp; (a âˆ¨ b) âˆ¨ c = a âˆ¨ (b âˆ¨ c)<br>
    (4) Absorption: a âˆ§ (a âˆ¨ b) = a, &nbsp; a âˆ¨ (a âˆ§ b) = a
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Dono definitions <strong>equivalent</strong> hain: poset wale version mein <span class="m">a âˆ§ b = GLB</span> aur <span class="m">a âˆ¨ b = LUB</span> rakh do, to ye saare laws apne aap nikal aate hain â€” aur ulta bhi. Exam mein aap koi bhi definition likh sakte ho, par <em>consistency</em> rakho.</p>
</div>
<p>Algebraic version ka fayda: <strong>duality principle</strong> turant mil jaata hai â€” koi bhi lattice identity sahi ho, to âˆ§ aur âˆ¨ ko aapas mein badalne se (aur â‰¤ ko â‰¥ se) bani identity bhi sahi hoti hai. Jaise absorption ka doosra form pehle ka "dual" hai.</p>

<h3 id="s4-3">4.3 Bounded lattice, sublattice aur kuch zaroori terms</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bounded lattice:</strong> Jisme greatest element (1) aur least element (0) maujood hon. Jaise Dâ‚ƒâ‚€ mein 1 aur 30; P(S) mein âˆ… aur S. Divisor lattice Dâ‚™ hamesha bounded hota hai: 0 = 1, 1 = n.</li>
  <li><strong>Sublattice:</strong> L ka non-empty subset jo khud lattice ho â€” yaani jo <em>same</em> âˆ§/âˆ¨ operations ke andar band ho. (Dhyan: sirf subset hone se sublattice nahi hota; closure chahiye.)</li>
  <li><strong>Distributive lattice (aage detail):</strong> jisme meet join par distribute kare.</li>
  <li><strong>Complemented lattice:</strong> bounded lattice jisme har element ka complement ho.</li>
</ul>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 1 â€” sublattice check</p>
  <p>Dâ‚ƒâ‚€ = {1,2,3,5,6,10,15,30} ka subset {1, 2, 3, 6} sublattice hai?</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>2 âˆ¨ 3 = lcm(2,3) = <strong>6</strong> â€” set mein hai âœ“. &nbsp; 2 âˆ§ 3 = gcd = <strong>1</strong> â€” set mein hai âœ“.</li>
    <li>1 âˆ¨ 6 = 6 âœ“, 1 âˆ§ 6 = 1 âœ“; 2 âˆ¨ 6 = 6 âœ“, 2 âˆ§ 6 = 2 âœ“; 3 âˆ¨ 6 = 6 âœ“, 3 âˆ§ 6 = 3 âœ“.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Saare pairs ka join/meet set ke andar hai â†’ <strong>haan, {1,2,3,6} sublattice hai</strong> (aur ye ek "diamond", yaani Mâ‚‚ = Bâ‚‚ hai).</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s4-4">4.4 Distributive lattice</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Distributive lattice</p>
  <p><strong>A lattice L is distributive if for all a, b, c âˆˆ L:</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    a âˆ§ (b âˆ¨ c) = (a âˆ§ b) âˆ¨ (a âˆ§ c) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (aur dual form bhi automatically chalti hai)
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Dual form: <span class="m">a âˆ¨ (b âˆ§ c) = (a âˆ¨ b) âˆ§ (a âˆ¨ c)</span>. Duality principle ke chalte ek form prove karna kaafi hai.</p>
</div>
