<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Theorem â€” distributivity ka test (Birkhoff)</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>A lattice is distributive if and only if it contains no sublattice isomorphic to Mâ‚ƒ or to Nâ‚….</strong> Isliye exam mein "distributive nahi" dikhane ke liye sirf <em>ek</em> counterexample pair likhna kaafi hai: usually <span class="m">a âˆ§ (b âˆ¨ c) â‰  (a âˆ§ b) âˆ¨ (a âˆ§ c)</span>.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s4-5">4.5 Complemented lattice</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Complement aur complemented lattice</p>
  <p><strong>Let L be a bounded lattice with least element 0 and greatest element 1. An element b âˆˆ L is a complement of a âˆˆ L if a âˆ§ b = 0 and a âˆ¨ b = 1. L is complemented if every element has at least one complement.</strong></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Note: complement <em>unique nahi</em> bhi ho sakta hai (Mâ‚ƒ mein har middle element ke do complements hote hain) â€” uniqueness ke liye distributivity chahiye (next point).</p>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 2 â€” complements: Dâ‚ƒâ‚€ vs Dâ‚â‚‚ (classic)</p>
  <p><strong>(a) Dâ‚ƒâ‚€ mein complements nikaalo.</strong> 30 = 2 Ã— 3 Ã— 5 (square-free), 0 = 1, 1 = 30.</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>2 ka complement: chahiye <span class="m">2 âˆ¨ x = 30</span> aur <span class="m">2 âˆ§ x = 1</span> â†’ x = <strong>15</strong> (lcm(2,15) = 30 âœ“, gcd = 1 âœ“).</li>
    <li>3 ka: x = 10 âœ“ (lcm 30, gcd 1). &nbsp; 5 ka: x = 6 âœ“. &nbsp; 1 ka complement 30 âœ“ (aur 30 ka 1). &nbsp; 6 ka 5 âœ“, 10 ka 3 âœ“, 15 ka 2 âœ“.</li>
    <li><strong>Har element ka complement mil gaya â†’ Dâ‚ƒâ‚€ complemented hai</strong> (aur complement unique hai).</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>(b) Dâ‚â‚‚ mein 2 ka complement dhoondho.</strong> 12 = 2Â²Â·3, 0 = 1, 1 = 12. Chahiye <span class="m">2 âˆ¨ x = 12</span> aur <span class="m">2 âˆ§ x = 1</span>:</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>x = 3: lcm(2,3) = 6 â‰  12 âœ— &nbsp;|&nbsp; x = 4: gcd(2,4) = 2 â‰  1 âœ— &nbsp;|&nbsp; x = 6: gcd = 2 âœ— &nbsp;|&nbsp; x = 12: gcd = 2 âœ—</li>
    <li>Koi bhi element dono conditions satisfy nahi karta â†’ <strong>2 ka complement hai hi nahi â†’ Dâ‚â‚‚ complemented nahi hai</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Rule yaad rakho:</strong> Dâ‚™ complemented hota hai <strong>âŸº n square-free ho</strong> (2 Ã— 3 Ã— 5 = 30 âœ“, par 12 = 2Â²Â·3 âœ—). Square-free ke case mein Dâ‚™ poora Boolean algebra ban jaata hai!</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s4-6">4.6 Distributive + Complemented = Boolean algebra</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Connection (aur next chapter ka bridge)</p>
  <p><strong>A lattice is called a Boolean algebra if it is (i) distributive, (ii) complemented, and (iii) bounded (0 aur 1 maujood hon).</strong></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Isliye: <span class="m">Dâ‚ƒâ‚€</span> ek Boolean algebra hai (distributive âœ“, complemented âœ“, bounded âœ“) â€” aur ye <span class="m">P({2,3,5})</span> ke bilkul "isomorphic" hai. Dâ‚â‚‚ lattice hai par Boolean algebra nahi (complement nahi). Ch 5 mein hum Boolean algebra ko alag se postulates se define karenge.</p>
</div>

<div class="box quiz">
