<html><head><meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><section id="ch5">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 5 Â· Unit I</span>Boolean Algebra</h2>

<p class="lead">Boolean algebra = <strong>logic ka algebra</strong>. Iska poora kaam sirf do values par hota hai (0 aur 1 â€” "false" aur "true"), par isi se computers, circuits aur search engines chalte hain. Pichhle chapter ka "distributive + complemented lattice" hi takneeki roop mein Boolean algebra hai.</p>

<h3 id="s5-1">5.1 Boolean algebra â€” postulates (Huntington's definition)</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Boolean algebra (postulates)</p>
  <p><strong>A Boolean algebra is a set B with two binary operations + (OR, join) and Â· (AND, meet), a unary operation â€² (complement), and two special elements 0 and 1 (0 â‰  1), satisfying the following postulates for all a, b, c âˆˆ B:</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    P1 (Closure): a + b âˆˆ B, a Â· b âˆˆ B<br>
    P2 (Commutative): a + b = b + a, a Â· b = b Â· a<br>
    P3 (Distributive): a Â· (b + c) = aÂ·b + aÂ·c, &nbsp; a + (bÂ·c) = (a + b)Â·(a + c)<br>
    P4 (Identity elements): a + 0 = a, &nbsp; a Â· 1 = a<br>
    P5 (Complement): a + aâ€² = 1, &nbsp; a Â· aâ€² = 0<br>
    P6 (0 â‰  1)
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Dhyan do ki <strong>distributive law dono forms mein maangi gayi hai</strong> â€” lattice mein ek form se doosri nikal aati hai, par postulate system mein dono maangte hain. Aur "complement" postulate se hi 0/1 ka role clear hota hai.</p>
</div>

<div class="tw">
