<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Set B</th><th>+ (OR/join)</th><th>Â· (AND/meet)</th><th>â€² (complement)</th><th>0</th><th>1</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td><strong>B = {0, 1}</strong> ("two-element Boolean algebra")</td><td>max(a,b) â€” yaani OR</td><td>min(a,b) â€” yaani AND</td><td>0â€² = 1, 1â€² = 0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td><strong>P(S)</strong> (power set of S, e.g. S = {2,3,5})</td><td>A âˆª B</td><td>A âˆ© B</td><td>Aâ€² = S âˆ’ A (complement set)</td><td>âˆ…</td><td>S</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Dâ‚™</strong> where n square-free (e.g. Dâ‚ƒâ‚€)</td><td>lcm(a,b)</td><td>gcd(a,b)</td><td>aâ€² = n/a</td><td>1</td><td>n</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Example 3 ko dhyaan se dekho: Dâ‚ƒâ‚€ mein <span class="m">2â€² = 30/2 = 15</span> âœ“ (yahi complement tha Â§4.5 mein!), <span class="m">5â€² = 6</span> âœ“. Isliye Dâ‚ƒâ‚€ aur P({2,3,5}) "same cheez" hain â€” bas ek mein numbers aur doosre mein sets hain.</p>

<h3 id="s5-2">5.2 Duality principle aur zaroori theorems</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Duality principle</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Dual of a Boolean expression:</strong> har <span class="m">+</span> ko <span class="m">Â·</span> se badlo, <span class="m">Â·</span> ko <span class="m">+</span> se, <span class="m">0</span> ko <span class="m">1</span> se aur <span class="m">1</span> ko <span class="m">0</span> se (variables aur complements waise hi rehte hain). <strong>Duality principle:</strong> agar koi identity sach hai, to uska dual bhi sach hai. Isliye theorems hamesha <em>jodi mein</em> milte hain â€” ek prove karo, doosra muft.</p>
</div>

<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Boolean algebra ke main theorems (har row "dual pair" hai)</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Theorem</th><th>Form 1</th><th>Form 2 (dual)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Idempotent</td><td>a + a = a</td><td>a Â· a = a</td></tr>
<tr><td>Boundedness</td><td>a + 1 = 1</td><td>a Â· 0 = 0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Absorption</td><td>a + (aÂ·b) = a</td><td>a Â· (a + b) = a</td></tr>
<tr><td>Involution</td><td colspan="2">(aâ€²)â€² = a</td></tr>
<tr><td>De Morgan</td><td>(a + b)â€² = aâ€² Â· bâ€²</td><td>(a Â· b)â€² = aâ€² + bâ€²</td></tr>
<tr><td>Associative</td><td>(a + b) + c = a + (b + c)</td><td>(aÂ·b)Â·c = aÂ·(bÂ·c)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Complement uniqueness</td><td colspan="2">agar aÂ·x = 0 aur a + x = 1, to x = aâ€² (complement sirf ek hi hota hai)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Proof â€” absorption law <span class="m">a + (a Â· b) = a</span> (proofs likhna aata hona chahiye)</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>Shuru: <span class="m">a + (aÂ·b)</span></li>
    <li>= <span class="m">aÂ·1 + aÂ·b</span> &nbsp;&nbsp;(P4: a = a Â· 1)</li>
    <li>= <span class="m">a Â· (1 + b)</span> &nbsp;&nbsp;(P3 distributive â€” a common liya)</li>
    <li>= <span class="m">a Â· 1</span> &nbsp;&nbsp;(boundedness: 1 + b = 1)</li>
    <li>= <span class="m">a</span> âœ“ &nbsp;&nbsp;(P4)</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Dual se</strong> <span class="m">a Â· (a + b) = a</span> apne aap mil jaata hai. De Morgan ke laws ko Venn diagram se ya truth table se bhi verify kiya ja sakta hai â€” dono methods exam mein chalte hain.</p>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” 3 galtiyan jo har saal hoti hain</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>De Morgan mein sign bhool jaana:</strong> <span class="m">(a + b)â€² = aâ€² Â· bâ€²</span> hai, <span class="m">aâ€² + bâ€²</span> <em>nahi</em>. Yaad rakho: <strong>"NOT andar jaata hai to AND â†” OR badal jaata hai"</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Ordinary algebra ke niyam ghalat jagah lagana:</strong> boolean algebra mein <span class="m">a + a = a</span> hai (2a nahi!) aur <span class="m">a + 1 = 1</span> hai. Yahan "addition" ka matlab OR hai, arithmetic nahi.</li>
    <li><strong>Complement aur inverse mix karna:</strong> complement (â€²) ek unary operation hai B ke andar; "inverse" ki baat group theory mein hoti hai. Boolean algebra mein sirf complement hota hai.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h3 id="s5-3">5.3 Boolean algebra ko lattice ki tarah dekhna</h3>
<p>Har Boolean algebra ek bounded, distributive, complemented lattice hai â€” aur har finite bounded-distributive-complemented lattice ek Boolean algebra hai. Isliye pichhle chapter ke saare tools yahan bhi chalte hain:</p>
<ul>
  <li><span class="m">a â‰¤ b âŸº a Â· b = a âŸº a + b = b</span> se partial order milta hai (aur ye ek lattice order hi hai).</li>
  <li>0 aur 1 boundedness ke liye; <span class="m">a âˆ¨ b</span> = a + b, <span class="m">a âˆ§ b</span> = aÂ·b.</li>
  <li>Distributivity P3 hai, complement P5 hai â€” yaani Boolean algebra "lattice + complement ki guarantee" hai.</li>
</ul>

<figure>
<svg viewBox="0 0 660 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="f8t f8d">
