<h3 id="s5-4">5.4 Isomorphism, sub-algebra aur Stone ka result</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Isomorphic Boolean algebras:</strong> do Boolean algebras "same" kehlate hain agar unke elements ke beech oneâ€“one correspondence ho jo operations (+ , Â·, â€²) aur 0, 1 ko preserve kare.</li>
  <li><strong>Sub-algebra:</strong> B ka subset jo khud (same operations ke andar) Boolean algebra ho â€” ismein 0 aur 1 bhi hone chahiye. Jaise P({1,2,3}) ka sub-algebra {âˆ…, {1}, {2,3}, {1,2,3}}.</li>
  <li><strong>Stone's representation theorem (finite version):</strong> har finite Boolean algebra <span class="m">P(S)</span> ke isomorphic hota hai (kisi finite S ke liye); isliye uske elements ki ginti <strong>2â¿</strong> hoti hai. Isi wajah se Dâ‚ƒâ‚€ (8 = 2Â³) Boolean algebra hai par Dâ‚â‚‚ (6 elements) <em>nahi</em> ho sakta â€” 6 kabhi 2â¿ nahi hota!</li>
</ul>

