  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Yaad rakho:</strong> convolution ka matlab "prefix sums" jaisa hai jab b = 1,1,1,â€¦ ho â€” isliye convolution se hi counting problems (jaise "kitne tarike") aate hain.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s10-3">10.3 Asymptotic behaviour â€” O, Î©, Î˜</h3>
<p>Algorithms ke liye sabse zaroori sawaal: "n badhne par ye function <em>kitni tez</em> badhta hai?" Uske liye teen symbols use hote hain:</p>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definitions (asymptotic notation)</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Big-O (upper bound):</strong> <span class="m">f(n) = O(g(n))</span> agar kuch constants c &gt; 0 aur nâ‚€ aise hon ki <span class="m">f(n) â‰¤ c Â· g(n)</span> har <span class="m">n â‰¥ nâ‚€</span> ke liye. Yaani "f, g se tez nahi badhta (constant factor tak)".</li>
    <li><strong>Big-Î© (lower bound):</strong> <span class="m">f(n) = Î©(g(n))</span> agar <span class="m">f(n) â‰¥ c Â· g(n)</span> aage ke liye â€” "f, g se dheere nahi badhta".</li>
    <li><strong>Big-Î˜ (tight bound):</strong> <span class="m">f(n) = Î˜(g(n))</span> agar f = O(g) <em>aur</em> f = Î©(g) dono hon â€” "f aur g same rate se badhte hain".</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 2 â€” O-estimate nikaalna</p>
  <p><strong>Dikhaiye ki f(n) = 3nÂ² + 5n + 2 = O(nÂ²).</strong></p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>n â‰¥ 1 ke liye: <span class="m">5n â‰¤ 5nÂ²</span> aur <span class="m">2 â‰¤ 2nÂ²</span>.</li>
    <li>Isliye <span class="m">3nÂ² + 5n + 2 â‰¤ 3nÂ² + 5nÂ² + 2nÂ² = 10nÂ²</span> âœ“ â†’ c = 10, nâ‚€ = 1 kaafi hai.</li>
    <li>Yahan se rule: <strong>f ke sabse bade degree wale term ko dekho, baaki terms aur constants hata do.</strong> Isliye 3nÂ² + 5n + 2 = O(nÂ²), aur wahi = Î˜(nÂ²) bhi (kyunki 3nÂ² se neeche nahi girega).</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Aur ye classic compare karo:</strong> 7nÂ³ + 100n = O(nÂ³), par ye O(nÂ²) <em>nahi</em> hai â€” kyunki n badhne par nÂ³ ka share badhta jaata hai (chahe coefficients chhote hon).</p>
</div>

