<h3 id="s11-2">11.2 Operations â€” additions, shifts aur product (convolution)</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Operations ki rules</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Addition:</strong> <span class="m">Î£aâ‚™xâ¿ + Î£bâ‚™xâ¿ = Î£(aâ‚™ + bâ‚™)xâ¿</span> â€” sequences ka sum.</li>
    <li><strong>Shift:</strong> <span class="m">xáµ G(x)</span> se sequence k positions aage khisak jaati hai (shuru ke k terms 0 ho jaate hain).</li>
    <li><strong>Product = convolution (sabse important):</strong> agar <span class="m">G(x) = Î£aâ‚™xâ¿</span> aur <span class="m">H(x) = Î£bâ‚™xâ¿</span>, to <span class="m">G(x)Â·H(x) = Î£câ‚™xâ¿</span> jahan <span class="m">câ‚™ = Î£<sub>k=0</sub><sup>n</sup> aâ‚– bâ‚™â‚‹â‚–</span> â€” bilkul wahi convolution jo Ch 10 mein tha!</li>
    <li><strong>Substitution:</strong> <span class="m">G(xÂ²)</span> se sirf even terms bachte hain (jaise 1/(1âˆ’xÂ²) = 1 + xÂ² + xâ´ + â€¦).</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 1 â€” product se convolution</p>
  <p><span class="m">(1 + 2x + 3xÂ²)(1 + x) = ?</span> aur iska sequence matlab kya hai?</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>Multiply karo: <span class="m">1 + x + 2x + 2xÂ² + 3xÂ² + 3xÂ³ = 1 + 3x + 5xÂ² + 3xÂ³</span>.</li>
    <li>Sequence: <span class="m">âŸ¨1, 3, 5, 3âŸ©</span> â€” ye bilkul convolution hai: âŸ¨1,2,3âŸ© * âŸ¨1,1âŸ© âœ“ (Ch 10 ka example mil gaya!).</li>
    <li>Yaad rakho: <strong>GF ka product = sequence ka convolution</strong>. Isliye counting problems mein (jaise "kitne tarike") product lena hi natural hai.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Quick check (coefficient method):</strong> xÂ² ka coefficient = aâ‚€bâ‚‚ + aâ‚bâ‚ + aâ‚‚bâ‚€ = (1)(0) + (2)(1) + (3)(1) = 5 âœ“ (kyunki b = 1,1,0,0,â€¦).</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s11-3">11.3 Counting problems â€” GF ka asli power</h3>
<p><strong>Core idea:</strong> har choice ke liye ek "factor" likho; saare factors ka product lo; phir <span class="m">xâ¿</span> ka coefficient = n size wale results ki ginti. Jaise ek aisa factor jo n objects ke liye <span class="m">xâ¿</span> deta hai wo hai <span class="m">1 + x + xÂ² + xÂ³ + â€¦ = 1/(1 âˆ’ x)</span> ("jitne chaho" wali choice).</p>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Theorem â€” combinations with repetition</p>
  <p><strong>Non-negative integer solutions ki ginti <span class="m">xâ‚ + xâ‚‚ + â‹¯ + x_k = n</span> ke liye:</strong></p>
  <div class="eq" style="margin:8px 0">
    <span class="m">[xâ¿] (1 âˆ’ x)<sup>âˆ’k</sup> = C(n + k âˆ’ 1, n) = C(n + k âˆ’ 1, k âˆ’ 1)</span>
    <span class="lbl">Ye "n identical objects ko k alag boxes mein baantne ke tarike" bhi hain</span>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Proof ka idea: har variable <span class="m">xáµ¢</span> aa sakta hai 0, 1, 2, â€¦ â†’ factor <span class="m">(1 + x + xÂ² + â€¦) = 1/(1âˆ’x)</span>. k variables â†’ <span class="m">(1âˆ’x)<sup>âˆ’k</sup></span>, aur iske expansion ka coefficient upar wala binomial hai.</p>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 2 â€” classical counting problems</p>
  <p><strong>(a) 5 identical balls, 3 alag boxes â€” kitne tarike?</strong> <span class="m">xâ‚ + xâ‚‚ + xâ‚ƒ = 5</span> ke solutions = <span class="m">C(5+3âˆ’1, 5) = C(7,5) = <strong>21</strong></span>.</p>
  <p><strong>(b) 3 types ki sweets se 5 sweets chuni jaayen (repetition allowed) â€” kitne tarike?</strong> Wahi formula: <span class="m">C(5+3âˆ’1, 5) = 21</span> âœ“ (identical objects, distinct types â€” same maths).</p>
  <p><strong>(c) Constraint ke saath:</strong> <span class="m">xâ‚ â‰¥ 2, xâ‚‚ â‰¥ 0, xâ‚ƒ â‰¥ 0</span> aur <span class="m">xâ‚ + xâ‚‚ + xâ‚ƒ = 5</span>. Trick: <span class="m">yâ‚ = xâ‚ âˆ’ 2</span> rakh do â†’ <span class="m">yâ‚ + xâ‚‚ + xâ‚ƒ = 3</span> â†’ <span class="m">C(3+2, 2) = C(5,2) = <strong>10</strong></span>. (GF language mein: pehla factor <span class="m">xÂ²/(1âˆ’x)</span> tha â€” isliye overall <span class="m">xÂ²</span> se shift, aur coefficient xÂ³ dekhna pada.)</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>(d) 20 rupaye ke change ke tarike</strong> (denominations 1, 2, 5, 10 ke coins, unlimited): GF = <span class="m">1/[(1âˆ’x)(1âˆ’xÂ²)(1âˆ’xâµ)(1âˆ’xÂ¹â°)]</span> â€” iska xÂ²â° ka coefficient exactly wahi jawab deta hai. Ye classic example sikhata hai ki GF "unlimited supply" wale problems ko kaise handle karta hai.</p>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 3 â€” compositions ki ginti (elegant)</p>
  <p><strong>n ko kitne tarikon se "ordered sum" ki tarah likha ja sakta hai?</strong> (Jaise n = 3: 3; 1+2; 2+1; 1+1+1 â†’ 4 tarike.)</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>Ek part ke liye GF: <span class="m">x + xÂ² + xÂ³ + â€¦ = x/(1âˆ’x)</span> (part ka size kam se kam 1).</li>
    <li>k parts ke liye: <span class="m">[x/(1âˆ’x)]áµ</span>; saare k â‰¥ 1 par jodo: <span class="m">Î£<sub>kâ‰¥1</sub> [x/(1âˆ’x)]áµ = x/(1âˆ’x) Ã· (1 âˆ’ x/(1âˆ’x)) = x/(1 âˆ’ 2x)</span>.</li>
    <li>Aur <span class="m">x/(1âˆ’2x) = xÂ·(1 + 2x + 4xÂ² + â€¦)</span> â†’ coefficient of xâ¿ = <span class="m">2<sup>nâˆ’1</sup></span> âœ“</li>
    <li>Check: n = 3 â†’ 2Â² = <strong>4</strong> âœ“ (upar gine the). n = 4 â†’ 8 tarike âœ“</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Ye example yaad rakho:</strong> isme poora "GF method" 4 steps mein dikh gaya â€” factor banao, multiply karo, closed form banao, coefficient padho.</p>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” GF ke 4 traps</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>"Identical" vs "distinct" objects:</strong> identical objects ko baantne mein GF/combinations with repetition chalti hai; distinct objects ke liye <span class="m">kâ¿</span> ya permutations wala logic. Galat choose karne se poora answer badal jaata hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Index 0 se shuru hota hai:</strong> âŸ¨aâ‚™âŸ© ka pehla term aâ‚€ hai; agar aapki counting n = 1 se shuru hoti hai to GF mein <span class="m">x</span> ka factor ya shift sambhalna padta hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Constraint ko bhool jaana:</strong> "xâ‚ â‰¥ 2" wale case mein pehle substitute karke chhota problem banao â€” warna formula seedha lagane se answer galat aayega.</li>
    <li><strong>Coefficient vs closed form:</strong> answer "coefficient of xâ¿ in G(x)" likh dena adhoora hai â€” jab tak possible ho, coefficient ki explicit value (binomial/closed form) nikaalo.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="box quiz">
<p class="bt" style="color:#123a6b">Mini knowledge check 11</p>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q1. Sequence âŸ¨1, 3, 9, 27, â€¦âŸ© ki generating function kya hai?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Ye geometric hai: aâ‚™ = 3â¿ â†’ <span class="m">G(x) = 1/(1 âˆ’ 3x)</span>. (Check: 1/(1âˆ’3x) = 1 + 3x + 9xÂ² + 27xÂ³ + â€¦ âœ“)</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q2. (1 âˆ’ x)â»Â³ mein xâ´ ka coefficient kya hai?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Formula: <span class="m">[xâ¿](1âˆ’x)^(âˆ’k) = C(n + k âˆ’ 1, n)</span> â†’ n = 4, k = 3 â†’ <span class="m">C(6, 4) = 15</span>. Iska matlab: xâ‚ + xâ‚‚ + xâ‚ƒ = 4 ke non-negative solutions 15 hain âœ“ (aur 4 identical objects 3 boxes mein 15 tarike se baant sakte ho).</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q3. 10 identical books, 4 students â€” har student ko kam se kam 1 book mile, kitne tarike?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">"Kam se kam 1" ka matlab xáµ¢ â‰¥ 1. Substitute <span class="m">xáµ¢ = yáµ¢ + 1</span> se <span class="m">yâ‚ + yâ‚‚ + yâ‚ƒ + yâ‚„ = 6</span> â†’ <span class="m">C(6+4âˆ’1, 6) = C(9, 6) = 84</span> tarike. (GF method: factor <span class="m">x/(1âˆ’x)</span> per student â†’ <span class="m">xâ´/(1âˆ’x)â´</span> â†’ coefficient of xÂ¹â° = [xâ¶](1âˆ’x)^(âˆ’4) = C(6+3, 3) = C(9,3) = 84 âœ“ same.)</p></div>
</details>
</div>

<div class="box recap">
  <p class="bt">Quick Recap â€” Chapter 11</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>OGF: <span class="m">G(x) = Î£ aâ‚™xâ¿</span>; coefficient se sequence: <span class="m">aâ‚™ = [xâ¿] G(x)</span>.</li>
    <li>Standard GFs: 1/(1âˆ’x), 1/(1âˆ’ax), 1/(1âˆ’x)Â², (1+x)áµ, (1âˆ’x)^(âˆ’k), Fibonacci ka x/(1âˆ’xâˆ’xÂ²).</li>
    <li>Operations: add, shift (xáµ), <strong>product = convolution</strong>.</li>
    <li>Counting: <span class="m">[xâ¿](1âˆ’x)^(âˆ’k) = C(n+kâˆ’1, n)</span> â€” identical objects baantna / combinations with repetition.</li>
    <li>Compositions of n = 2^(nâˆ’1) (GF: x/(1âˆ’2x)). "Unlimited coins" problems bhi GF se.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box exam">
  <p class="bt">Exam corner â€” Chapter 11</p>
  <p><strong>Short:</strong> (i) Generating function define kijiye. (ii) âŸ¨1,1,1,â€¦âŸ© ki GF. (iii) Product ka sequence par kya asar hota hai? (iv) [xâµ](1âˆ’x)â»Â² kya hai? <span class="small">[C(5+1,5) = 6]</span> (v) 4 identical balls ko 2 boxes mein baantne ke tarike? <span class="small">[C(4+1,4) = 5]</span></p>
  <p><strong>Long:</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Generating function ki definition likhiye aur 5 standard sequences ki GFs nikaaliye (geometric, âŸ¨n+1âŸ©, binomial, Fibonacci, âŸ¨1,1,1,â€¦âŸ©).</li>
    <li>GF method se dikhaiye ki <span class="m">xâ‚ + xâ‚‚ + â‹¯ + x_k = n</span> ke non-negative integer solutions ki ginti C(n+kâˆ’1, n) hoti hai. Ek numerical example solve kijiye.</li>
    <li>n ke "compositions" (ordered sums) ki ginti GF se nikaaliye aur dikhaiye ki wo 2^(nâˆ’1) hai. <span class="small">[GF = x/(1âˆ’2x); coefficient = 2^(nâˆ’1)]</span></li>
    <li>"20 rupaye ko 1, 2, 5, 10 ke coins se dene ke tarike" â€” is problem ki GF likhiye aur samjhaiye ki coefficient kaise answer deta hai (exact coefficient nikaalna optional).</li>
  </ol>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Conceptual:</strong> GF mein <span class="m">x</span> ki "value" kya hoti hai? <span class="small">[Koi value nahi â€” x ek formal symbol (placeholder) hai jiska kaam coefficient ki position batana hai. Isliye convergence ki chinta nahi hoti; hum bas <em>formal power series</em> ki tarah algebra karte hain. (Convergence ki baat sirf tab aati hai jab hum G(x) ko asli function ki tarah evaluate karna chahen.)]</span></p>
</div>
