<html><head><meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><section id="ch7">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 7 Â· Unit I</span>Pigeonhole Principle</h2>

<p class="lead">Ye Unit I ka sabse chhota aur sabse powerful principle hai â€” itna simple ki school ke bachcho ko bhi samajh aata hai, par isse non-obvious cheezein prove hoti hain. Poora naam: <em>Dirichlet's box principle</em>.</p>

<h3 id="s7-1">7.1 Statement â€” simple aur generalized form</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Pigeonhole principle (simple form)</p>
  <p><strong>If k + 1 (ya zyada) objects are placed into k boxes, then at least one box contains two or more objects.</strong></p>
  <p class="bt" style="margin-top:10px">Generalized form ("strong" version)</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>If N objects are placed into k boxes, then at least one box contains at least âŒˆN / kâŒ‰ objects.</strong> (âŒˆ âŒ‰ = ceiling function â€” "upar wala number", jaise âŒˆ3.1âŒ‰ = 4, âŒˆ4âŒ‰ = 4.)</p>
</div>

<div class="box why">
  <p class="bt">Why â€” ye itna zaroori kyun hai?</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Ye <strong>existence</strong> prove karta hai â€” "kuch to hai", bina usko dhoondhe. Yani "kitne hone chahiye taaki pakka do repeat ho" type ke sawaal ka jawab mil jaata hai.</li>
    <li>Naam hi picture de deta hai: agar 13 kabootar 12 ghoslon mein jaayein, to kisi ghosle mein <strong>do</strong> honge hi. Isi tarah 25 students 12 months mein â†’ kisi month mein kam se kam 3 students ke birthday honge (<span class="m">âŒˆ25/12âŒ‰ = 3</span>).</li>
    <li>Proof ki technique: "maan lo koi box 2 se zyada nahi rakhta â€” phir total kam pad jaayega." Yehi <strong>contradiction</strong> ka argument hai, jo exam mein likhna hai.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

