<h3 id="s7-2">7.2 Classic examples (ye exam mein seedha poochhe jaate hain)</h3>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Example 1 â€” birthday months</p>
  <p>(a) 13 logon mein pakka do ke birthday ek hi month mein honge. <strong>Reason:</strong> 12 months = 12 boxes, 13 log = 13 objects â†’ <span class="m">âŒˆ13/12âŒ‰ = 2</span>.</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">(b) <strong>Kitne log chahiye taaki pakka 3 logon ka birthday ek hi month mein ho?</strong> Har month mein pehle 2-2 log daal do (total 24), phir 25th banda kisi bhi month mein jaayega aur us month mein 3 ho jaayenge. Isliye answer <span class="m">12 Ã— 2 + 1 = 25</span>.</p>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Example 2 â€” Coprime pair (classic and elegant)</p>
  <p><strong>Statement:</strong> {1, 2, â€¦, 2n} mein se koi bhi <span class="m">n + 1</span> numbers chuno â€” unmein pakka do numbers <strong>coprime</strong> (relative prime) honge.</p>
  <p><strong>Proof (boxes = consecutive pairs):</strong> Numbers ko n boxes mein baanto: {1,2}, {3,4}, {5,6}, â€¦, {2nâˆ’1, 2n}. Ye n boxes hain, par hum <span class="m">n + 1</span> numbers chun rahe hain â†’ pigeonhole se ek box se do numbers aayenge. Aur ek box wale do numbers consecutive hote hain (jaise k aur k+1), aur consecutive integers <strong>hamesha coprime</strong> hote hain (gcd(k, k+1) = 1). <strong>Proved âœ“</strong></p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Lesson:</strong> Pigeonhole ka asli kaam "boxes" ko cleverly choose karna hai â€” yahan boxes ne consecutive pairs ko pakda.</p>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Example 3 â€” Divisibility wala (remainder boxes)</p>
  <p><strong>Statement:</strong> Koi bhi n + 1 integers chuno â€” unmein do aise honge jinka difference n se divisible ho.</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>Boxes = remainders mod n: [0], [1], â€¦, [nâˆ’1] â€” total <strong>n boxes</strong>.</li>
    <li>n + 1 numbers ko in n boxes mein daalo â†’ ek box mein 2 numbers padenge, maan lo a aur b.</li>
    <li>Dono ka remainder same hai â†’ <span class="m">a â‰¡ b (mod n)</span> â†’ isliye <span class="m">n | (a âˆ’ b)</span> âœ“</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Yaad rakho:</strong> "difference divisible by n" wale questions ka box = <em>remainder classes</em>. Isi trick se aage "any 5 integers mein 3 ka sum 3 se divisible hota hai" jaisi problems banti hain.</p>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Example 4 â€” 5 cards aur suits</p>
  <p><strong>Statement:</strong> 52-card deck se 5 cards nikaalo â€” pakka do cards ek hi suit ke honge.</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Proof:</strong> Boxes = 4 suits (spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs); objects = 5 cards â†’ <span class="m">âŒˆ5/4âŒ‰ = 2</span> cards ek suit mein honge. (Isi tarah 14 cards nikaalo to pakka 4 cards ek hi suit ke honge: <span class="m">âŒˆ14/4âŒ‰ = 4</span>.)</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s7-3">7.3 Generalized principle ke numericals</h3>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Example 5 â€” class mein students</p>
  <p>(a) 40 students ki class mein kam se kam kitne students ka birthday ek hi month mein hoga? <span class="m">âŒˆ40/12âŒ‰ = âŒˆ3.33âŒ‰ = <strong>4</strong></span>.</p>
  <p>(b) Kitne students chahiye taaki pakka 4 ka birthday same month ho? <span class="m">12 Ã— 3 + 1 = <strong>37</strong></span>.</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">(c) Ek shelf mein 5 subjects ki books hain. Kam se kam kitni books rakhi jaayein taaki kisi ek subject ki 6 books pakka ho? <span class="m">5 Ã— 5 + 1 = <strong>26</strong></span> books.</p>
</div>
<div class="box fact">
  <p class="bt">Did you know? â€” Party wala Ramsey fact (verified logic)</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Kisi bhi party mein <strong>6 log</strong> ho, to pakka ya to 3 log aapas mein dost honge, ya 3 log aapas mein ajnabi. Ye pigeonhole jaisa hi argument hai (har bande ke 5 "edges" 2 categories mein baant do â€” 3 ek taraf, 2 doosri; phir cases check karo). Isko <strong>Ramsey number R(3,3) = 6</strong> kehte hain â€” "theory of friends and strangers". Dhyan do: 5 logon ke liye ye sach <em>nahi</em> hai (5-cycle se counterexample banta hai) â€” R(3,3) = 6 minimum hai.</p>
</div>
<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” pigeonhole ke 3 traps</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Ceiling function bhool jaana:</strong> <span class="m">âŒˆN/kâŒ‰</span> likhna hai, N/k nahi. 25 students ke liye 25/12 = 2.08 nahi, <strong>3</strong> likhna hai.</li>
    <li><strong>"Kam se kam" aur "pakka" wale wording par dhyaan do:</strong> "kam se kam kitne chahiye taaki pakka â€¦" type sawaal mein jawab <em>k Ã— (m âˆ’ 1) + 1</em> hota hai, aur "N objects mein at least kitne" type mein <em>âŒˆN/kâŒ‰</em>. Dono alag hain.</li>
    <li><strong>Ye sirf existence deta hai, exact count nahi:</strong> "kisi box mein 2 hain" â€” ye nahi batata <em>kaunse</em> box mein. Isliye numericals mein distribution ki ginti ke liye aapko inclusionâ€“exclusion ya alag counting chahiye (next chapter).</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="box quiz">
<p class="bt" style="color:#123a6b">Mini knowledge check 7</p>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q1. Ek bag mein 10 laal aur 10 neeli gend hain. Aankh band karke kitni gendein nikaalni hongi taaki pakka 2 same colour ki mil jaayein?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a"><strong>3 gendein.</strong> Boxes = colours (2 boxes). 3 objects â†’ <span class="m">âŒˆ3/2âŒ‰ = 2</span> ek hi colour ke. (Worst case: pehli laal, doosri neeli, teesri kisi bhi colour ki â†’ us colour ki 2 ho gayi.)</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q2. {1, 2, â€¦, 8} mein se kitne numbers chunne par pakka do coprime milenge?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Boxes: {1,2}, {3,4}, {5,6}, {7,8} â€” 4 boxes. Isliye <span class="m">n = 4</span> ke liye <span class="m">n + 1 = 5</span> numbers chahiye. (4 numbers chunne par counterexample: {2,4,6,8} â€” inme koi jodi coprime nahi, kyunki sab even hain.)</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q3. Ek classroom mein 13 students hain. Prove karo ki kam se kam 2 students ka birthday same month mein hoga. Generalized form se 40 students ka kya?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a"><strong>13 students:</strong> 12 months = 12 boxes, 13 objects â†’ at least ek box mein 2 â†’ same month ke 2 students âœ“. <strong>40 students:</strong> <span class="m">âŒˆ40/12âŒ‰ = 4</span> â†’ kam se kam 4 students ka birthday ek hi month mein hoga. Note: ye "exactly 4" nahi, "kam se kam 4" hai â€” ye wording exam mein zaroor likho.</p></div>
</details>
</div>

<div class="box recap">
  <p class="bt">Quick Recap â€” Chapter 7</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
