<section id="ch9">
<h2 class="chapter"><span class="chnum">Chapter 9 Â· Unit I</span>Propositional Calculus, Truth Tables, Tautologies &amp; Contradictions</h2>

<p class="lead">Ye chapter "mathematical logic" ki pehli seedhi hai: sentence ko symbol banao, connectives se jodo, aur truth table se check karo kab wo sach hai. Aage Boolean algebra (Ch 5â€“6) isi ki algebraic roop hai â€” isliye yahan aur wahan ke symbols ek hi hain (âˆ§ â†” Â·, âˆ¨ â†” +, Â¬ â†” â€²).</p>

<h3 id="s9-1">9.1 Proposition aur basic logical operations</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Proposition (statement)</p>
  <p><strong>A proposition is a declarative sentence that is either true or false, but not both.</strong> Sawaal, order ya exclamation propositions nahi hote (kyunki unki truth value nahi hoti).</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Jaise: "Delhi India ki rajdhani hai" (proposition, true), "2 + 2 = 5" (proposition, false), "Ye kaam kar do" (order â€” proposition nahi), "x &gt; 5" (variable hai, isliye abhi proposition nahi â€” x ka value pata chale to <em>predicate</em> ban jaata hai).</p>
</div>
