<h3 id="s9-3">9.3 Logical equivalence aur zaroori laws</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Logical equivalence</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Do statements "logically equivalent" (â‰¡) kehlate hain agar unki truth tables har row par same hon â€” yaani wo hamesha same truth value dete hain.</strong> Note: <span class="m">P â‰¡ Q</span> tab bhi sach hai jab <span class="m">P â†” Q</span> ek tautology ho.</p>
</div>
<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Laws of logic (ye table ratta nahi â€” Boolean algebra se compare karke dekho, bilkul same hain)</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Law</th><th>Form</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Identity</td><td>p âˆ§ T â‰¡ p &nbsp;|&nbsp; p âˆ¨ F â‰¡ p</td></tr>
<tr><td>Domination</td><td>p âˆ¨ T â‰¡ T &nbsp;|&nbsp; p âˆ§ F â‰¡ F</td></tr>
<tr><td>Idempotent</td><td>p âˆ§ p â‰¡ p &nbsp;|&nbsp; p âˆ¨ p â‰¡ p</td></tr>
<tr><td>Double negation</td><td>Â¬(Â¬p) â‰¡ p</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commutative</td><td>p âˆ§ q â‰¡ q âˆ§ p &nbsp;|&nbsp; p âˆ¨ q â‰¡ q âˆ¨ p</td></tr>
<tr><td>Associative</td><td>(p âˆ§ q) âˆ§ r â‰¡ p âˆ§ (q âˆ§ r) (same OR ke liye)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Distributive</td><td>p âˆ§ (q âˆ¨ r) â‰¡ (p âˆ§ q) âˆ¨ (p âˆ§ r); p âˆ¨ (q âˆ§ r) â‰¡ (p âˆ¨ q) âˆ§ (p âˆ¨ r)</td></tr>
<tr><td>De Morgan</td><td>Â¬(p âˆ§ q) â‰¡ Â¬p âˆ¨ Â¬q &nbsp;|&nbsp; Â¬(p âˆ¨ q) â‰¡ Â¬p âˆ§ Â¬q</td></tr>
<tr><td>Absorption</td><td>p âˆ¨ (p âˆ§ q) â‰¡ p &nbsp;|&nbsp; p âˆ§ (p âˆ¨ q) â‰¡ p</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implication</td><td><strong>p â†’ q â‰¡ Â¬p âˆ¨ q</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Contrapositive</td><td>p â†’ q â‰¡ Â¬q â†’ Â¬p</td></tr>
<tr><td>Negation of implication</td><td><strong>Â¬(p â†’ q) â‰¡ p âˆ§ Â¬q</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Biconditional</td><td>p â†” q â‰¡ (p â†’ q) âˆ§ (q â†’ p)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exportation / others</td><td>(p âˆ§ q) â†’ r â‰¡ p â†’ (q â†’ r); &nbsp; p â†’ (q âˆ§ r) â‰¡ (p â†’ q) âˆ§ (p â†’ r)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 2 â€” equivalence prove karna (bina poori truth table)</p>
  <p><strong>Prove:</strong> <span class="m">Â¬(p â†’ q) â‰¡ p âˆ§ Â¬q</span>.</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li><span class="m">p â†’ q â‰¡ Â¬p âˆ¨ q</span> &nbsp;(implication law)</li>
    <li><span class="m">Â¬(p â†’ q) â‰¡ Â¬(Â¬p âˆ¨ q)</span></li>
    <li><span class="m">â‰¡ Â¬(Â¬p) âˆ§ Â¬q</span> &nbsp;(De Morgan â€” OR ka NOT AND ban jaata hai)</li>
    <li><span class="m">â‰¡ p âˆ§ Â¬q</span> &nbsp;(double negation) âœ“</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Sanity check:</strong> "agar baarish hui to chhata laaya" ka <em>jhoota</em> hona matlab: baarish hui (p) <strong>aur</strong> chhata nahi laaya (Â¬q) â€” bilkul sahi.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="s9-4">9.4 Tautology, contradiction aur contingency</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definitions (exam mein exact words likho)</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>Tautology (valid statement):</strong> aisa compound statement jiski truth table ka last column <strong>saara T</strong> ho â€” yaani har possible case mein sach.</li>
    <li><strong>Contradiction (unsatisfiable):</strong> jiska last column <strong>saara F</strong> ho â€” kabhi sach nahi hota.</li>
    <li><strong>Contingency:</strong> jisme kuch rows T aur kuch F hon â€” ye na tautology na contradiction.</li>
    <li><strong>Satisfiable:</strong> jiska last column mein kam se kam ek T ho (isliye tautology aur contingency dono satisfiable hain, contradiction nahi).</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Standard examples â€” inhe yaad rakho, viva mein poochhte hain</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Type</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>p âˆ¨ Â¬p</td><td><strong>Tautology</strong></td><td>"ya to p, ya p nahi" â€” excluded middle</td></tr>
<tr><td>p âˆ§ Â¬p</td><td><strong>Contradiction</strong></td><td>p aur Â¬p ek saath sach nahi ho sakte</td></tr>
<tr><td>p â†’ p</td><td>Tautology</td><td>Self-implication</td></tr>
<tr><td>(p âˆ§ q) â†’ p</td><td>Tautology</td><td>Simplification</td></tr>
<tr><td>p â†’ (p âˆ¨ q)</td><td>Tautology</td><td>Addition</td></tr>
<tr><td>(p â†’ q) â†” (Â¬q â†’ Â¬p)</td><td>Tautology</td><td>Contrapositive equivalence</td></tr>
<tr><td>p â†’ q</td><td>Contingency</td><td>p = T, q = F par false hota hai</td></tr>
<tr><td>p â†” Â¬p</td><td>Contradiction</td><td>Same truth value kabhi nahi</td></tr>
<tr><td>(p âˆ§ q) â†’ r</td><td>Contingency</td><td>Kuch cases false (p, q true par r false)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h3 id="s9-5">9.5 Arguments ki validity aur rules of inference</h3>
<div class="box def">
  <p class="bt">Definition â€” Valid argument</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>An argument with premises Pâ‚, Pâ‚‚, â€¦, Pâ‚™ and conclusion Q is valid if (Pâ‚ âˆ§ Pâ‚‚ âˆ§ â€¦ âˆ§ Pâ‚™) â†’ Q is a tautology.</strong> Yaani jab bhi saari premises sach hon, conclusion bhi sach hona hi chahiye. (Dhyaan do: validity conclusion ke <em>sach hone</em> ki baat nahi â€” sirf "premises se follow hone" ki baat hai.)</p>
</div>
<div class="tw">
<table>
<caption>Rules of inference (saarÐµ naam + pattern)</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Form</th><th>Hindi mein</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Modus Ponens</strong></td><td>p, p â†’ q &nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp; q</td><td>p sach hai aur "p se q nikalta hai" â†’ q sach hai</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Modus Tollens</strong></td><td>Â¬q, p â†’ q &nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp; Â¬p</td><td>q galat hai aur "p se q nikalta hai" â†’ p galat hai</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hypothetical syllogism</td><td>p â†’ q, q â†’ r &nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp; p â†’ r</td><td>"chain rule" â€” implications ko jod do</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disjunctive syllogism</td><td>p âˆ¨ q, Â¬p &nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp; q</td><td>ek option khatam â†’ doosra bacha</td></tr>
<tr><td>Addition</td><td>p &nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp; p âˆ¨ q</td><td>sach ko "weak" banaya ja sakta hai</td></tr>
<tr><td>Simplification</td><td>p âˆ§ q &nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp; p (ya q)</td><td>AND se koi bhi hissa nikal do</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conjunction</td><td>p, q &nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp; p âˆ§ q</td><td>dono sach â†’ AND bhi sach</td></tr>
<tr><td>Resolution</td><td>p âˆ¨ q, Â¬p âˆ¨ r &nbsp;âŸ¹&nbsp; q âˆ¨ r</td><td>computer proofs (Prolog) ka base</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="box ex">
  <p class="bt">Worked Example 3 â€” argument ki validity (exam type)</p>
  <p><strong>Premises:</strong> (1) "Agar aaj exam hai, to main padh raha hoon." &nbsp;(2) "Main padh nahi raha." <br><strong>Conclusion:</strong> "Aaj exam nahi hai." Valid hai?</p>
  <ul class="deriv">
    <li>Symbols: p = "aaj exam hai", q = "main padh raha hoon".</li>
    <li>Premises: <span class="m">p â†’ q</span> aur <span class="m">Â¬q</span>. Conclusion: <span class="m">Â¬p</span>.</li>
    <li>Ye exactly <strong>Modus Tollens</strong> hai â†’ valid âœ“</li>
    <li>Truth table check: <span class="m">[(p â†’ q) âˆ§ Â¬q] â†’ Â¬p</span> ki last column saara T aayega (try karo!)</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Aur ye classic fallacy dekho:</strong> "p â†’ q" aur "q" se "p" nikaalna <strong>galat</strong> hai (asserting the consequent). "Agar exam ho to padhta hoon; main padh raha hoon; isliye exam hai" â€” par log bina exam ke bhi padhte hain! Isliye ye <em>invalid</em> argument hai.</p>
</div>

<div class="box supp-box">
  <p class="bt">Beyond syllabus Â· Quantifiers (aage ka kaam, isliye jaan lo)</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0">Predicate logic mein do quantifiers hote hain: <strong>âˆ€ (for all)</strong> aur <strong>âˆƒ (there exists)</strong>. Unka negation De Morgan jaisa hai: <span class="m">Â¬âˆ€x P(x) â‰¡ âˆƒx Â¬P(x)</span> aur <span class="m">Â¬âˆƒx P(x) â‰¡ âˆ€x Â¬P(x)</span>. Jaise "sab students pass hue" ka negation "koi na koi student fail hua" hai â€” "sab fail hue" nahi! Ye galti competitive exams mein bahut poochhi jaati hai.</p>
</div>

<div class="box warn">
  <p class="bt">Careful! â€” logic ke 4 traps</p>
  <ol style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li><strong>p â†’ q ka converse q â†’ p maan lena:</strong> ye equivalence <em>nahi</em> hai. "Baarish â†’ chhata" ye nahi kehta "chhata â†’ baarish".</li>
    <li><strong>Â¬(p â†’ q) ko Â¬p â†’ Â¬q samajhna:</strong> sahi form <span class="m">p âˆ§ Â¬q</span> hai (Worked Example 2 dekho).</li>
    <li><strong>âˆ¨ ko "ya ye ya wo" samajhna:</strong> maths ka âˆ¨ <em>inclusive</em> hai (dono sach ho sakte hain); exclusive ke liye alag symbol âŠ• hota hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Tautology likhne se pehle truth table ke saare rows check karna:</strong> ek bhi F bach gaya to wo contingency ho jaayegi â€” "lagta hai hamesha sach hoga" se marks nahi milte.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="box quiz">
<p class="bt" style="color:#123a6b">Mini knowledge check 9</p>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q1. (p â†’ q) âˆ§ (q â†’ p) kaise simplify karoge?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Biconditional law se: <span class="m">(p â†’ q) âˆ§ (q â†’ p) â‰¡ p â†” q</span>. Ya implication law se: <span class="m">(Â¬p âˆ¨ q) âˆ§ (Â¬q âˆ¨ p)</span>. Aur ye statement sach hai jab p aur q ki truth values same hon â€” yaani "p if and only if q".</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q2. (p âˆ§ Â¬q) âˆ¨ (Â¬p âˆ§ q) kya hai â€” tautology, contradiction ya contingency?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a">Ye <strong>p âŠ• q (XOR)</strong> hai â€” truth table: p = q par F, alag hone par T â†’ <strong>contingency</strong> (na tautology na contradiction). Kaise pehchana: expression do "mixed" products ka sum hai, aur dono kabhi ek saath true nahi ho sakte.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Q3. "Agar main mehnat karunga to pass ho jaunga. Main pass ho gaya. Isliye maine mehnat ki." â€” kya ye valid hai?</summary>
  <div class="dbody"><p class="a"><strong>Invalid!</strong> Ye "asserting the consequent" fallacy hai: p â†’ q aur q se p nahi nikalta (pass hone ke aur bhi kaaran ho sakte hain â€” easy paper, luck). Valid form hoti: q â†’ p hone par hi conclusion nikalta. (Modus Tollens bhi valid hota: Â¬q se Â¬p.)</p></div>
</details>
</div>

<div class="box recap">
  <p class="bt">Quick Recap â€” Chapter 9</p>
  <ul style="margin-bottom:0">
    <li>Proposition = statement jiski truth value definite ho (T ya F).</li>
    <li>Connectives: Â¬, âˆ§, âˆ¨, âŠ•, â†’, â†”; implication sirf ek case mein false (T â†’ F).</li>
    <li>Truth table: <span class="m">2â¿</span> rows; compound statement ka last column hi verdict deta hai.</li>
    <li>Tautology (saara T), contradiction (saara F), contingency (mix).</li>
    <li>Laws: De Morgan, implication (<span class="m">p â†’ q â‰¡ Â¬p âˆ¨ q</span>), contrapositive, negation of implication (<span class="m">p âˆ§ Â¬q</span>), biconditional split.</li>
    <li>Inference: Modus Ponens, Modus Tollens, syllogisms, resolution; <span class="m">(premises) â†’ conclusion</span> tautology ho to argument valid.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box exam">
  <p class="bt">Exam corner â€” Chapter 9</p>
  <p><strong>Short:</strong> (i) Proposition define kijiye (do examples ke saath). (ii) p â†’ q ki truth table likhiye. (iii) Tautology aur contradiction mein farq. (iv) De Morgan ke laws likhiye. (v) Modus Tollens likhiye.</p>
  <p><strong>Long:</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Basic logical operations (Â¬, âˆ§, âˆ¨, âŠ•, â†’, â†”) ki definitions aur truth tables likhiye. Implication ko "vacuous truth" ke example se samjhaiye.</li>
    <li>Dikhaiye ki <span class="m">(p âˆ§ q) â†’ (p âˆ¨ q)</span> tautology hai (truth table banao) aur <span class="m">(p â†’ q) â†” (Â¬q â†’ Â¬p)</span> bhi tautology hai (laws se prove karo).</li>
    <li><span class="m">Â¬(p â†’ q) â‰¡ p âˆ§ Â¬q</span> prove kijiye (laws ke steps) aur iska real-life example dijiye.</li>
    <li>Rules of inference (kam se kam 5) likhiye aur ek argument ki validity truth table se check kijiye.</li>
  </ol>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Conceptual:</strong> Kya <span class="m">p â†’ q</span> aur <span class="m">Â¬p â†’ Â¬q</span> equivalent hain? <span class="small">[Nahi â€” pehla original hai, doosra <em>inverse</em>. Equivalent pair hai: original â‰¡ contrapositive (Â¬q â†’ Â¬p), aur converse â‰¡ inverse. Truth table se verify karo: p = F, q = T par original T deta hai par inverse F.]</span></p>
</div>
